var __index = {"config": {"lang": ["en"], "separator": "[\\s\\-]+", "pipeline": ["stopWordFilter"], "fields": {"title": {"boost": 1000.0}, "text": {"boost": 1.0}, "tags": {"boost": 1000000.0}}}, "docs": [{"location": "index.html", "title": "About Sensoraia", "text": ""}, {"location": "index.html#what-is-sensoraia", "title": "What Is Sensoraia?", "text": "\u003cp>Sensoraia is an AI-enabled, in-flight sensing suite for connecting cameras, microphones and supported processing to an aircraft's onboard computer. It takes care of requesting captures, saving the results and keeping the available flight information with them. It also connects live sensor processing back to the flight: MORA can exchange data with the airborne aircraft while the mission is running, not just collect files for you to inspect after landing. The result is more useful than a folder of photographs whose story you have to reconstruct afterwards.\u003c/p> \u003cp>You might use it to look for vehicles with a neural network, listen for an alarm, inspect thermal observations, or feed supported live results into the next flight-plan decision. You choose the sensors and the job; Sensoraia provides a shared way to connect them to the flight.\u003c/p> \u003cp>New to the names? The Sensoraia glossary explains the shared vocabulary and each module's terms.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#i-just-want-the-code", "title": "I Just want the code", "text": "\u003cp>So in a hurry huh? Just want the code, find all sourcecode on github. But advised is to read the getting started page before you confuse yourself.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#why-use-it", "title": "Why Use It?", "text": ""}, {"location": "index.html#spend-less-time-joining-the-pieces", "title": "Spend Less Time Joining The Pieces", "text": "\u003cp>A sensor, an AI model, an autopilot and an onboard computer do not automatically agree on when to observe, how to process the result or which flight position belongs to it. Sensoraia handles the shared coordination, so supported modules can concentrate on their sensing and processing jobs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#keep-the-context-not-just-the-picture", "title": "Keep The Context, Not Just The Picture", "text": "\u003cp>A photograph becomes much more useful when you can relate it to the aircraft's position, attitude and capture request. Sensoraia records the available context in image metadata and reports what completed. When timing or calibration is uncertain, it keeps that distinction visible rather than turning an estimate into a promise.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#explore-before-you-connect-hardware", "title": "Explore Before You Connect Hardware", "text": "\u003cp>Run the suite on a Linux PC with a simulated aircraft and camera views made from cached aerial imagery. Follow a request through capture, processing and supported mission results, then move to your real onboard computer and sensors. Your first experiment does not need a flying aircraft. The propeller is welcome to sit this one out.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#the-same-application-code-simulated-or-airborne", "title": "The Same Application Code, Simulated Or Airborne", "text": "\u003cp>Local simulation runs the same production application source code as the software built for MORA, not a separate demonstration reimplementation. The simulated aircraft and camera inputs stand in for physical hardware; the capture coordination, processing and result-handling code remain the application you are preparing to deploy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The PC and ARM64 executables are different builds, and real sensors have their own drivers, timing and calibration. Simulation lets you exercise the application workflow before making those hardware connections.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#let-your-pc-do-the-heavy-lifting", "title": "Let Your PC Do The Heavy Lifting", "text": "\u003cp>Use the cores on your PC to build in parallel, including cross-compiling the ARM64 applications for MORA. You do not need to wait for the small companion board to compile its own software or install a development toolchain there: build on the fast machine, deploy the ready-to-run binaries, and keep MORA focused on sensing and processing. The build instructions use \u003ccode>make -j\"$(nproc)\"\u003c/code> to use your PC's available CPU cores.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#a-live-conversation-with-the-aircraft", "title": "A Live Conversation With The Aircraft", "text": "\u003cp>During flight, the controller sends pose information and capture requests to MORA. USHER coordinates the sensor work and returns supported completion or target-point results. A compatible flight plan can use fresh results to update a waypoint, follow a returned point or choose its next mission action. The flight controller remains responsible for flying the aircraft.\u003c/p> \u003cp>This is live, real-time interaction while flying: sensing, supported processing and mission decisions can happen during the flight, without waiting for a post-flight download. Actual update rates depend on the sensor, model and available computing resources; live operation is not a guarantee of hard real-time deadlines.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#neural-networks-can-take-part-in-the-flight", "title": "Neural Networks Can Take Part In The Flight", "text": "\u003cp>Neural-network processing is not limited to an offline report. Spottercam produces vehicle detections from the IMX500 during capture. Its built-in output is image detections and metadata; geographic target publishing for flight response is an additional integration step. Diplacam's experimental neural planner can return XY points that the supplied rotorcraft flight plan follows and refreshes in simulation. With the appropriate result interface and flight-plan logic, observations can therefore influence mission behavior while the aircraft is airborne.\u003c/p> \u003cp>You decide which results the flight plan may act on. A detection alone does not command a maneuver, and a network does not take over the motors. Each module's guide explains what it currently returns and its qualification limits; Diplacam is experimental, and LOOM's guarded return-vector integration awaits its trained homing model and physical accuracy validation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#how-the-hardware-fits-together", "title": "How The Hardware Fits Together", "text": "\u003cp>Think of it as three parts with different jobs:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>The flight controller flies the aircraft. Your flight plan or   operator requests a capture at the appropriate moment.\u003c/li> \u003cli>MORA carries the sensor software. MORA is the aircraft's companion-   computer role, not a particular board. Sensoraia uses the Raspberry Pi Zero   2 W as its initial, resource-constrained reference platform; another suitable   compute board can fill the role. It connects to the flight controller over   UART and to cameras or microphones through their own interfaces.\u003c/li> \u003cli>USHER coordinates the work. USHER is Sensoraia's service on MORA:   it receives the request, works with the selected sensor, saves the   result with its context, and sends completion or supported target results   back to the flight controller. The connection works in both directions.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>Sensoraia is the whole suite, MORA is the computer, and USHER is the coordinating service. Three names, not three things you must learn before you can take a picture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The current ready-made ARM64 build, package and setup instructions target 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie on the Pi Zero 2 W. That is a starting point, not a hardware lock-in: other Linux compute boards can run Sensoraia when their architecture, OS libraries, sensor drivers, interfaces and available resources are supported or adapted. They need a matching build and deployment configuration; Pi-specific instructions do not automatically apply.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Paparazzi examples connect this workflow to simulated and real flight controllers. You can also run individual sensor tools from a terminal. You do not need every sensor or every module to make a useful setup.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#what-could-you-do-with-it", "title": "What Could You Do With It?", "text": "\u003cp>Search for vehicles with live AI. Spottercam runs a compatible trained vehicle network on the IMX500, keeping its detections tied to captured images. Use the results for live review and recording; add a qualified geographic-target integration when your mission should act on a location.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Respond to supported observations in flight. Feed fresh target or planner results into compatible flight-plan logic: update a waypoint, follow up an observation or choose a mission branch. Each backend defines what it returns; the flight controller retains navigation and control.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Listen while you fly. EARcam detects an alarm tone and combines useful sound readings from different positions into an acoustic search estimate.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Explore thermal targets. Save thermal images, examine supported temperature samples and regions, and use measured lens/mounting calibration when projecting thermal observations onto the ground. A warm patch is a candidate, not an automatic identification of a particular object.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Add the sensor your mission needs. The architecture separates sensor adapters, processing and the flight-controller protocol. That gives developers a place to integrate a gas, particle or radiation sensor and define the result a mission may use. These are extension examples, not built-in Geiger, gas or particle drivers; new sensors need an adapter and an agreed data/result contract. It is extensible, not a universal plug-and-play driver collection.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Try a new idea without changing the flight interface. Use the local simulation with the same production application source, then connect MORA and the actual sensor. Check both your result handling and the physical sensor's behavior before relying on the combination.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Build better datasets and keep useful evidence. Original photographs, metadata and permitted MIMIC imagery can support external model training and evaluation. PANGEA maps and tiles can help with scene selection and annotation context; they are an additional tool, not the main mission. Training still needs suitable labels, imagery rights and independent evaluation, and camera-view models need representative camera-view inputs.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Keep conventional capture alongside AI. Use supported visible-light and thermal cameras for ordinary photographs too. You choose where inference adds value instead of making every capture depend on a model.\u003c/p> \u003cp>These are options, not a checklist. A dependable setup with one sensor is a perfectly good place to begin.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "index.html#start-small-grow-deliberately", "title": "Start Small, Grow Deliberately", "text": "\u003cp>If you are curious, explore the example below. If you already have a sensor in mind, choose its guide next. When you are ready to try it yourself, the installation guide walks you through a PC build, a local simulation and deployment to MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sensoraia helps you collect and interpret observations; it is not a flight controller or certified navigation, rescue or measurement system. Real-world accuracy depends on your sensors, calibration and timing. The guides explain those limits where they matter, so you can make informed choices as you go.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html", "title": "Try Sensoraia With Paparazzi", "text": "\u003cp>Fly a complete mission from takeoff to landing in Paparazzi NPS\u2014even without a MORA board or physical camera attached. Paparazzi flies the simulated aircraft; USHER supplies sensor observations and target updates over a simulated local UART (\u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code>) or a real FTDI cable to MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Prepare your first demo | Glossary | Install Sensoraia\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#quick-start-install-links-with-kickstartsh", "title": "Quick Start: Install Links With \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code>", "text": "\u003cp>With Paparazzi already installed at \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>, build the native PC applications and link the Sensoraia configurations into Paparazzi:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make native\n./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> installs \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code> and the unified \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> into \u003ccode>~/paparazzi/conf/\u003c/code>, links the self-contained \u003ccode>SENSORAIA\u003c/code> flight plans, airframes, radios, and telemetry profiles, and selects the demo configuration (preserving any previous \u003ccode>conf.xml\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>control_panel.xml\u003c/code> links). Prefer creating the symlinks yourself or keeping your active \u003ccode>conf.xml\u003c/code> untouched? Use \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh --no-activate\u003c/code> or follow the exact manual \u003ccode>ln -s\u003c/code> commands in Manual Symlink Setup.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#start-a-simulated-flight-takeoff-to-landing", "title": "Start A Simulated Flight (Takeoff To Landing)", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Launch Paparazzi Center: \u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003cli>In Config, ensure \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code> and    \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> are selected.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Pick the aircraft for the camera you want to fly (for example    \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code>), select target nps, and click    Build.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Open the Session tab, select the matching Sensoraia \\&lt;Camera&gt; NPS    session, and click Execute.\u003c/li> \u003cli>In PprzGCS, wait for live aircraft telemetry and a valid 3D GPS fix    (\u003ccode>Holding point\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Ground standby\u003c/code>), then click Takeoff.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Watch the aircraft climb, fly its camera mission, and complete its landing    sequence (\u003ccode>Land\u003c/code> $\\rightarrow$ \u003ccode>Final\u003c/code> $\\rightarrow$ \u003ccode>Flare\u003c/code> on fixed-wing    demos, or Return $\\rightarrow$ Land Here on \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code>), then    click Stop in Center before starting another session.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>Starting the session programs does not command takeoff. Do not start a second USHER or simulator on the same endpoints. For MORA hardware-in-the-loop over an FTDI cable, select Sensoraia MORA desk NPS (which omits PC \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> so \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code> connects straight to \u003ccode>/dev/ttyUSB0\u003c/code>). See the complete flight walkthrough.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#choose-your-camera-demo", "title": "Choose Your Camera Demo", "text": "Camera / Mode Supplied Aircraft Paparazzi Center Session What Happens In Flight Spottercam \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia Spottercam NPS Captures mock vehicle views, runs native YOLO11n ONNX detection, and projects ranked hits onto \u003ccode>T1..T5\u003c/code> AI Camera \u003ccode>Easystar_3_AICAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia AIcam NPS Periodic wings-level visible-camera survey photos tagged with EXIF flight pose LWIRcam \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LWIRCAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia LWIRcam NPS Thermal survey capture and session protocol flow CHDKbridge \u003ccode>Easystar_3_CHDKCAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia CHDKcam NPS Ticketed optical zoom, distance-triggered survey photos, and pre-landing lens retraction EARcam \u003ccode>Easystar_3_EARCAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia EARcam NPS Flower search over a simulated acoustic beacon and guarded \u003ccode>TARGET\u003c/code> waypoint update LOOMcam \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LOOMCAM\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS Outbound reference archiving (\u003ccode>START\u003c/code>) followed by return-leg evaluation (\u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code>) MIMIC / PANGEA \u003ccode>Easystar_3_MIMIC\u003c/code> (Fixed wing) Sensoraia MIMIC PANGEA NPS Renders camera views from cached aerial tiles and reconstructs a georeferenced photo map Diplacam \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code> (Rotorcraft) Sensoraia Diplacam NPS Native GP/diffusion planner refreshing eight 2 m sampling waypoints (\u003ccode>P1..P8\u003c/code>) \u003cp>Fixed-wing flight plans and the \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code> rotorcraft flight plan are not interchangeable: always pair a flight plan with the matching vehicle firmware.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#add-the-paparazzi-module-to-your-own-aircraft", "title": "Add The Paparazzi Module To Your Own Aircraft", "text": "\u003cp>The supplied \u003ccode>Easystar_*\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code> demo aircraft already include everything needed. When adding Sensoraia to your own Paparazzi aircraft:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Point Paparazzi at Sensoraia's shared protocol headers/sources:    Export \u003ccode>SENSORAIA_SRC=/path/to/sensoraia\u003c/code> in the environment before building    your aircraft in Paparazzi Center or \u003ccode>make\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart\u003c/code> compiles    \u003ccode>usher_protocol.c\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>camera_control.c\u003c/code> directly from \u003ccode>$SENSORAIA_SRC/src\u003c/code>    while preserving Paparazzi's standard \u003ccode>dc.c\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>dc.h\u003c/code> boundary.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Add \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart\u003c/code> to your airframe XML (\u003ccode>&lt;target name=\"ap\"&gt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>&lt;target name=\"nps\"&gt;\u003c/code>):\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;module name=\"digital_cam_uart\"&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Hardware autopilot target (\"ap\"): select the FC UART wired to MORA's 3.3 V GPIO14/15 --&gt;\n  &lt;configure name=\"CAMERA_PORT\" value=\"uart4\"/&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Stream periodic pose updates (required by EARcam and pose-aligned sensors) --&gt;\n  &lt;define name=\"DIGITAL_CAM_UART_POSE_STREAM\" value=\"1\"/&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Optional default camera bitmask (1=CHDK, 2=AIcam/Spotter/LOOM, 4=LWIR, 8=EARcam, 16=Diplacam) --&gt;\n  &lt;define name=\"DIGITAL_CAM_UART_CAMERA_MASK\" value=\"2\"/&gt;\n&lt;/module&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Do not enable a second conflicting \u003ccode>digital_cam_*\u003c/code> transport in the same    target. On the \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code> simulation target, \u003ccode>CAMERA_PORT\u003c/code> is replaced    automatically by the local \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> bridge (or your FTDI cable). 3. Add \u003ccode>digital_cam_point\u003c/code> in your flight plan XML whenever your mission    consumes geographic target points returned by USHER (\u003ccode>EARcam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Spottercam\u003c/code>,    \u003ccode>LWIRcam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LOOMcam\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>Diplacam\u003c/code>):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;modules&gt;\n  &lt;module name=\"digital_cam_point\"/&gt;\n&lt;/modules&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use \u003ccode>uart_cam_ctrl_set_camera_mask(...)\u003c/code> to choose which camera triggers,    \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart_start(CAM)\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart_stop(CAM)\u003c/code> for session    sensors, and \u003ccode>cam_point_fresh(CAM)\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>cam_point_valid(CAM)\u003c/code> +    \u003ccode>cam_point_to_waypoint(CAM, WP_TARGET)\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>cam_point_consume(CAM)\u003c/code> to validate    and apply returned target coordinates safely without altering flight altitude.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#mimic-and-pangea", "title": "MIMIC And PANGEA", "text": "\u003cp>Cache aerial imagery over the example route and surrounding camera footprint in pprzgcs before starting. The session renders camera-view photographs from the aircraft pose, and PANGEA works from those saved photographs, not by stitching the provider's original tiles directly.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use the MIMIC guide for cache preparation and PANGEA for map inputs and output files. Missing terrain imagery is a capture problem to resolve, not a reason to substitute invented field texture.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#understand-waypoint-updates", "title": "Understand Waypoint Updates", "text": "\u003cp>An observation, an accepted point and a navigation decision are different events. A GCS shot marker records an admitted trigger, not necessarily a saved photograph. Moving a target waypoint does not itself select a block or tell the aircraft to visit it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The UART module can suppress shot reports independently of triggering and returned results. The Diplacam demo uses this to avoid dense yellow shot markers; see reporting controls for airframe defaults, flight-plan calls and the live GCS setting.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The flight plan retains control of the route and flight altitude. Invalid, stale or unconfirmed points must not be treated as new targets. A normal mission can finish without a usable detection or sound fix.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#mora-in-the-loop", "title": "MORA In The Loop", "text": "\u003cp>Use the Laptop to MORA desk test to connect NPS through an FTDI cable to real USHER. It includes the setup illustration, 5 V power and 3.3 V UART pin table, dedicated Center session and mock-then-real capture steps. This is a supported user workflow, not a private regression procedure.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "autopilot-integration.html#if-the-demo-does-not-start", "title": "If The Demo Does Not Start", "text": "\u003cp>Check that the configuration selects the intended flight plan, every session program remains running, and no earlier session owns the ports. Read the first reported error rather than starting duplicate programs. Module guides explain their specific model, cache and sensor requirements.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html", "title": "Control A Canon Camera From A Flight Plan", "text": "\u003cp>The supported CHDK path can request optical zoom and powered lens retraction through USHER. These are separate from taking a photograph. The flight controller sends a request; CHDKbridge performs the camera operation on MORA and reports its outcome.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Canon setup | Camera-control terms\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#before-you-start", "title": "Before You Start", "text": "\u003cp>Use a supported Canon camera with the correct stable CHDK build already running, matching FC/USHER software and the live CHDK backend. Check one standalone camera operation on the bench before adding it to a mission.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Other camera backends currently report these controls as unsupported. Mock camera sessions do not demonstrate mechanical zoom or retraction.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#use-the-supplied-example", "title": "Use The Supplied Example", "text": "\u003cp>The example is \u003ccode>examples/paparazzi/conf/flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_uart_cam_ctrl_demo.xml\u003c/code>. Install the Paparazzi examples, then inspect its ticketed camera-control blocks with your aircraft configuration. It is an integration example, not a complete takeoff-and-landing mission.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Camera actions must not remove the aircraft's normal navigation guidance or make stable flight depend on a camera responding.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#choose-an-action", "title": "Choose An Action", "text": "Action Meaning Important Distinction Zoom 0 Minimum optical zoom Not lens retraction Zoom 128 Middle of the normalized control range Not a guaranteed magnification ratio Zoom 255 Maximum optical zoom Actual steps depend on the camera Retract Request playback mode while keeping power on Confirmation is mode evidence, not a lens-position measurement \u003cp>A later capture can extend the lens again. Mechanical retraction delay also depends on the camera's menu setting; see powered lens retraction.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#request-once-then-read-the-result", "title": "Request Once, Then Read The Result", "text": "\u003cp>For flight-plan authors, the existing \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart\u003c/code> interface provides:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>camera_request = uart_cam_ctrl_zoom(USHER_CAMERA_MASK_CHDK, 128);\ncamera_request = uart_cam_ctrl_retract(USHER_CAMERA_MASK_CHDK);\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>These are alternative examples, not a sequence to send together. Keep the returned \u003ccode>uint32_t\u003c/code> ticket. A zero ticket means the request was not sent; a nonzero ticket means admitted, not completed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>In XML, use a one-shot statement for the chosen action:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;call_once\n  fun=\"camera_request = uart_cam_ctrl_retract(USHER_CAMERA_MASK_CHDK)\"/&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Declare \u003ccode>camera_request\u003c/code> in the plan's variables and include the control header as shown in the supplied example. Do not resend an action on every control tick.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Read \u003ccode>uart_cam_ctrl_command_pending(camera_request)\u003c/code> while waiting, then inspect \u003ccode>uart_cam_ctrl_command_result(camera_request)\u003c/code>. A finished wait is not success. If a later action depends on the camera, provide an explicit failure branch while preserving appropriate aircraft guidance.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#understand-the-outcome", "title": "Understand The Outcome", "text": "Outcome Meaning \u003ccode>NOT_SENT\u003c/code> No admitted command; inspect configuration/readiness \u003ccode>PENDING\u003c/code> Still waiting for an outcome \u003ccode>APPLIED\u003c/code> The backend confirmed the defined effect \u003ccode>BUSY\u003c/code> The camera could not accept this operation \u003ccode>UNSUPPORTED\u003c/code> This backend does not implement it \u003ccode>INVALID_VALUE\u003c/code> The requested value was not accepted \u003ccode>FAILED\u003c/code> Execution reported failure \u003ccode>UNKNOWN\u003c/code> Completion could not be established; do not assume nothing happened \u003cp>A timeout does not prove the camera never acted. Avoid automatic replay of an ambiguous physical operation. Check the camera and diagnostics before deciding what the mission should do next.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-control.html#check-before-onboard-use", "title": "Check Before Onboard Use", "text": "\u003cp>Confirm actual camera behaviour, power, USB reliability and the intended failure response with your setup. A GCS message or successful compilation does not measure lens position, mechanical timing or flight readiness.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html", "title": "Try Different Lenses And Shutters", "text": "\u003cp>The lens emulator adds declared optical and exposure effects to supported MIMIC photographs. Use it to compare views, not to claim a physically calibrated camera. The original clean photo remains available.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MIMIC flight setup | Lens terms | LOOM flight camera selection\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#run-with-usher", "title": "Run With USHER", "text": "\u003cp>Build on the PC, then start a local camera peer with a supplied profile:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 usher soda camera-lens\n./usher --local --mock-map --debug --mock-lens ./bin/camera_lens_aicam.json\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Connect your NPS aircraft using the MIMIC walkthrough, but do not also start a second session-managed USHER. Start with a single AIcam photo at a suitable simulated pose.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To try the wider fisheye profile, restart the peer using \u003ccode>bin/camera_lens_fisheye160.json\u003c/code>. Its quoted 160 degrees is diagonal, not horizontal. A wider view needs more cached surrounding imagery.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#compare-the-two-images", "title": "Compare The Two Images", "text": "\u003cp>For a clean capture such as \u003ccode>ma000006.jpg\u003c/code>, the processed companion is \u003ccode>ma000006.lens.jpg\u003c/code>. Open both at the same scale. USHER selects the derivative before AIcam/LOOM saved-image consumers: outbound LOOM reference links, return-phase neural inference, and SODA all see the same processed photo, only after successful emulation. Real hardware captures are unaffected.\u003c/p> Image Keep It For Original \u003ccode>.jpg\u003c/code> The clean camera view and available flight metadata \u003ccode>.lens.jpg\u003c/code> The declared optical/exposure experiment \u003cp>Check the current log and file time if processing fails. An old derivative left on disk is not a new successful result. Different profiles use the same derivative name, so save comparison copies before processing again.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#process-a-saved-mimic-photo", "title": "Process A Saved MIMIC Photo", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>./bin/soda --local --aicam \\\n  --lens-profile ./bin/camera_lens_aicam.json \\\n  --lens-cache \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map\" /absolute/path/ma000006.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use an original MIMIC photo with its metadata intact and the same suitable terrain cache. This does not take a new photo or change the aircraft's altitude.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#choose-effects-deliberately", "title": "Choose Effects Deliberately", "text": "\u003cp>Make a copy of a supplied JSON profile before changing it. Compare one effect at a time so you know what caused the difference.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For example, copy \u003ccode>bin/camera_lens_aicam.json\u003c/code> to your own \u003ccode>my-lens.json\u003c/code>. Keep the other fields in this complete profile and change selected values; do not replace it with an incomplete JSON fragment.\u003c/p> Effect What To Look For Exposure/brightness Lost shadow or highlight detail Exposure duration and speed Motion blur across the ground Distortion or fisheye field of view Bent lines and changed ground coverage Chromatic aberration Colour separation near edges Vibration Wobble and blur Rolling shutter Different row times causing geometric skew"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#which-settings-change-the-picture", "title": "Which Settings Change The Picture?", "text": "Profile Key Meaning \u003ccode>exposure_ev\u003c/code> Brightness offset; 0 is the baseline \u003ccode>exposure_s\u003c/code> Exposure duration in seconds, not milliseconds \u003ccode>motion_scale\u003c/code> Scale of the aircraft-translation motion effect \u003ccode>shutter\u003c/code> \u003ccode>global\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>rolling\u003c/code> \u003ccode>readout_s\u003c/code> Time across rolling readout, separate from exposure duration \u003ccode>vibration_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vibration_hz\u003c/code> Angular vibration size and frequency \u003ccode>samples\u003c/code> Maximum temporal samples; actual count follows predicted image motion \u003ccode>vignetting\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>optical_blur_px\u003c/code> Residual corner shading and lens softness \u003ccode>sensor_full_well_e\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sensor_read_noise_e\u003c/code> Effective shot/read noise after camera processing \u003ccode>isp_sharpen\u003c/code> Luma sharpening strength \u003cp>Keep brightness fixed and compare \u003ccode>exposure_s\u003c/code> values such as 0.002 and 0.02 with motion enabled. The result also depends on recorded speed and AGL: a stationary view need not blur like a fast low pass. Restart the local peer with \u003ccode>--mock-lens /absolute/path/my-lens.json\u003c/code>, or use that file with the saved-photo command's \u003ccode>--lens-profile\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>The height used comes from the photograph's AGL information. Selecting an example profile does not make the simulated aircraft fly at that profile's example height. A transformed image cannot recover terrain outside the available source imagery.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#pi-zero-2-w-resource-baseline", "title": "Pi Zero 2 W Resource Baseline", "text": "\u003cp>The Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W is the 512 MB reference used when considering onboard resource limits; it is not the only board that can run Sensoraia. Begin these experiments on the PC. Large images, wide fisheye views and multiple temporal samples can be expensive. Check actual combined camera, analysis and operating-system use on the intended MORA board; a successful PC example is not an onboard throughput measurement.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#profile-reference", "title": "Profile Reference", "text": "\u003cp>Profiles are JSON objects with \u003ccode>\"schema\": \"USHER_LENS_PROFILE_V1\"\u003c/code>. Unknown keys, nonfinite numbers, wrong types, and out-of-range values are errors. Omitted optional keys use the defaults below. Copy a supplied profile and edit it; validate before flying:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./bin/camera_lens --validate-profile /absolute/path/flight-camera.json\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> Key Units, accepted values, and default \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>calibration\u003c/code> Nonempty text, at most 160 characters; record the camera/mode and measurement provenance \u003ccode>model\u003c/code> \u003ccode>brown\u003c/code> (default) or \u003ccode>fisheye\u003c/code> \u003ccode>horizontal_fov_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vertical_fov_deg\u003c/code> Brown full field of view, 1..170 degrees; defaults 66.3, 52.3 \u003ccode>diagonal_fov_deg\u003c/code> Fisheye full diagonal field of view, 1..170 degrees; default 160 \u003ccode>distortion\u003c/code> Brown \u003ccode>[k1,k2,p1,p2,k3]\u003c/code> (five zeros by default), or fisheye \u003ccode>[k1,k2,k3,k4]\u003c/code>; each -2..2 \u003ccode>output_width\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>output_height\u003c/code> Both \u003ccode>null\u003c/code>/omitted (keep input dimensions), or both integers 2..4096; product at most 8,388,608 pixels \u003ccode>chromatic_aberration\u003c/code> Relative red/blue ray scaling, 0..0.01; default 0.0005 \u003ccode>exposure_s\u003c/code> Seconds, 0.000001..1; default 0.002 \u003ccode>exposure_ev\u003c/code> Relative exposure stops, -8..8; default 0 \u003ccode>shutter\u003c/code> \u003ccode>rolling\u003c/code> (default) or \u003ccode>global\u003c/code> \u003ccode>readout_s\u003c/code> Seconds across rolling readout, 0..0.2; default 0.02; must be positive for rolling shutter, ignored for global \u003ccode>readout_direction\u003c/code> \u003ccode>top_to_bottom\u003c/code> (default) or \u003ccode>bottom_to_top\u003c/code> \u003ccode>motion_scale\u003c/code> Ground-velocity multiplier, 0..10; default 1 \u003ccode>angular_rate_deg_s\u003c/code> Constant roll, pitch, yaw Euler rates, each -360..360 degrees/s; default \u003ccode>[0,0,0]\u003c/code> \u003ccode>vibration_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vibration_hz\u003c/code> Sinusoidal angular amplitude 0..5 degrees and frequency 0..1000 Hz; defaults 0.03, 80 \u003ccode>vibration_phase_deg\u003c/code> -360..360 degrees; default 0 \u003ccode>samples\u003c/code> Odd integer 1..65 maximum temporal samples; default 7; stationary cases use one. Moving cases target 0.5 px spacing, capped here; compare convergence for long exposures \u003ccode>height_override_m\u003c/code> \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> (default, use flight AGL), or 0.1..10000 metres; non-null requires a map cache \u003ccode>jpeg_quality\u003c/code> Integer 1..100; default 95; lossy JPEG encoding follows rendering \u003ccode>vignetting\u003c/code> Residual linear-light loss at a corner, 0..0.95; default 0 (off); cos-fourth falloff shape \u003ccode>optical_blur_px\u003c/code> Gaussian optical blur sigma in output pixels, 0..5; default 0 (off), applied in linear light \u003ccode>sensor_full_well_e\u003c/code> Effective full-well electrons, 10..1,000,000, or 0 (default, noise off). Controls Gaussian shot-noise approximation, not a measured RAW sensor capacity \u003ccode>sensor_read_noise_e\u003c/code> Effective RMS read noise in electrons, 0..100; default 0; positive values require full well enabled \u003ccode>isp_sharpen\u003c/code> Luma-only unsharp-mask amount, 0..3; default 0 (off); fixed 1 px Gaussian radius \u003ccode>max_tiles\u003c/code> Integer 1..225 for the complete projected tile rectangle; default 121 \u003ccode>threads\u003c/code> Integer 1..32 requested workers; default 4, further restricted by available resources \u003ccode>memory_budget_mb\u003c/code> Integer 96..1024 MiB working-set budget; default 192, further restricted by available resources \u003ccode>f_number\u003c/code> 0.5..64, default 1.79; metadata only, not a depth-of-field/diffraction model \u003ccode>focal_length_mm\u003c/code> \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> (default) or 0.1..1000 mm; metadata only; projection uses field of view \u003cp>Calibration coefficients follow OpenCV Brown and fisheye conventions. Projection assumes a centred principal point and a fixed nadir mount. Calibrate the lens, crop, and saved-image mode together; the stock AI Camera profile's nominal field of view does not measure distortion, readout, or aircraft vibration. The profile JSON is not a PANGEA camera INI or an ONNX assignment.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#realism-boundary", "title": "Realism Boundary", "text": "\u003cp>The renderer integrates motion/exposure in linearized sRGB and then re-encodes JPEG. Time zero is the shot pose at the centre row's mid-exposure. Translation uses the photo's speed/course/AGL; angular rates and sinusoidal vibration are configured, not inferred from neighbouring IMU samples. Brightness is relative EV, not sensor RAW radiometry. Optional residual shading, Gaussian optical softness, signal-dependent shot/read noise, sRGB encoding and luma sharpening approximate the saved camera JPEG, not the actual Bayer sensor/libcamera ISP. Noise is deterministic for the same effective profile and shot metadata, independent of worker count; changed shot metadata changes the seed. Saturated highlights clip. No autoexposure, automatic white balance, Bayer demosaicing, spatial denoising, physical depth of field, weather, buildings, or terrain-height reconstruction is included. The cache itself already contains an aerial image's imaging/compression effects.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use paired captures and held-out real-flight JPEGs to validate geometry, ground sampling, blur, shutter skew, colour, compression, and model performance. An attractive simulated photo is not evidence that the neural-network domain gap is closed. Preserve the effective profile and its EXIF SHA-256 with results.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#calibrate-against-real-captures", "title": "Calibrate Against Real Captures", "text": "\u003cp>The supplied AIcam profile matches USHER's saved 2028x1520, quality 90, 4:2:0 JPEG format (see the AIcam capture guide). Its optical distortion, readout, noise and ISP values are nominal, not measurements. No real AIcam flight-photo calibration is supplied.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Collect real photos in the actual capture mode, with representative    lighting, speed, height and terrain. Keep a separate held-out flight.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Calibrate FOV/distortion using    OpenCV camera calibration.    Measure rolling readout, exposure and vibration independently; photo    statistics alone cannot recover them.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Generate matching simulated views and compare batches:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./bin/camera_lens --measure /data/real-flight/*.jpg &gt; /tmp/real-camera.json\n./bin/camera_lens --measure /data/sim-flight/*.lens.jpg &gt; /tmp/sim-camera.json\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This works on ordinary JPEGs without MIMIC EXIF. JSON contains per-file    values and medians: dimensions, estimated IJG JPEG quality, luma mean and    1st/99th percentiles (0..255), flat-region noise sigma (luma levels),    Laplacian variance (detail), corner/centre luma ratio, and R/G and B/G.    \u003ccode>jpeg_table_error=0\u003c/code> means an exact standard IJG luma quantization table;    vendor tables give an approximation. Colour subsampling is reported per file.    Saturated-only images without usable noise samples are explicitly rejected.    Undefined ratios are \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> and excluded from their batch median. 4. Tune \u003ccode>optical_blur_px\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>isp_sharpen\u003c/code> for detail, then effective full    well/read noise for flat-region grain, \u003ccode>vignetting\u003c/code> for residual shading,    and \u003ccode>exposure_ev\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>jpeg_quality\u003c/code> for brightness/compression. Higher full well    reduces noise. Disable each optional effect with 0. To disable the whole    emulator in LOOM use    \u003ccode>LOOM_CAMERA_PROFILE=none\u003c/code>. 5. Record the camera mode, conditions, measurements and date in \u003ccode>calibration\u003c/code>.    Validate the profile, restart with fresh LOOM references, and evaluate    held-out real-flight neural results before calling the profile representative.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Noise, sharpness, colour and corner ratios are scene-dependent diagnostics, not automatic calibration or proof of a domain match. Use comparable terrain and uniform targets for shading/noise; avoid fitting texture as sensor noise. White-balance differences require matching the real capture/model preprocessing; this profile does not provide a colour-calibration matrix.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#performance-and-validation", "title": "Performance and Validation", "text": "\u003cp>Rendering uses bounded strips and a bounded tile cache. A nonblocking job lock prevents concurrent renderers from multiplying their working sets. The budget is a conservative renderer estimate, not a whole-system RAM guarantee. No Python runtime is required for emulation or LOOM inference.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Each successful render prints \u003ccode>elapsed_s\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>process_peak_rss_kib\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>effective_threads\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>effective_samples\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>estimated_peak_bytes\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>center_translation_blur_px_level_estimate\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>exposure_blur_px_max\u003c/code> estimates motion from nine image probes; \u003ccode>ray_grid_step_px\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ray_interpolation_error_px\u003c/code> report the green/reference channel's node spacing and maximum cell-centre forward-reprojection residual. Each colour grid halves its spacing until that residual is at most 0.02 px, or uses exact per-pixel inversion. This is a sampled accuracy check, not a mathematical bound everywhere. Measure at the actual JPEG resolution, flight AGL, speed, and widest required view. In LOOM, rendering finishes before reference admission/inference; slow rendering can reduce the 3-second flight-plan photo cadence. The shared launcher cancels on owner shutdown or after 90 seconds; this limit does not promise real-time operation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On the development PC, the supplied full-effect AIcam profile rendered a 2028x1520 clean MIMIC source into a 2028x1520 q90 JPEG in 0.13..0.14 s (warm-cache runs including EXIF and \u003ccode>fsync\u003c/code>, eight workers, about 77 MiB peak process RSS). The 160-degree fisheye profile (spanning 35 zoom-18 tiles) took 0.16..0.18 s at 2028x1520 with about 79 MiB peak process RSS. Both used five effective exposure samples; the AIcam reference-grid residual was 0.0161 px. These are renderer-only host measurements on cached real aerial tiles, not complete flight-cadence, MORA, or real-camera accuracy guarantees.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Reduce temporal \u003ccode>samples\u003c/code> (odd values only) after checking convergence, or disable unneeded chromatic aberration to avoid three independent colour-ray passes. Do not lower resolution or remove measured effects solely to obtain a favourable timing number. Keep enough cached terrain for the entire distorted, moving footprint; missing imagery fails rather than filling neural inputs with invented terrain.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-lens.html#when-emulation-fails", "title": "When Emulation Fails", "text": "\u003cp>Check that the source is a supported clean MIMIC photo, the profile parses, its pose/height information is available, and the cache covers the wider view. Read SODA's error rather than assuming the clean image was used as a quiet fallback. Preserve the original while adjusting the profile.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html", "title": "Camera Speed Test", "text": "\u003cp>Measure completed still-image throughput with USHER's built-in \u003ccode>--speedtest\u003c/code>. It uses the selected camera backend, retains its photographs and exits. There is no separate speed-test executable to install.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#prepare-the-target", "title": "Prepare The Target", "text": "\u003cp>Build on the development PC and follow deployment to install the current ARM64 release on MORA. Never compile on MORA. For a PC-attached camera, build with \u003ccode>make native\u003c/code> and run from the checkout instead. Use the camera's normal settings, output storage and connection. Ensure enough free disk space for eleven images per run and any configured companion files.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Only one process may own the camera and USHER endpoint. On MORA, connect over SSH, note whether \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> is active, and stop it before testing:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local\nsystemctl is-active usher.service\nsudo -n systemctl stop usher.service\ncd /home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use the actual host and installation directory. If sudo requires a password, authenticate directly in your terminal; never put the password into a command or log. Stop any other manually started camera owner too.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#run-a-test", "title": "Run A Test", "text": "\u003cp>Choose exactly one real image backend:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --lwircam --speedtest\n./usher --aicam --speedtest\n./usher --aicam --native-photo --speedtest\n./usher --spottercam --speedtest\n./usher --spottercam --native-photo --speedtest\n./usher --chdk --speedtest\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Run only the command for your connected camera. No flight controller or UART connection is needed. Do not combine this mode with daemon/local simulation, mock images, EARcam, pose logging or LWIR processing options. The worker's normal settings still apply, so record the settings used for comparisons. The two \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code> commands use the same direct libcamera capture and do not load or run an object-recognition model. Plain \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code> uses the OS camera tool, while plain \u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code> uses its configured detector. Set \u003ccode>USHER_AI_CAM_TIMING=1\u003c/code> to report native frame-acquisition and JPEG-save times separately on standard error.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Capture a log without adding terminal backpressure to the benchmark:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --lwircam --speedtest &gt; /tmp/lwir-speedtest.log 2&gt;&amp;1\nresult=$?\ncat /tmp/lwir-speedtest.log\nprintf 'exit=%s\\n' \"$result\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Require exit status zero and ten successful measured captures. Initialization is reported separately; one additional warm-up capture is excluded. Each run uses a new \u003ccode>speedtest-*\u003c/code> subdirectory under the backend's normal photo root, printed in the final report. Original images are retained, not overwritten. Inspect the saved JPEGs; the benchmark checks the returned path and nonempty regular file, not complete JPEG decoding or temperature-layer correctness.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Repeat several times to distinguish repeatable throughput from a lucky frame boundary. Keep all results, including failures, rather than reporting only the fastest run. On Raspberry Pi, record \u003ccode>vcgencmd get_throttled\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vcgencmd measure_temp\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>free -h\u003c/code> and the output filesystem alongside the application versions and camera settings.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#interpret-the-report", "title": "Interpret The Report", "text": "Report Meaning Practical sustained speed Ten sequential captures divided by elapsed sequence time, including between-call overhead Average camera-call speed Ten divided by the sum of timed backend calls Average / fastest / slowest capture time Latency distribution over those ten calls Fastest single capture Best individual call; its reciprocal is not demonstrated sustained throughput Initialization Camera preparation before the warm-up; excluded from the rate \u003cp>Backend-call timing includes the work required by that backend to return a saved image, including encoding/storage where performed there. UART traffic, the normal flight-trigger loop, USHER EXIF/SODA processing and initialization are not part of that number. The sustained interval includes between-call validation/publication work, except work following the final timed return. Do not treat this short benchmark as an endurance or whole-board headroom test.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The Tiny1-C supplies a 25 fps stream, but fresh-request still capture plus durable publication can deliver fewer saved images per second. Kernel frame timestamps are not independently validated exposure timestamps. See LWIRcam for startup, freshness, JPEG quality and exact temperature preservation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#measured-ai-camera-example", "title": "Measured AI Camera Example", "text": "\u003cp>On 2026-11-01, a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W MORA and its connected IMX500 were tested with temporary ARM64 binaries and \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> unavailable on the process PATH. Each run saved ten measured 2028x1520 photographs after one excluded warm-up. Both native selections used the same quality-90 4:2:0 IJG JPEG path, without a detector model.\u003c/p> Native Selection Run 1 Photos/s Run 2 Photos/s Run 3 Photos/s \u003ccode>--aicam --native-photo\u003c/code> 1.190 1.189 1.190 \u003ccode>--spottercam --native-photo\u003c/code> 1.190 1.190 1.190 \u003cp>All 66 JPEGs decoded at the expected dimensions; their sensor-frame sequences and timestamps increased within each run. No detection sidecars were produced. The board reported \u003ccode>throttled=0x0\u003c/code> before and after, with temperatures of 36.5 and 39.7 C. USHER was restored afterward. This is a short capture test, not an endurance or complete flight-processing measurement.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Earlier on the same board, three plain \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code> runs using \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> measured 2.105, 2.121 and 2.103 photos/s. That path used the tool's quality-93 default and zero-shutter-lag mode, so the two results do not isolate camera access alone: their capture and JPEG pipelines differ.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#measured-mora-example", "title": "Measured MORA Example", "text": "\u003cp>On 2026-10-30, the current ARM64 release was deployed to \u003ccode>theatre\u003c/code>, a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W Rev 1.0, and tested with the real Tiny1-C. Five independent \u003ccode>./usher --lwircam --speedtest\u003c/code> runs completed all 50 measured captures plus five excluded warm-ups. USHER was stopped for the tests and restored afterward.\u003c/p> Run Sustained Photos/s Mean Camera Call, ms 1 8.255 121.103 2 8.387 119.184 3 8.427 118.612 4 8.464 118.102 5 8.402 118.977 \u003cp>The best observed sustained rate was 8.464 saved photos/s, about 118 ms per image. The fastest individual call across these runs was 103.883 ms; its reciprocal, 9.626 photos/s, is not a sustained result. Initialization was 3.28-3.39 seconds and excluded, as was the extra warm-up capture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The worker used native V4L2, bundled IJG JPEG, default quality 95 and 4:4:4 sampling, with native inference settings (no model analysis or mock input). Images were saved to the board's normal microSD-backed ext4 root filesystem, under \u003ccode>/home/air/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code>. No extra raw companions or statistics were requested. Durable publication remained enabled. The board reported \u003ccode>throttled=0x0\u003c/code> before and after, with temperatures of 46.2 and 45.6 C. All 55 JPEGs were subsequently decoded and their embedded 256x192 temperature layers validated on the PC; each contained 49,152 valid temperature samples. No raw companions were retained, so this is not an independent raw-byte comparison. This is a short board-backed throughput observation, not an absolute hardware maximum, endurance test or thermal-accuracy qualification.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Board logs are retained at \u003ccode>/home/air/lwir-speedtest-20261030-dFvUqFOE/\u003c/code>; the logs identify each image directory. A development-PC evidence copy also retains all images and validation output.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "camera-speedtest.html#restore-the-service", "title": "Restore The Service", "text": "\u003cp>If the service was active before testing, restart it even after a failed test:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo -n systemctl start usher.service\nsystemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service\njournalctl -u usher.service -n 30 --no-pager\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Leave a previously inactive service inactive. Retain the logs and image paths with the tested release before removing any benchmark output.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html", "title": "Use A Canon Camera With CHDKbridge", "text": "\u003cp>CHDKbridge lets USHER request photographs and supported controls from a Canon camera over USB. CHDK runs inside the camera; CHDKbridge runs on MORA or your PC. You need both\u2014CHDK loaded on the camera's SD card and CHDKbridge on the host\u2014for this camera path.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Canon-camera terms | Capture with USHER\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#prepare-the-camera", "title": "Prepare The Camera", "text": "\u003cp>Obtain a stable, non-beta CHDK v1.6 build matching the exact camera model and Canon firmware revision. Sensoraia does not install CHDK, prepare the SD card or replace the camera's firmware for you.\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Supported cameras\u003c/li> \u003cli>Downloads and build selection\u003c/li> \u003cli>CHDK 1.6 manual\u003c/li> \u003cli>Prepare an SD card\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Verify that CHDK loads after a power cycle and that the camera can save a normal JPEG. Leave CHDK's USB Remote shutter-switch feature disabled;  it is different from USB/PTP control.\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Firmware\u003c/li> \u003cli>Interesting other library\u003c/li> \u003c/ul>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#take-your-first-photo", "title": "Take Your First Photo", "text": "\u003cp>Build on the development PC. Stop USHER and other camera owners before a standalone bench capture, connect a USB data cable and provide reliable power.\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 chdkbridge\nbin/chdkbridge --list\nbin/chdkbridge --info\nbin/chdkbridge photo-001.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use a new filename in a writable directory. Open the saved JPEG and check focus and exposure. Ordinary capture saves the original on the camera's SD card and downloads it. Existing destination files are not overwritten.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On MORA, deployment installs \u003ccode>99-chdkbridge.rules\u003c/code>, which gives the \u003ccode>plugdev\u003c/code> group (and so the USHER service) access to Canon cameras in PTP mode. If a desktop has mounted the camera, CHDKbridge releases that mount when it starts.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If several cameras are connected, use the port printed by \u003ccode>--list\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --port 1-3 photo-002.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The example port is not universal. USB bus/device addresses can change after reconnecting; verify the selected device each time.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#choose-settings-deliberately", "title": "Choose Settings Deliberately", "text": "\u003cp>Unspecified standalone settings are left unchanged. Set only the options your camera supports, and inspect a real photo after changing them:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --iso-mode 400 --flash 2 --resolution 0 --quality 0 photo-003.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> Option Meaning \u003ccode>--iso-mode\u003c/code> Camera ISO-mode property; supported values depend on the model \u003ccode>--flash 2\u003c/code> Flash off \u003ccode>--resolution 0\u003c/code> Usually the largest image; confirm your camera's mapping \u003ccode>--quality 0\u003c/code> Requested superfine JPEG quality where supported \u003ccode>--date-stamp 0\u003c/code> Date stamp off \u003ccode>--configure\u003c/code> Apply supplied settings without taking a photo \u003cp>Readback errors can leave earlier settings changed. Check the camera before retrying rather than assuming the whole operation was rolled back.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#focus-and-exposure", "title": "Focus And Exposure", "text": "\u003cp>Start with autofocus and verify sharpness. For a camera that supports the requested mode, examples include:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --focus-mode af photo-af.jpg\nbin/chdkbridge --focus-mode manual --focus-distance inf photo-infinity.jpg\nbin/chdkbridge --shutter 1/1000 --iso-real 400 photo-exposure.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Manual/infinity focus, adjustable aperture and ND-filter controls depend on the camera. A successful distance/property readback does not establish image sharpness or actual exposure quality. Do not choose infinity merely because the aircraft will fly; measure the useful focus for your optics and scene.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003ccode>--iso-mode\u003c/code> is not the same as a real-sensitivity override. Use \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code> for supported alternatives and ranges rather than combining conflicting exposure options. Save comparison photographs before choosing a mission setup.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#zoom", "title": "Zoom", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge zoom 0\nbin/chdkbridge zoom 128\nbin/chdkbridge zoom 255\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>These are separate commands. Zero is the wide end and 255 the tele end of the normalized optical range. The middle value is not a guaranteed 2x ratio. Zoom does not take a photo, and changing it can require a fresh focus check.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#powered-lens-retraction", "title": "Powered Lens Retraction", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --retract-lens\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This requests playback mode without powering the camera off. To request prompt retraction on cameras offering the setting, select Lens Retraction: 0 sec in Canon's menu. Confirm the physical lens has retracted: playback confirmation alone does not measure its position. A later capture can extend it again.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#keep-the-camera-awake", "title": "Keep The Camera Awake", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --configure --keep-awake 1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This changes CHDK's idle-shutdown policy to Always; \u003ccode>--keep-awake 0\u003c/code> selects Never, not restoration of an earlier intermediate setting. It consumes power and cannot prevent low-battery shutdown or wake an already powered-off camera. Omitting the option leaves the setting unchanged.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Check the result without changing anything. This reads CHDK's setting and, when the firmware reports them, battery voltage and temperatures:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --power-status\n# idle_shutdown_policy=3; keep_awake=1; battery_mv=3987; optics_c=30; ...\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>It exits with an error unless the policy is Always, so it suits a pre-flight check. The bridge never powers the camera off or restarts it, and it never resets the USB device when a command ends.\u003c/p> \u003cp>When the camera exposes them, the same read-only report includes \u003ccode>zoom_step\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>focus_mm\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>focus_mode\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>af_lock\u003c/code>. Focus mode 0 is autofocus and 1 is manual in the bridge's supported property convention. A focus-distance readback alone does not establish sharpness.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USHER applies \u003ccode>--keep-awake 1\u003c/code> on every shot by default (see below), so the policy is re-asserted before each exposure and a camera that lost its saved setting is corrected on the next shot. Before flight, charge the battery and run \u003ccode>--power-status\u003c/code>: no software setting can prevent shutdown from an empty battery or a camera fault.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#retrieve-a-camera-crash-log", "title": "Retrieve A Camera Crash Log", "text": "\u003cp>After an unexpected camera shutdown, switch it on without taking another photo. In CHDK's Miscellaneous menu, enable Enable Lua Native Calls? and confirm its prompt. Stop other camera owners before retrieving the log:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --timeout 60 --usb-timeout 5000 --romlog camera-crash.log\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This exports Canon's stored ROM crash log to \u003ccode>A/ROMLOG.LOG\u003c/code> on the camera's SD card and downloads it to a new local file, limited to 64 KiB. It does not enter record mode, shoot, zoom or power off the camera. The native-call permission must be enabled on the camera; the bridge does not change it. Disable that permission again after the diagnostic when it is no longer needed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Read the log's timestamp, assertion or exception, and task name before repeating the failed capture. A stored log can describe an earlier failure; compare it with the host's USB and capture logs. For manual export instead, use CHDK's Miscellaneous &gt; Debug parameters &gt; Save ROM crash log command.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#optional-direct-usb-capture", "title": "Optional Direct USB Capture", "text": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>--direct-download\u003c/code> requests direct JPEG transfer rather than an SD-saved JPEG. Support depends on the actual CHDK camera port. If unavailable, resolve compatibility or use ordinary capture; do not assume a failed direct request can be replayed without another exposure.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The bridge reports \u003ccode>direct JPEG unsupported; USB capture mask=N\u003c/code> when the camera port lacks it. The mask is a bit set: 1 JPEG, 2 RAW, 4 YUV. A Canon SX730 HS (CHDK PTP 2.9) reports mask 6, so only RAW and YUV transfers exist and direct JPEG capture is not available on that camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#use-with-usher", "title": "Use With USHER", "text": "\u003cp>To select CHDKbridge as MORA's active camera service, set \u003ccode>CAMERA = chdk\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> at the Sensoraia root or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=chdk\u003c/code> when deploying:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy CAMERA=chdk MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>See the deployment guide for full MORA verification steps.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#choose-ushers-capture-profile", "title": "Choose USHER's Capture Profile", "text": "\u003cp>USHER supplies its own capture profile on each shot. A setting left by a standalone command is not necessarily what USHER will use. For example, select the Canon ISO-mode request while packaging on the PC:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 mora-package USHER_CHDK_ISO_MODE=400\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Keep this choice consistent when rebuilding. The default ISO request is 1600 and the default JPEG quality is 2 (normal). Related settings include \u003ccode>USHER_CHDK_FLASH\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>USHER_CHDK_QUALITY\u003c/code>. Confirm supported values and actual photos on your camera; the mode number alone is not measured exposure. Packaging does not activate the service.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The ISO request is applied only when the camera's mode dial is in P, Tv, Av or M. In AUTO and scene modes Canon chooses ISO itself and does not accept a user value; forcing one there made the SX730 HS exposure driver assert (\u003ccode>DegeenGain.c\u003c/code> in its crash log) and power off when shooting zoomed in dim light. CHDKbridge therefore leaves ISO to the camera in those modes and still applies the other settings. Set the dial to P if you want the configured ISO. On the SX730 HS, ISO 3200 at a zoomed position also powered the camera off, so check any value above 1600 on your own camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#increase-the-shot-rate", "title": "Increase The Shot Rate", "text": "\u003cp>For the SX730 HS, start with Canon Image Review: Off, flash off and CHDK RAW saving off. Disable continuous/servo AF when using a focus lock. Image review is a camera-menu setting here, not an option applied by CHDKbridge. These settings remove avoidable camera work; their timing benefit has not been measured on this setup.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The largest measured improvement is JPEG compression, without changing resolution, which is why USHER uses quality 2 (normal) by default. In standalone SX730 captures on MORA, resolution 0 with quality 0 (superfine) produced roughly 8.4--10.5 MB files in 4.85--5.3 s once warm; quality 2 produced roughly 2.8--3.9 MB files in 2.7--3.1 s at the wide end and about 1.9 MB in 3.4--3.5 s zoomed to \u003ccode>zoom 128\u003c/code> indoors. These are scene-dependent capture-and-download times, not a guaranteed flight cadence. Normal quality loses some fine detail through stronger compression; inspect small features before relying on it for mapping or detection. To keep superfine JPEGs instead:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 mora-package USHER_CHDK_QUALITY=0\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Packaging alone does not deploy it. Keep the setting on subsequent builds. Do not probe other resolution codes: the SX730 HS uses 0 (L), 2 (M1), 3 (M2) and 5 (S), and an unsupported code ended in a USB disconnect.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To compare fixed focus, with no other camera owner:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --usb-timeout 5000 --focus-mode auto --focus-distance inf photo-focus.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Here \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code> selects the CHDK port's supported manual-focus or AF-lock distance mechanism; it does not request autofocus. Unsupported modes or rejected distance readback fail explicitly. Check sharpness at the intended zoom and subject distance. If the comparison succeeds, carry it into USHER using \u003ccode>USHER_CHDK_FOCUS_MODE=auto USHER_CHDK_FOCUS_DISTANCE=inf\u003c/code> on the packaging command. Recheck after zoom changes; fixed infinity focus measured about 0.2 s faster per wide-angle shot on the bench but has not been checked zoomed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>M or Tv mode and a suitable short shutter time can limit exposure time and motion blur, but do not eliminate all camera processing. Lower ISO is not inherently faster: in dim light it may require a longer exposure. Select daylight white balance for consistent color when appropriate; no measured speed gain is established here. The existing \u003ccode>--shutter\u003c/code> and ISO overrides are available for comparison, but should not replace a correctly exposed photo with a dark one merely to shorten capture time.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USHER overlaps CHDK metadata writing with the next capture. The camera/USB capture lane is still single-flight, so triggers faster than capture and download can finish are not guaranteed to produce photos. Direct JPEG USB capture is unavailable on this SX730 port; it is not a speed option here.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On a camera whose CHDK port supports direct JPEG capture, USHER can ask the bridge for it instead of saving to the SD card first:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 mora-package USHER_CHDK_DIRECT=1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The default is 0 (SD-saved capture). USHER does not fall back to the other mode when direct capture fails, because that would expose twice.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Every shot also carries \u003ccode>--keep-awake 1\u003c/code> (CHDK \"Always\" idle-shutdown prevention). Select \u003ccode>USHER_CHDK_KEEP_AWAKE=0\u003c/code> to request Never, or \u003ccode>USHER_CHDK_KEEP_AWAKE=\u003c/code> (empty) to leave the camera's own setting alone.\u003c/p> \u003cp>With no other camera owner, a separately managed bench session uses:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --chdk --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Do not run standalone CHDKbridge commands while USHER owns the camera. For mission controls use the camera-control guide.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Symptom Check No camera Power, USB data cable and the selected port Camera busy Desktop photo importers or another camera owner CHDK/protocol rejected Exact camera firmware and stable CHDK build Setting rejected Your camera's supported values and current state Timeout after capture Inspect the camera and destination before deciding whether to request another photo Blurred JPEG Focus, zoom, exposure duration, vibration and real scene detail \u003cp>When an earlier run was interrupted (stopped, timed out or unplugged), the camera can still hold that run's PTP session or unsent data. CHDKbridge then prints \u003ccode>Recovering camera PTP state left by an earlier run\u003c/code>, sends the still-image class reset once and opens a fresh session; it never resets the USB port. Likewise it waits for a camera script left running by an interrupted run instead of killing it. USHER removes partial \u003ccode>cNNNNNN.jpg.part.*\u003c/code> downloads left in the photo directory by a forcibly stopped controller. A camera that has powered off still needs to be switched on again.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Keep usable originals and the diagnostic. A successful build or USB reply does not replace a bench check of the actual camera configuration.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chdkbridge.html#recover-before-requesting-another-photo", "title": "Recover Before Requesting Another Photo", "text": "\u003cp>A timeout after triggering can mean the exposure succeeded but transfer or confirmation failed. Check the camera's SD card and output directory before repeating it. If you know the saved camera path, download the existing JPEG without another exposure:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge --download A/DCIM/100CANON/IMG_0042.JPG recovered.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use the actual camera filename and a new local destination, with \u003ccode>--port\u003c/code> when needed. Do not delete originals or kill an unrelated camera script to force recovery. Once the camera is idle and the connection healthy, a new independent capture can be requested.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chemical-vapors-and-emissions.html", "title": "Chemical Vapors and Emissions", "text": "\u003cp>There is more to a landscape than a camera can show. With the right onboard sensors, a drone can sample carbon dioxide in the air, look for methane leaks, or survey radiation emitted from the ground. It is another way to explore a place: not just what it looks like, but what is happening around it. Pair the readings with the aircraft's position and time, and an invisible change becomes something you can follow, compare and investigate, whether you are checking a biogas installation, studying air quality or looking for an unusual emission.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chemical-vapors-and-emissions.html#ten-things-a-drone-can-detect", "title": "Ten Things A Drone Can Detect", "text": "\u003cp>Each example needs a suitable sensor; no single instrument detects everything in this list.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Carbon dioxide (CO2). Measure changes around combustion sources,    industrial processes or natural releases with an infrared gas sensor.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Methane (CH4). Look for leaks from gas infrastructure, landfills or    biogas facilities with a methane-sensitive instrument.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Carbon monoxide (CO). Detect this combustion gas in smoke or exhaust    using an appropriate gas sensor.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Ammonia (NH3). Check for releases around livestock buildings,    fertilizer handling or industrial refrigeration.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Hydrogen sulfide (H2S). Detect a gas associated with wastewater,    decomposing organic material and some industrial operations.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Sulfur dioxide (SO2). Investigate emissions from sulfur-containing    fuels, industrial processes or volcanic activity.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Nitrogen dioxide (NO2). Study a combustion-related air pollutant    found in traffic and industrial exhaust.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Volatile organic compounds (VOCs). Screen for groups of vapors from    fuels, paints and solvents. Identifying an individual compound needs a    more selective instrument or analysis.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Airborne particles, such as dust and smoke. Measure particle levels    with an optical particle sensor; particles are not gases or vapors.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Gamma radiation. Survey radiation levels with a dedicated detector.     A suitable gamma spectrometer can also help identify radioactive materials.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "chemical-vapors-and-emissions.html#different-signals-different-sensors", "title": "Different Signals, Different Sensors", "text": "\u003cp>For many gas measurements, a small pump draws air past a sensor on the aircraft. Other instruments use light to detect particular gases along a viewing path. Radiation sensing is different: the detector measures radiation reaching it, not the smell or chemical makeup of an air sample. A normal camera or thermal imager does not replace these specialist instruments.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Wind moves and dilutes a gas plume, and propeller airflow can change the air reaching an inlet. Sensor placement, response time and calibration therefore matter just as much as where the drone flies. For radiation surveys, distance, shielding, background levels and measurement time affect what can be detected. A higher reading is a useful clue, not automatically the source location or the amount being released.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chemical-vapors-and-emissions.html#bringing-a-new-sensor-to-sensoraia", "title": "Bringing A New Sensor To Sensoraia", "text": "\u003cp>Gas, particle and radiation payloads need their own compatible connection and Sensoraia integration; these are not ready-to-use sensor backends included with the camera and microphone modules. The aim is familiar: keep each reading with its units, measurement time and flight context, so it can become a useful part of an airborne sensing workflow.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Explore Sensoraia's current sensors or see how the autopilot link connects airborne observations to the rest of a mission.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "chemical-vapors-and-emissions.html#explore-real-world-examples", "title": "Explore Real-World Examples", "text": "\u003cp>At the moment there is not yet a sensor module in this class but if you need it now then add code andd behaviour to make it a reallity. We look froward to integrate a new class of sensor create by you into Sensoraia. Please make a pull request  if you feel you are ready to share it with the world.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html", "title": "Laptop To MORA Desk Test", "text": "\u003cp>Run the simulated aircraft on the laptop and real USHER on MORA. The FTDI cable supplies 5 V power and carries camera requests/replies over a 3.3 V UART. Start with a mock photo on the board, then use the real camera.\u003c/p> \u003cp>PC demo setup | Prepare the reference Pi board\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#1-connect-power-and-uart", "title": "1. Connect Power And UART", "text": "\u003cp>Make connections while unpowered. Use the cable's 5 V power lead and 3.3 V UART signals; those voltages have different jobs.\u003c/p> FTDI Lead Pi Zero 2 W Reference Pin And Purpose 5 V Pin 2, 5 V; power MORA and its camera GND Pin 6, GND; common ground TXD Pin 10, GPIO15 / RXD; laptop sends to MORA RXD Pin 8, GPIO14 / TXD; MORA replies to laptop \u003cp>Check connector labels, not assumed wire colours\u2014adapter makers treat wire colours as creative expression, and feeding 5 V into a 3.3 V GPIO pin ends the bench test faster than intended. Never connect the 5 V lead to TXD/RXD or the Pi's 3.3 V pin. Connect the camera ribbon while MORA is off, then plug the FTDI cable into the laptop. Keep a real flight controller disconnected for this simulated-aircraft exercise.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MORA needs the installed release, matching sensor runtime and configured UART. Both ends use 115200 baud. Wi-Fi/SSH starts programs and retrieves files; the FTDI UART carries the camera protocol, not an SSH login.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#2-choose-the-laptop-port", "title": "2. Choose The Laptop Port", "text": "\u003cp>On the laptop:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use your adapter's stable by-id path, or its actual device such as \u003ccode>/dev/ttyUSB0\u003c/code>. The laptop account needs serial access, normally through \u003ccode>dialout\u003c/code> on Debian/Ubuntu.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Stop the previous local session. The example launcher automatically prefers a connected MORA cable and skips local sensor companions. Hardware sessions pin both NPS endpoints to that cable; startup refuses a leftover \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> instead of silently testing the PC. Do not run a serial monitor on the FTDI port.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#3-prepare-nps-for-the-cable", "title": "3. Prepare NPS For The Cable", "text": "\u003cp>Use your working fixed-wing NPS aircraft and the public setup. From the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>export PAPARAZZI_HOME=\"$HOME/paparazzi\"\n./kickstart.sh\nmake -C \"$PAPARAZZI_HOME\" -f Makefile.ac AIRCRAFT=Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM nps.compile\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>By default, the \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code> transport uses \u003ccode>/dev/ttyUSB0\u003c/code> whenever no local PC \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> peer is running. If several USB-serial adapters are attached, pass \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_...\u003c/code> via \u003ccode>DIGITAL_CAM_UART_FALLBACK_DEVICE=\"$MORA_UART_DEVICE\"\u003c/code> on the \u003ccode>make\u003c/code> command above (after removing \u003ccode>var/aircrafts/Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM/nps/modules/digital_cam/uart_cam_ctrl.o\u003c/code>). This photo-survey plan requests the AI camera slot; USHER on MORA selects whether to capture a mock or physical image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Start Paparazzi Center:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cd \"$PAPARAZZI_HOME\"\n./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Select \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code>, aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code> (or your own configured aircraft) and nps (click Build if you have not built \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code> yet). Choose \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> and Sensoraia MORA desk NPS. This session starts \u003ccode>Simulator\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Data Link\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Server\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>PprzGCS\u003c/code>, with no local sensor peer, so \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code> connects straight to MORA over the FTDI cable.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#4-check-real-uart-with-a-mock-photo", "title": "4. Check Real UART With A Mock Photo", "text": "\u003cp>In another laptop terminal, connect to MORA over SSH and run foreground USHER against \u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh -t air@theatre.local 'sudo systemctl stop usher.service &amp;&amp; cd /home/air/sensoraia &amp;&amp; ./usher --serial /dev/serial0 --aicam --test --debug; status=$?; sudo systemctl start usher.service; exit $status'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Substitute your MORA SSH target (\u003ccode>air@theatre.local\u003c/code>) and installation path (\u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia\u003c/code>). The command stops the managed service, runs foreground USHER over SSH and restores \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> after the foreground command ends.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If you prefer running each step interactively in an SSH terminal on MORA:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo systemctl stop usher.service\ncd /home/air/sensoraia\n./usher --serial /dev/serial0 --aicam --test --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use your actual installation path. End foreground USHER with Ctrl+C when finished, then run \u003ccode>sudo systemctl start usher.service\u003c/code>. Do not leave the managed service and foreground program competing for the UART or camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#optional-full-hands-free-automation-with-homeairpasstxt", "title": "Optional: Full Hands-Free Automation With \u003ccode>/home/air/pass.txt\u003c/code>", "text": "\u003cp>If you want completely hands-free deployment (\u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code>) and SSH desk testing with zero password typing, you can place your MORA account's \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> password in \u003ccode>/home/air/pass.txt\u003c/code> on MORA (replacing \u003ccode>replacewithyourpass\u003c/code> with your actual password) and lock its permissions to owner-only (\u003ccode>chmod 600\u003c/code>):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'umask 077 &amp;&amp; printf \"%s\\n\" \"replacewithyourpass\" &gt; ~/pass.txt &amp;&amp; chmod 600 ~/pass.txt'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Edit \u003ccode>~/pass.txt\u003c/code> on MORA so it contains your real \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> password (the literal placeholder \u003ccode>replacewithyourpass\u003c/code> is safely ignored until you change it). \u003ccode>deploy_mora.sh\u003c/code> automatically detects \u003ccode>~/pass.txt\u003c/code> on MORA and authenticates \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> without prompting. You can also start and restore foreground bench runs directly from your PC in one line:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'sudo -S -p \"\" systemctl stop usher.service &lt; /home/air/pass.txt &amp;&amp; cd /home/air/sensoraia &amp;&amp; exec ./usher --chdk --debug'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>After the foreground process exits, restore the managed service:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'sudo -S -p \"\" systemctl start usher.service &lt; /home/air/pass.txt'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Wait for USHER to report \u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code> and readiness. Start the Center desk session, wait for live telemetry/GPS, then select Takeoff. Watch requests arrive on MORA and mock JPEGs complete during the survey. Do not use \u003ccode>--local\u003c/code> on MORA: the physical UART is the point of this step.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#5-change-to-the-physical-camera", "title": "5. Change To The Physical Camera", "text": "\u003cp>Stop the desk session and end foreground USHER with Ctrl+C before changing the backend. With the AI Camera prepared:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh -t air@theatre.local 'sudo systemctl stop usher.service &amp;&amp; cd /home/air/sensoraia &amp;&amp; ./usher --serial /dev/serial0 --aicam --debug; status=$?; sudo systemctl start usher.service; exit $status'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Repeat the desk flight. Without \u003ccode>--test\u003c/code>, this uses actual camera exposures. Other sensors need their corresponding camera mask and mission selection, not just a different backend flag.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The desk session checks UART/capture operation. It does not run the PC T1..T5 ranking companion. Physical pixels paired with simulated pose do not measure real-flight geolocation accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#inspect-results-and-finish", "title": "Inspect Results And Finish", "text": "\u003cp>For the default account, in another laptop terminal:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'ls -lt ~/Pictures/sensoraia/ | head'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use the actual output directory printed by USHER. The files are on MORA, not in a PC companion's run directory. Check the new readable JPEG, completion diagnostic and EXIF; a GCS shot marker alone does not prove capture finished.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Let NPS land, stop its session programs and end foreground USHER with Ctrl+C. Check that \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> is active again. To return to PC-only simulation, disconnect the cable, unset any explicit \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE\u003c/code> override, and restart the normal session. An explicitly empty \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE\u003c/code> can force PC-only operation while the cable remains connected.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "desk-test.html#if-nothing-arrives", "title": "If Nothing Arrives", "text": "\u003cp>Check crossed TX/RX, common ground, serial permissions, the actual laptop device and MORA's disabled serial login console. Check that no local peer is taking precedence and no second application owns a UART. If requests arrive but capture fails, investigate the sensor error rather than rewiring a working link.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html", "title": "Diplacam: Choose Where To Measure Next", "text": "\u003cp>Diplacam uses measurements already collected to propose eight useful places to sample next. Its map belief tracks both estimated values and uncertainty, so the next proposal can change as new observations arrive.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The supplied experiment samples a numerical field from a CSV. It runs the native planner and selected ONNX model, not a physical full-spectrum-camera driver. Start with the standalone command, then try the rotorcraft simulation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Planning terms | Paparazzi setup\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#try-a-standalone-plan", "title": "Try A Standalone Plan", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 diplacam\nbin/diplacam --field modules/dipla_cam/data/map_26_NAIP_grid_counts.csv --east 4 --north 4\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The command prints eight \u003ccode>slot,east_m,north_m\u003c/code> rows. Internal slots 0..7 correspond to P1..P8 in the example. This does not start a flight or move waypoints. Read model/field diagnostics on stderr if planning fails.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#choose-the-model-and-field", "title": "Choose The Model And Field", "text": "\u003cp>The assignment lives in \u003ccode>modules/dipla_cam/diplacam_settings.ini\u003c/code>. Keep the graph, descriptor and deployment metadata together. Paths are relative to the settings file; \u003ccode>--settings\u003c/code> selects another assignment.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The current field uses \u003ccode>x,y,count\u003c/code> CSV rows on a 51-by-51, two-metre grid covering 0..100 m east/north. Only the sensor footprint is sampled into the belief. Unmeasured field truth is not handed to the network as the answer. The supplied profile uses a two-grid-cell flight margin: XY stays within 4..96 m. This also applies to the standalone command's printed points. \u003ccode>flight_margin_cells\u003c/code> changes that inset for both planners without changing the model grid or the demo height.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The worker INI selects the field, not its geographic location. The flight plan chooses where the aircraft begins the survey; Diplacam anchors the field to its first accepted position. For custom experiments, an absolute field path avoids working-directory ambiguity.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#place-the-survey", "title": "Place The Survey", "text": "\u003cp>The existing START command arms the survey. Its first valid admitted shot provides the measured latitude/longitude that becomes local (50,50), the centre of the 100-by-100-metre field. Local X increases east and Y increases north. With the supplied two-cell margin, targets span approximately 46 m in each direction from that measured centre.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Paparazzi owns the approach and GPS checks. The supplied Begin survey block flies to CENTRE, waits there at the configured height, and starts Diplacam. Choose that entry waypoint using Paparazzi's normal local or geographic coordinates; its existing relative-waypoint facilities can also position an entry manoeuvre. No geographic origin is duplicated in the worker settings.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The anchor is the accepted shot's actual position, not an assumption that the aircraft is exactly at the waypoint. The mission's area check around CENTRE allows for entry-position error as well as the field extent. Keep that check consistent with the entry location when adapting the mission. HOME and landing geometry remain independent.\u003c/p> Event Geographic Frame And Belief START from stopped Wait for the first valid admitted shot, then capture its position once Another START while active Retain the anchor and belief; request the next eight-point chunk Duplicate or rejected shot Do not establish or move the anchor STOP, then START Start a new survey with a new first-position anchor and belief \u003cp>Shot numbering does not have to start at one. The existing session token and shot number correlate commands and measurements; they do not replace the flight controller's GPS-validity checks. Before anchoring or before a complete route is ready, replies cannot authorize waypoint updates.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Standalone planning has no geographic frame: \u003ccode>--east\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--north\u003c/code> select an observation directly on the CSV grid and still default to \u003ccode>(4,4)\u003c/code>. To exercise the worker's centred first-observation case with a fresh standalone stream:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/diplacam --field modules/dipla_cam/data/map_26_NAIP_grid_counts.csv --east 50 --north 50\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#how-the-method-works", "title": "How The Method Works", "text": "\u003cp>A Gaussian Process updates the estimated field and uncertainty from the observed footprint. A learned diffusion model, trained to imitate informative plans, then supports twenty denoising steps that produce eight locations. This is a measurement planner, not an object detector or a motor controller.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Each accepted shot supplies one measurement footprint. Measurements include reproducible Gaussian sensor noise, separate from the diffusion sampler's random stream. They update the GP in order while the aircraft follows its retained route, rather than being replaced by whichever pose arrives last.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The bundled model uses XYZ internally and was trained for 10..40 m heights. The current demo deliberately commands 2 m and uses only predicted XY. Predicted Z never commands altitude. Correct software execution at 2 m does not establish planning quality outside that training range. In the supplied profile, injected noise and GP likelihood both use variance 0.01. The reference's height-dependent law cannot be extrapolated unchanged to 2 m because it would produce a negative variance.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#configure-an-experiment", "title": "Configure An Experiment", "text": "\u003cp>Two flat INI files have different responsibilities:\u003c/p> File Purpose Path Base \u003ccode>diplacam_settings.ini\u003c/code> Model assignment, inference limits, GP, synthetic sensor and comparison policy Paths inside this file are relative to the file Worker INI selected by \u003ccode>--config\u003c/code> Field, planning cadence and planning-pose age \u003ccode>field\u003c/code> is relative to the worker's working directory; an absolute path avoids ambiguity \u003cp>The module supplies a commented \u003ccode>diplacam_settings.ini\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>diplacam-example.ini\u003c/code>. Use one \u003ccode>key=value\u003c/code> per line, no sections, and put comments on separate lines. Unknown or duplicate keys, malformed numbers, nonfinite values and out-of-range settings stop startup instead of silently choosing another experiment.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Inspect the effective application settings without loading a graph, field or ONNX Runtime:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/diplacam --show-config\nbin/diplacam --settings /absolute/path/experiment.ini --show-config\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This reports resolved application paths and defaults, not worker configuration. Both files are read once at process startup. Restart the standalone program or USHER's Diplacam worker to apply edits; requesting another chunk does not reload settings. Standalone and worker planning use the same application snapshot.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#gp-sensor-and-flight-margin", "title": "GP, Sensor And Flight Margin", "text": "Setting Default Meaning And Accepted Values \u003ccode>diffusion_seed\u003c/code> \u003ccode>123\u003c/code> Independent sampler seed, unsigned 64-bit integer including zero \u003ccode>sensor_noise_seed\u003c/code> \u003ccode>149\u003c/code> Independent synthetic measurement-noise seed, same integer range \u003ccode>flight_margin_cells\u003c/code> \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> Integer \u003ccode>0..24\u003c/code>; inset is this value times 2 m on each XY edge \u003ccode>gp_prior_mean\u003c/code> \u003ccode>0.2\u003c/code> Finite initial field estimate, in field units \u003ccode>gp_kernel_variance\u003c/code> \u003ccode>0.0079\u003c/code> Positive finite Matern marginal variance, in squared field units \u003ccode>gp_lengthscale_m\u003c/code> \u003ccode>4.78\u003c/code> Positive finite spatial correlation length, in metres \u003ccode>sensor_fov_deg\u003c/code> \u003ccode>60\u003c/code> Finite full footprint angle, strictly between 0 and 180 degrees \u003ccode>sensor_noise_variance\u003c/code> \u003ccode>0.01\u003c/code> Positive finite measurement variance, used for both injected noise and GP likelihood \u003cp>For example, \u003ccode>flight_margin_cells=5\u003c/code> constrains published XY to 10..90 m and also constrains comparison controls. It does not change raw DDIM output, the observation domain or the fixed 2 m mission height. The GP prior mean is independent of \u003ccode>cmaes_utility_threshold\u003c/code>; changing one does not change the other.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Identical seeds reproduce each stream for the same inputs and call sequence; they do not make differently ordered observations equivalent. A standalone run starts fresh streams. The worker retains both streams and its belief across active START chunk requests. STOP followed by START creates a new survey and resets the sensor stream; the diffusion stream continues until process restart. Rejected duplicate observations consume no sensor noise.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Changing the GP or sensor profile changes the belief presented to the trained model. The graph and its normalization are not retrained by an INI edit. Keep the supplied profile as the reproducible baseline when evaluating changes.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#cached-conditioning", "title": "Cached Conditioning", "text": "\u003cp>An optional matched model bundle computes the map and position conditioning once per route instead of repeating it in all twenty denoising calls. The number of diffusion steps, GP observations, noise and flight-height policy are unchanged. Select it in model settings with:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>conditioning_cache=conditioning-cache\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The path is relative to the settings file. The bundle must match the original model and contain its verified encoder, denoiser and tensor descriptors. An invalid selected bundle is an error, not a silent fallback. Omitting the setting retains the original sampler; \u003ccode>--conditioning-cache DIR\u003c/code> selects a bundle for one invocation, while an explicitly empty CLI value disables it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Releases packaged with this option include both \u003ccode>diplacam_cached_settings.ini\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>diplacam_reference_settings.ini\u003c/code> under \u003ccode>share/diplacam/\u003c/code>. Compare either from the installation root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/diplacam --field share/diplacam/field.csv \\\n   --settings share/diplacam/diplacam_reference_settings.ini\nbin/diplacam --field share/diplacam/field.csv \\\n   --settings share/diplacam/diplacam_cached_settings.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For the managed service, copy the chosen settings over \u003ccode>share/diplacam/diplacam_settings.ini\u003c/code> and restart \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> to apply it. Keep the named copies for switching back without rebuilding. A measured Pi Zero 2 W comparison reduced warm inference from about 1.68 seconds to 0.56 seconds. Across sixteen complete test routes, the largest difference from the original sampler was 0.44 mm; the two paths are numerically close, not bit-for-bit identical.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#worker-field-and-timing", "title": "Worker Field And Timing", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>field=/absolute/path/map_26_NAIP_grid_counts.csv\ncadence_ms=1000\nplanning_pose_max_age_ms=1000\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Only \u003ccode>field\u003c/code> is required. Geographic placement is captured from the first valid admitted shot of each survey, not read from this file. The small-field conversion accepts an anchor within absolute 80 degrees latitude and 179.99 degrees longitude; polar and date-line-crossing surveys are not supported.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003ccode>cadence_ms\u003c/code> defaults to 1000, accepts \u003ccode>100..5000\u003c/code>, and sets the minimum cooldown after a planning attempt finishes, not a fixed-rate timer, measurement cadence or route-completion rule. Another requested chunk and fresh input are required. \u003ccode>planning_pose_max_age_ms\u003c/code> defaults to 1000 and accepts \u003ccode>1..5000\u003c/code>: an admitted pose must still be this fresh when a new plan starts. An older accepted measurement can update the belief without being eligible to start a plan. Continue providing fresh observations while waiting for a route.\u003c/p> \u003cp>This plan-start age check is separate from the fixed 5000 ms observation/reply lease and the transport's point-cycle timeout. Those remain transport rules, not experiment tuning controls. The fixed model contract also remains 51-by-51 cells over 0..100 m, eight XYZ points, three belief channels and twenty denoising steps. The model's 10..40 m Z normalization does not change the demo's 2 m command height in the original plan. A smaller physical survey uses the explicit frame settings below; the original defaults remain unchanged.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#cyberzoo-and-optitrack", "title": "Cyberzoo And OptiTrack", "text": "\u003cp>Select \u003ccode>sensoraia_dipla_cam_cyberzoo_demo.xml\u003c/code> instead of the original \u003ccode>sensoraia_dipla_cam_demo.xml\u003c/code>. It retains the same START, load-eight, follow-eight, STOP, return and landing sequence, with the supplied Cyberzoo corners and a centre at local east/north \u003ccode>(0,0)\u003c/code>. It commands 1 m height, limits survey speed to 0.35 m/s, and requires 20 cm horizontal arrival at each P1..P8. These explicit arrival checks do not inherit an airframe's larger outdoor waypoint radius. Survey entry requires a 15 cm centre check and height between 0.9 and 1.1 m before the first anchor is captured.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use the supplied worker profile \u003ccode>modules/dipla_cam/diplacam-cyberzoo.ini\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>field=modules/dipla_cam/data/map_26_NAIP_grid_counts.csv\ncadence_ms=100\nplanning_pose_max_age_ms=3000\nfield_size_m=4\nfield_rotation_deg=125\nflight_height_m=1\nflight_height_tolerance_m=0.15\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>field_size_m\u003c/code> is the physical side of the square, from 1 to 100 m; its default is 100. \u003ccode>field_rotation_deg\u003c/code> rotates the model's X axis counterclockwise from east, from -180 to 180 degrees; its default is zero. The 125-degree orientation follows the supplied room edges. The complete 4 m square has at least 0.58 m nominal clearance inside the supplied inner polygon before anchor or tracking error. The model's existing two-cell margin adds a further 0.16 m inset along each square edge.\u003c/p> \u003cp>All three measured coordinates use the same scale: physical metres are multiplied by \u003ccode>100 / field_size_m\u003c/code> before planning. Thus 4 m maps to the unchanged 100-unit field, and a real 1 m AGL maps to 25 model units. Position, sensor footprint, CSV field and GP length scales remain geometrically consistent; stretching X and Y independently or rescaling only output points would not preserve them. Predicted model Z is still discarded. Returned XY points are rotated and scaled back into physical metres, while the aircraft continues to command 1 m. Field-value noise variance is not a distance and is not rescaled. The graph, normalization constants and twenty DDIM steps are unchanged; no retraining is implied.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003ccode>flight_height_m\u003c/code> defaults to 2 m and accepts 0.2..40 m. \u003ccode>flight_height_tolerance_m\u003c/code> defaults to 0.75 m and must be positive and less than the selected height. A resized survey is rejected unless its entire accepted height interval maps inside the model's 10..40 altitude domain. The profile above maps 0.85..1.15 physical metres to 21.25..28.75 model units. Its 3000 ms planning-pose admission setting accommodates the initial MORA GP update while the aircraft holds CENTRE. The existing point freshness and flight-plan loading limits remain unchanged. Reading retained route points is independent of processing a pending sensor observation, so expensive GP work does not serialize delivery of P1..P8. Point-only reads do not create additional sensor observations; every admitted observation is processed once. Resized profiles use the diffusion planner; the optional fixed-height CMA-ES comparison rejects them explicitly. The original outdoor profile retains its original fixed 2 m synthetic footprint, while resized profiles use the measured, uniformly scaled AGL for both their sensor footprint and neural conditioning.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a PC-only native session, build \u003ccode>make usher diplacam\u003c/code> from the Sensoraia root. The launcher selects \u003ccode>diplacam-cyberzoo.ini\u003c/code> automatically when the saved aircraft configuration selects the Cyberzoo plan. The original plan selects \u003ccode>diplacam-nps.ini\u003c/code>. No worker-profile environment setting is needed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Start Center from the Paparazzi directory. The explicit empty UART override below keeps this test entirely on the PC even with a MORA cable connected. Clear an old experiment override so automatic profile selection can work:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>unset DIPLACAM_CONFIG\ncd \"$HOME/paparazzi\"\nMORA_UART_DEVICE=\"\" ./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#manual-cyberzoo-nps-flight", "title": "Manual Cyberzoo NPS Flight", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Stop the old session programs. In Center select Bebop_DIPLACAM    (\u003ccode>airframes/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_parrot_bebop.xml\u003c/code>) and choose    \u003ccode>flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_dipla_cam_cyberzoo_demo.xml\u003c/code> as the flight    plan. Save the configuration and confirm the Flight Plan field contains    cyberzoo before building. If the XML was edited outside Center, reload    the configuration first so a cached selection cannot overwrite that edit.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Select target nps, build it, then use session Sensoraia Diplacam NPS    in \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code>. Do not select    either MORA session for this PC-only test. If Center was open while its panel    changed, switch to another control panel and back to Sensoraia to reload    the program arguments.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Start the session's five programs: Sensoraia USHER,    Sensoraia NPS, Data Link, Server, and GCS Classic.    The USHER console must print \u003ccode>worker profile:\u003c/code> followed by    \u003ccode>diplacam-cyberzoo.ini\u003c/code>. The simulator console must report PC execution.    An already running USHER does not reload its profile when the flight plan    changes; restart that program as well as NPS.\u003c/li> \u003cli>In the GCS select mode Nav. Wait for a valid position and    Ground standby after Wait GPS and Geo init. NPS supplies this    position; physical OptiTrack hardware is not needed for this simulation.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Press Takeoff. This starts the motors and climbs while holding the    takeoff position. Wait for Standby at approximately 1 m. Do not    manually start Start Dipla, Load eight, or Follow.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Press Dipla once. The aircraft moves to CENTRE, settles within    15 cm at 0.9..1.1 m, then passes through Start Dipla and    Load eight. Once all eight points are accepted it enters Follow eight    automatically. These short transition blocks can be missed in telemetry.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Check that P1..P8 lie in the small rotated survey square and that the    aircraft follows them at 1 m. A route is retained while its eight points    are followed; a new batch is requested after that route completes. USHER    prints one \u003ccode>anchor_lat_e7\u003c/code> for the survey and successive \u003ccode>diplacam chunk=\u003c/code>    records. No JPEG files or yellow \u003ccode>DC_SHOT\u003c/code> markers are expected: this is a    file-backed sensor experiment, and its shot-report mask is 239.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Press Return, wait until the aircraft is back at HOME, then press    Land Here. Completion is Ground standby with the motors off, not    merely entry into a landing block.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>If the aircraft takes off to 2 m, the outdoor plan was built. If the USHER console names diplacam-nps.ini while the aircraft holds 1 m, the peer and flight plan do not match. Stop both, save the Cyberzoo selection, clear any \u003ccode>DIPLACAM_CONFIG\u003c/code> override, and restart the session. If Load eight returns to Standby, inspect the USHER console for worker/configuration errors; pressing Follow does not bypass the missing-route checks.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On MORA, select the indoor worker profile through USHER's \u003ccode>--diplacam-config\u003c/code> argument instead of the outdoor profile. Automatic PC-launcher selection does not reconfigure an already running service on MORA. The normal deployment can package this profile directly:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy MORA_PROFILE=diplacam \\\n   DIPLA_WORKER_CONFIG=\"$PWD/modules/dipla_cam/diplacam-cyberzoo.ini\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The package keeps the selected geometry and timing settings, replaces \u003ccode>field\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>share/diplacam/field.csv\u003c/code>, and includes the selected field data. Omit \u003ccode>DIPLA_WORKER_CONFIG\u003c/code> to package the original outdoor profile. Model and conditioning-cache selection remain separate application settings.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For physical Cyberzoo use, the aircraft's OptiTrack input must feed its normal position/INS and GPS-validity interface with the same geographic origin and east/north orientation as the supplied plan. Paparazzi's \u003ccode>gps_datalink\u003c/code> publishes valid external tracking as a 3D fix, so the existing \u003ccode>GpsFixValid()\u003c/code> gates remain meaningful without an indoor GNSS receiver. The supplied Bebop airframe still selects Furuno GPS; selecting the plan does not reconfigure that aircraft input. Clocking, tracking alignment and room coordinates must be checked on the actual installation. The existing geographic UART points have 1e-7-degree quantization, about 11 mm north and 7 mm east here.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Local NPS exercises the native USHER/Diplacam path, room geometry, point sequencing, return and landing. It does not measure OptiTrack or physical flight accuracy. The supplied field remains a scaled file-backed experiment, not an added physical sensor driver.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#run-the-pc-demo", "title": "Run The PC Demo", "text": "\u003cp>With Paparazzi installed at \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>, build native USHER and Diplacam and link the demo configurations from the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make usher diplacam\n./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> installs \u003ccode>sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code>, the unified control panel \u003ccode>sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code>, and symlinks for the Sensoraia flight plans and \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code> rotorcraft airframe into \u003ccode>~/paparazzi/conf/\u003c/code> (preserving previous \u003ccode>conf.xml\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>control_panel.xml\u003c/code> links under \u003ccode>~/.local/state/sensoraia/kickstart/\u003c/code>). No Python simulation adapter is used.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Start Paparazzi Center (\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\u003c/code>), select    Bebop_DIPLACAM and target nps, then click Build.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Select control panel \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code>    and session Sensoraia Diplacam NPS, then click Execute.\u003c/li> \u003cli>In GCS wait for valid telemetry and Ground standby. Select NAV mode    before pressing Takeoff.\u003c/li> \u003cli>When the aircraft settles at 2 m in Standby, press Dipla    (the Begin survey block). It approaches CENTRE, dwells there,    seeds the initial P8 hold at the current position, and enters Start Dipla.\u003c/li> \u003cli>The first admitted shot anchors the field. The mission fills P1..P8 from    native cam-point replies and enters    Follow eight automatically. P1..P8 stay fixed while that route is flown.    On completing the path, the mission requests and loads the next route.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Press Return, wait until the aircraft is back at HOME, then press    Land Here. Wait for Ground standby and motors off before stopping    the session programs.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>With no cable, the session starts exactly one local USHER, which starts its native Diplacam worker on demand. With a cable, the same session skips the PC peer and uses MORA. The NPS console prints execution: PC or execution: MORA, followed by the selected UART device. The launcher refreshes the selected NPS transport build before starting, including when the cable choice changed since Center's Build action. There is no separate waypoint adapter. The simulator runs at real time because freshness checks use elapsed wall-clock time. The flight plan holds at the configured HOLD point if initial loading times out or updates stop.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#deploy-diplacam-to-mora", "title": "Deploy Diplacam To MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Prepare the board once using Install and get started. The ready-made release targets 64-bit Trixie on the Pi Zero 2 W reference board. The examples below use \u003ccode>air@theatre.local\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>From the Sensoraia root on your PC:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 mora-deploy MORA_PROFILE=diplacam MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This builds the ARM64 applications on the PC, packages Diplacam with its matching ONNX Runtime, model, settings and field CSV, uploads the release, and starts USHER on MORA. Repeat the same command for an update. No compiler or Python planner is installed on the board. To prepare the release without contacting MORA, use \u003ccode>make -j32 mora-package MORA_PROFILE=diplacam\u003c/code> instead.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Every Diplacam package includes the complete \u003ccode>modules/dipla_cam/models/\u003c/code> directory, including additional models, metadata and data files, under \u003ccode>share/diplacam/models/\u003c/code> on MORA. Redeployment refreshes these files even when they are not the selected model. Copying another model does not select it or establish that it is compatible with the current planner; the application settings still choose the active graph, descriptor and metadata. The default full-suite \u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code> includes Diplacam and these assets automatically while starting Spottercam by default; \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=diplacam\u003c/code> starts USHER with the packaged Diplacam worker configuration. The dedicated \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=lwircam\u003c/code> remains an LWIR-only package and service.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The diplacam profile packages USHER, Diplacam and their runtime assets, without the unrelated camera applications or their OS dependencies. It does not start LWIRcam or EARcam. USHER uses the board UART, normally \u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code>, and starts its native Diplacam worker when the mission requests it. This remains the supplied CSV-field experiment, not a physical spectrum-camera driver. Deploying does not arm an aircraft or start an NPS session.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The installed worker configuration is \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia/share/diplacam/example.ini\u003c/code>; it selects the packaged \u003ccode>share/diplacam/field.csv\u003c/code>. Model and experiment settings are beside it in \u003ccode>share/diplacam/diplacam_settings.ini\u003c/code>. The service working directory is the installation root. The PC demo instead uses \u003ccode>modules/dipla_cam/diplacam-nps.ini\u003c/code>; its paths point into the source checkout.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Confirm the selected service:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service'\nssh air@theatre.local 'journalctl -u usher.service -n 50 --no-pager'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Expect an active USHER service whose command includes \u003ccode>--diplacam-config\u003c/code>. If activation needs administrative authentication, follow Deployment recovery using the staging path printed by that upload. A running service confirms startup; returned planner points are checked in the next exercise.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#simulate-with-diplacam-on-mora", "title": "Simulate With Diplacam On MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Keep the aircraft simulated on the PC while the real board runs USHER and Diplacam. Connect the laptop FTDI adapter to MORA using the desk-test wiring: 5 V power, common ground and crossed 3.3 V UART. Leave a real flight controller disconnected for this exercise. Wi-Fi/SSH carries deployment and logs; the FTDI UART carries flight requests and returned points at 115200 baud.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Deploy the Diplacam profile above and stop the previous Sensoraia session.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Connect the MORA FTDI cable.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Ensure \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> has been run once from the Sensoraia root, then start    Paparazzi Center from \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\ncd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Select Bebop_DIPLACAM, target nps, \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code>, and the ordinary Sensoraia Diplacam NPS session. The cable is selected automatically; the PC peer entry prints that it is using MORA instead of starting local USHER. Sensoraia MORA desk NPS is also available when you want startup to require a cable rather than permit PC-only operation. With an already open Center, stop the old session and reload the control panel after running \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Detection uses a single FTDI by-id device, otherwise \u003ccode>/dev/ttyUSB0\u003c/code>. If you have several FTDI adapters, choose MORA's actual path explicitly when starting Center:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; MORA_UART_DEVICE=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_YOUR_ADAPTER ./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Both NPS serial endpoints are pinned to that device in a hardware session, so a local peer cannot take over later. A leftover \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> makes startup stop with an explanation instead of silently testing the PC. End the earlier local session before retrying. No serial monitor should own the same FTDI port.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use the same NAV, Takeoff, Dipla, Return and Land Here sequence as Run the PC demo. Run NPS at real time. Watch the board in another PC terminal:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh -t air@theatre.local 'journalctl -fu usher.service'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>After Dipla reaches Start Dipla, inspect the native processes:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'ps -o pid,ppid,pcpu,rss,etime,args -C usher -C diplacam'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>During the survey, \u003ccode>diplacam\u003c/code> is a child of the board's \u003ccode>usher\u003c/code>; its PPID matches USHER's PID. The journal reports \u003ccode>anchor_lat_e7\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>initial_gp_update_ms\u003c/code>, and successive \u003ccode>chunk=... inference_ms=...\u003c/code> lines. Before Dipla, and after a STOP/Return ends the survey, only USHER may be present. Its \u003ccode>--diplacam-config\u003c/code> argument enables the backend; it does not keep a planner busy continuously. Low idle CPU is normal, and CPU alone cannot identify where a route was computed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Check all of these before calling the exercise successful:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>The MORA journal shows Diplacam starting, the first-position anchor, and    planner results while the simulated aircraft requests measurements.\u003c/li> \u003cli>GCS receives all eight P1\u2013P8 updates and the mission enters Follow eight.\u003c/li> \u003cli>The simulated aircraft follows the retained route at 2 m, then requests    another chunk without moving the geographic anchor.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Return reaches HOME and Land Here ends in Ground standby with    motors off.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Diplacam does not produce JPEGs in this experiment, and its normal mission suppresses Diplacam-only shot markers. Inspect returned points and the board journal, not photo counts. UART errors, worker failures or an expired loading deadline are failed results, even if USHER remains active. PC simulation results alone do not establish the board's planning latency or memory headroom.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Stop the Center session after landing; the managed USHER service stays on MORA. To return to PC-only simulation, disconnect the cable and restart the session. To deliberately keep the cable attached while using the PC, start Center with \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE=\"\" ./paparazzi\u003c/code>; the explicitly empty value disables cable selection for that session.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#if-the-demo-stops", "title": "If The Demo Stops", "text": "Symptom Action \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> reports missing Paparazzi Install Paparazzi (or set \u003ccode>PAPARAZZI_HOME\u003c/code>), then repeat \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> Native binaries not built yet Run \u003ccode>make usher diplacam\u003c/code> from the Sensoraia root Center shows an old aircraft or session Select \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> NPS reports no compiled aircraft Select Bebop_DIPLACAM, target nps, and build before starting the session No local UART connection Check the printed execution location first; a cable-selected session uses board USHER, not a local peer Entry remains at CENTRE Check GPS, height and arrival/dwell conditions; the mission has not started measurements yet Loading returns to Standby Inspect the USHER console for model, field or pose errors; press Dipla again only after resolving the error Updates stop while following The mission returns to HOLD/Standby after its update deadline; use Return and Land Here Worker reports a lost survey A STOP must end the lost survey before starting again; the supplied Standby and Begin survey blocks issue it Board service starts LWIRcam/EARcam instead of Diplacam Repeat deployment with \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=diplacam\u003c/code> MORA journal stays quiet during the desk flight Confirm execution: MORA, press Dipla after takeoff, then check the selected cable, crossed UART wiring and permissions Multiple FTDI cables are detected Set \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE\u003c/code> to MORA's stable by-id path before kickoff Startup reports an existing \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> Stop the earlier local USHER session; do not start a second peer or silently switch computers Board receives requests but no route loads Read the worker error in the MORA journal; check model/field paths, memory and planning time rather than changing the flight height Deployment reports another listener on \u003ccode>127.0.0.1:32000\u003c/code> Find its owner with \u003ccode>ss -lunp '( sport = :32000 )'\u003c/code> on MORA; stop the older camera service before repeating deployment Journal reports permission errors under \u003ccode>/root\u003c/code> Redeploy the current service template; USHER must run with \u003ccode>air\u003c/code>'s home directory, not a \u003ccode>HOME=%h\u003c/code> override NPS exits with \u003ccode>Floating point exception\u003c/code> Inspect the simulator backtrace before retrying; a speed-norm/integer-square-root crash is on the FC side, not a MORA inference timeout \u003cp>The USHER console contains native worker diagnostics and shot results. The worker reports the captured anchor latitude/longitude in integer 1e-7-degree units and the shot number that established it. Kickoff keeps the build log and standalone preflight output under \u003ccode>~/.local/state/sensoraia/kickoff/\u003c/code> (or the corresponding \u003ccode>XDG_STATE_HOME\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#understand-a-refresh", "title": "Understand A Refresh", "text": "\u003cp>The flight plan owns route completion, including Paparazzi's configured arrival margin and segment-passing rules. Diplacam does not run a second arrival check. Its eight XY targets stay fixed until the path completes. The mission then requests the next chunk using the existing START command, holds at P8 while loading, and enters Follow only after all eight slots have arrived. Active START retains both the geographic anchor and accumulated belief; STOP ends that survey. The repeating chunk loop does not revisit CENTRE.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Initial loading uses P8 seeded at the entry position. Later loading holds at the previous route's P8. Worker loss cannot silently relocate the survey: USHER rejects further chunk starts until STOP explicitly ends the lost survey.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USHER retrieves pinned slots and the FC applies them through its existing camera-point API, preserving the configured 2 m height. The eight moves are not atomic, so the mission loads them before following the new chunk. HOME and landing points never move. The GCS displays points; it does not plan the route.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The worker has one GP owner and room for 64 pending observations. If processing cannot keep up and that queue fills, it invalidates the route and reports the failure instead of silently losing measurements. The mission's existing timeout then returns it to Standby. Resolve the cause and restart the session programs before trying again. A retained route also requires continuing fresh observation replies; a long execution is not permission to replay stale replies.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Entering Standby, Return or landing clears loaded-route readiness. Press Dipla to load a fresh route before following again; manually selecting Follow eight does not reuse a stopped route.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#control-gcs-shot-markers", "title": "Control GCS Shot Markers", "text": "\u003cp>The supplied mission suppresses \u003ccode>DC_SHOT\u003c/code> telemetry for Diplacam-only triggers, so repeated measurements do not add yellow shot circles to the GCS. P1..P8 updates, capture requests, shot numbering and returned results continue normally. This does not clear circles already drawn; use Clear DCSHOTS for those.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The UART module's shot report mask setting is independent of its camera mask, which selects what to trigger:\u003c/p> Report Mask Behavior \u003ccode>255\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>0xff\u003c/code>) Report triggers from every camera slot \u003ccode>239\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>0xef\u003c/code>) Silence Diplacam-only triggers; keep other cameras' reports \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> Silence all shot reports without disabling captures \u003cp>A mixed trigger still produces one report if any requested camera's bit is enabled. A silent trigger still advances its shot number once, after UART admission. Rejected requests advance neither numbering nor reports. Silent shots are absent from \u003ccode>DC_SHOT\u003c/code> telemetry logs, not from the camera protocol.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For an aircraft startup default, add this define to its existing UART module:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;module name=\"digital_cam_uart\"&gt;\n   &lt;define name=\"DIGITAL_CAM_UART_SHOT_REPORT_MASK\" value=\"239\"/&gt;\n&lt;/module&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Omitting the define preserves normal reporting (\u003ccode>255\u003c/code>). A flight plan can set the policy before starting autoshoot:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;call_once fun=\"uart_cam_ctrl_set_shot_report_mask(239)\"/&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The supplied Start Dipla block applies \u003ccode>239\u003c/code> each time it starts. To keep reports enabled in this mission, change that call to \u003ccode>255\u003c/code>. During a session, change control / tb / shot report mask in the GCS settings to toggle reports without rebuilding or restarting USHER. Include \u003ccode>modules/digital_cam_uart.xml\u003c/code> in the aircraft's \u003ccode>settings_modules\u003c/code> list to expose that setting; the supplied configuration already does so. Only integer masks \u003ccode>0..255\u003c/code> are accepted.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Runtime changes override the startup default and survive a USHER reconnect; entering Start Dipla reapplies the mission's value. No GCS or USHER update is needed for this policy, but the FC must be rebuilt with the matching UART module and generated settings.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#compare-with-cma-es", "title": "Compare With CMA-ES", "text": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>cmaes=true\u003c/code> in the settings explicitly selects the classical comparison planner instead of ONNX diffusion. It is not a fallback after model failure. Use the same field/observations when comparing results; one attractive route does not establish a model's performance.\u003c/p> Setting Default Meaning And Accepted Values \u003ccode>cmaes_budget_ms\u003c/code> \u003ccode>1000\u003c/code> Cooperative whole-search budget, integer \u003ccode>1..4000\u003c/code> ms \u003ccode>cmaes_maxfevals\u003c/code> \u003ccode>300\u003c/code> Objective evaluation ceiling, integer \u003ccode>12..3000\u003c/code>; population size remains 12 \u003ccode>cmaes_seed\u003c/code> \u003ccode>55\u003c/code> Positive optimizer seed, integer \u003ccode>1..2147483647\u003c/code> \u003ccode>cmaes_utility_threshold\u003c/code> \u003ccode>0.3\u003c/code> Finite importance threshold in field units \u003ccode>cmaes_confidence_beta\u003c/code> \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> Finite nonnegative multiplier of posterior standard deviation \u003ccode>cmaes_step_xy_m\u003c/code> \u003ccode>20\u003c/code> Positive finite initial XY search standard deviation, in metres \u003cp>These settings are validated at startup even when \u003ccode>cmaes=false\u003c/code>, but affect only the selected comparison planner. It uses the same GP/sensor profile and flight margin as diffusion. Cancelled or incomplete searches publish no partial route. A tight wall-clock budget can interrupt a search at different points under different load; a fixed seed does not make timed-out runs identical.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#onnx-runtime", "title": "ONNX Runtime", "text": "\u003cp>Diplacam uses the shared native runtime, with one default inference thread and a budget for the whole plan. See model selection for compatible assignments and packaging.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003ccode>onnx_threads\u003c/code> accepts \u003ccode>1..32\u003c/code>. The supplied INI sets \u003ccode>inference_timeout_ms=4000\u003c/code> for all twenty passes together; omitting that key uses the shared application default of 2000 ms. These are cooperative limits, not hard-real-time guarantees.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "diplacam.html#before-onboard-use", "title": "Before Onboard Use", "text": "\u003cp>The simulation exercises native USHER/Diplacam IPC and the shared FC point transport. It does not establish real-sensor performance, MORA resource headroom or field accuracy. The configured NPS height is supplied from the simulated FC state; a physical installation needs its own valid height source.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Check real sensor integration, model usefulness at the intended height, terrain/clearance, aircraft response and combined board resource use before relying on an onboard mission. The dense field belief alone uses substantial memory; one process's average size is not whole-board headroom.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html", "title": "EARcam: Search By Sound", "text": "\u003cp>EARcam listens for an alarm through a USB microphone and combines sound measurements with aircraft positions to estimate a useful search location. Start by listening on the bench, then try the supplied simulated flight.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sound-search terms | Simulated flights\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#listen-on-the-bench", "title": "Listen On The Bench", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 earcam\narecord -L\nbin/earcam --device hw:CARD=Device,DEV=0\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use the actual ALSA device listed on your computer, not necessarily the example name. Stop with Ctrl+C. Place the intended alarm in a controlled test area and confirm its tone is detected before adding aircraft noise.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Keep microphone gain and mounting fixed during a comparison. A dramatic volume knob is useful for music and much less useful for measuring whether you moved closer to a sound source.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#what-the-readings-mean", "title": "What The Readings Mean", "text": "\u003cp>EARcam looks for useful tone energy rather than treating every loud sound as the desired alarm. Its signal checks reduce the influence of unrelated noise, but wind, propulsion, reflections and alarm direction still matter.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Digital microphone levels are normally dBFS, not calibrated dB SPL. Relative changes can be useful with fixed gain; physical sound-pressure and rough distance estimates need the appropriate microphone calibration.\u003c/p> Observation Interpretation Stronger clean alarm tone Useful evidence, not a location by itself Loud broadband noise May be wind/propulsion rather than the target Clipped samples Reduce excessive gain and repeat the check No accepted tone Check device, frequency band, gain and the actual alarm"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#how-it-finds-the-source", "title": "How It Finds The Source", "text": "\u003cp>One microphone does not point at the alarm. Movement supplies different measurement positions; the software fits a sound model to useful readings and reduces the effect of outliers. It does not simply select the single loudest sample's coordinates.\u003c/p> \u003cp>At height, small horizontal movements near the source can change loudness very little. Several useful passes around an area provide better geometry than assuming the peak of one pass is an exact fix. Wind and reflections can also distort the apparent peak.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The result is a search aid. Independent measurements are needed before claiming position accuracy or making a rescue or flight-safety decision.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#try-a-simulated-search", "title": "Try A Simulated Search", "text": "\u003cp>Use the Paparazzi demo setup. The EARcam session runs USHER natively with a simulated alarm source, so no physical microphone or MORA board is needed:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 usher soda earcam\n./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Start Paparazzi Center (\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\u003c/code>), select \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code>, aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_EARCAM\u003c/code> (or your own fixed-wing aircraft assigned \u003ccode>flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_ear_cam_demo.xml\u003c/code>), and target nps, then click Build. In the Session tab select \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> and start Sensoraia EARcam NPS. Wait for live telemetry/GPS in GCS Classic, then click Takeoff; the plan climbs and enters its flower listening mission automatically.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Watch the search pattern, result stage and normal landing. An accepted answer can update \u003ccode>TARGET\u003c/code>; a timeout or unusable answer can finish without a fix. A completed simulated search does not establish microphone performance.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#use-with-usher", "title": "Use With USHER", "text": "\u003cp>Choose the microphone device and a suitable alarm band in the actual USHER configuration. A separately managed live listening session can use:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --earcam --earcam-band 2400,3200 --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The band is an example for the intended alarm, not a universal setting for every sound. Avoid another program owning the same input. Check the sensor alone before combining it with other workloads on MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Capture uses nonblocking PCM reads with waits of at most 100 ms. Two seconds without PCM progress, including persistent device suspension, fails the worker so USHER can supervise its restart. This is a device-progress check, not a sound-level gate: quiet audio still contains PCM samples.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USHER rejects timed measurements whose original audio-window end is more than 500 ms old; a late classifier completion cannot make old audio fresh again. Unknown chunk timing leaves the whole classifier window explicitly unaligned. These are software policies, not measured USB latency or synchronization accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#inspect-the-search-result", "title": "Inspect The Search Result", "text": "\u003cp>Keep the recorded observations, position context and generated sound picture. The JPEG is a computed heat map, not a photograph taken by a microphone. Inspect result validity before treating it as a current target.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#replay-and-view-a-heatmap-on-a-map", "title": "Replay And View A Heatmap On A Map", "text": "\u003cp>The replay and overlay applications run on the development PC. Replay rebuilds the EARcam heatmap from a saved session log; overlay adds satellite context and redraws the source marker and optional observation/flight tracks. This is a visual review tool, not another localization step, and it never changes the recorded measurements.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Build both native programs from the Sensoraia root, then replay the session log to create the decorated image, its \u003ccode>.geo\u003c/code> sidecar and an undecorated \u003ccode>_field.jpg\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 ear_heatmap_replay ear_heatmap_overlay\nbin/ear_heatmap_replay /path/to/ear-session.csv /path/to/ear-result.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Overlay that result, optionally adding the session samples, aircraft track, search center and a known simulation source:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/ear_heatmap_overlay /path/to/ear-result.jpg \\\n  --log /path/to/ear-session.csv \\\n  --track /path/to/aircraft-track.csv \\\n  --search 48.8105,7.8516,25 \\\n  --truth 48.8105,7.8516\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The default output is \u003ccode>ear-result_sat.jpg\u003c/code>. The application automatically uses the matching \u003ccode>_field.jpg\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>.geo\u003c/code> files when present; keep all three replay outputs together. \u003ccode>--search\u003c/code> takes latitude, longitude and optional radius in metres. Use \u003ccode>--truth\u003c/code> only when the known position is available, such as in a simulation; it is drawn separately from the estimated loudest spot.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Google imagery reuses Paparazzi GCS's existing \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code> cache by default (or \u003ccode>$XDG_DATA_HOME/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map\u003c/code> when \u003ccode>XDG_DATA_HOME\u003c/code> is set). \u003ccode>--source esri\u003c/code> selects Esri imagery, and \u003ccode>--cache DIR\u003c/code> chooses the cache root. Existing tiles are opened directly; there is no network-library dependency. On a cache miss, the program uses the system \u003ccode>curl\u003c/code> command to fetch that tile. Add \u003ccode>--offline\u003c/code> to require a complete local cache and fail instead of attempting a download. Tile imagery is visual context only: it cannot improve acoustic evidence or turn an uncertain estimate into ground truth.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use \u003ccode>bin/ear_heatmap_overlay --help\u003c/code> for zoom, opacity, margin, obstacle-grid, and other options. An obstacle CSV, when available, must contain \u003ccode>x_east_m,lat,lon,mnh_m\u003c/code> columns.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#optional-onnx-sound-classification", "title": "Optional ONNX Sound Classification", "text": "\u003cp>Select a compatible assignment in \u003ccode>earcam_settings.ini\u003c/code>. The model must match the declared PCM sample rate and window; its probability is separate from raw microphone level. A classifier does not automatically provide a geographic source. See model selection.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#why-height-and-more-than-one-pass-matter", "title": "Why Height And More Than One Pass Matter", "text": "\u003cp>In an ideal free field, doubling source distance reduces sound pressure by about 6 dB. On an aircraft, slant distance includes height: at 40 m above a source, moving 10 m sideways changes the distance only to about 41.2 m. That is roughly 0.26 dB, easily obscured by ordinary noise.\u003c/p> \u003cp>This broad, nearly flat loudness peak is why the loudest single reading is not an exact ground location. Passes from different directions help constrain the fit. Reflections, alarm orientation and propulsion can distort the pattern; the distance relationship explains the method, not a guaranteed useful range for every installation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#read-level-tone-and-quality-together", "title": "Read Level, Tone And Quality Together", "text": "\u003cp>Where the selected mode reports these diagnostics:\u003c/p> Reading What It Tells You Level Strength of the selected tone, not all nearby sound Frequency Which tone is strongest within the selected search band Contrast How distinct that tone is from the remaining in-band sound Envelope/trend Smoothed change across pulses: approaching, receding or steady Alarm/clip flags Whether tone checks passed or the recording range was exceeded \u003cp>Compare a clean alarm recording with quiet/background and propulsion/noise conditions. Strong RMS alone does not identify the alarm. Keep microphone, gain and mounting fixed when comparing positions or runs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "\u003cp>Check the correct audio device, gain, clipping and whether the alarm is actually within the selected band. Compare quiet and propulsion/noise conditions. If readings change after moving the microphone or changing gain, start a new controlled comparison rather than combining incompatible measurements into one search.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "earcam-loudest-spot-explained.html#glossary", "title": "Glossary", "text": "\u003cp>Sound and shared project definitions live in the Sensoraia glossary, including its dedicated EARcam section.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html", "title": "Read The Information In Your Photos", "text": "\u003cp>A photo is more useful when you know where it came from. Sensoraia keeps available aircraft, capture and analysis information with supported JPEGs using EXIF metadata. Keep the original images: editing or exporting them through another program may discard that information.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Metadata terms | Capture with USHER\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#inspect-a-saved-jpeg", "title": "Inspect A Saved JPEG", "text": "\u003cp>On the PC, ExifTool can display the supported records without changing them:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo apt install libimage-exiftool-perl\nexiftool -G1 -a -s /absolute/path/photo.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Start with the image dimensions, GPS fields, image description and user comment. Read the whole record rather than assuming a single GPS field says everything about a capture. A generic viewer may not display custom evidence.\u003c/p> Information What It Helps You Check Camera and photo identity Which observation a result belongs to Position, altitude and attitude Where the aircraft was reported and how it was oriented Request/observation timing Whether the pose describes a request or an estimated sensing interval Mock or lens-emulation records Whether the pixels are simulated or derived Detection or thermal records What optional processing returned \u003cp>Availability depends on the backend. Missing data must not be interpreted as zero, and a precise-looking coordinate is not a measured accuracy claim.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#request-pose-is-not-always-exposure-pose", "title": "Request Pose Is Not Always Exposure Pose", "text": "\u003cp>The aircraft can move between requesting a capture and the actual exposure. Where supported timing evidence is available, Sensoraia can estimate a pose for the observation interval. Other captures retain the request-time pose.\u003c/p> \u003cp>When comparing a detected object with a ground reference, check which timing information is actually present. At 10 m/s, a tenth of a second corresponds to about one metre of travel, before other errors are considered.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#embedded-capture-and-loom-evidence", "title": "Embedded Capture And LOOM Evidence", "text": "\u003cp>Native camera paths can retain capture identity and timing inside the JPEG. LOOMcam can also retain model-related features. These records help associate processing with the correct frame; they do not prove physical timing accuracy or make an unspecified feature vector a navigation direction.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Stock camera tools do not necessarily provide the same evidence. A usable photo can therefore have less qualified timing than another backend's photo. Do not copy evidence records from a different image to make it appear complete.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#original-review-copy-and-sidecar", "title": "Original, Review Copy And Sidecar", "text": "\u003cp>The original flight JPEG is the archive. SODA may create separate annotated copies or crops, and saved-image model analysis may write a neighbouring result file. Keep required sidecars with their source image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Thermal JPEGs may also contain a temperature plane. A resized screenshot or colourized export cannot replace those samples. Use the LWIRcam guide for supported readback.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#use-photos-for-mapping-or-evaluation", "title": "Use Photos For Mapping Or Evaluation", "text": "\u003cp>Use original images and the input contract required by PANGEA. An arbitrary GPS-tagged JPEG may lack the attitude, height or camera geometry needed by a mapping workflow.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For model evaluation, retain the image's origin, model identity and independent labels. Provider imagery terms still apply to MIMIC-derived material.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#if-a-field-looks-wrong", "title": "If A Field Looks Wrong", "text": "\u003cp>Check the sensor, coordinate units, altitude reference and timing first. Compare an original with any edited copy to see whether metadata was removed. If the backend never supplied a measurement, changing an EXIF field cannot recover it. Keep the original and investigate the capture source instead.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "exif.html#read-a-detailed-application-record", "title": "Read A Detailed Application Record", "text": "\u003cp>Inspect the text payload without the rest of ExifTool's listing:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>exiftool -b -UserComment /absolute/path/photo.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Look for sensor/result type, photo identity, available pose/time information and whether observations are simulated. Field availability varies by backend; an absent measurement is different from a valid zero.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If an object position looks wrong, first distinguish the aircraft's GPS tag from a projected target record. Then check timing, lens/mounting geometry and altitude reference. The coordinate alone does not explain those inputs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html", "title": "Install And Get Started", "text": "\u003cp>Welcome to Sensoraia. This guide takes you from a source checkout to a running camera service, first on your Linux PC and then on MORA. The Pi Zero 2 W is our initial reference board, not a requirement; you can stop after simulation or adapt the deployment to another compatible compute board.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The guide keeps PC preparation, local use and onboard deployment as separate steps, so you can take the route that matches the equipment you have today.\u003c/p> \u003cp>New to MORA, USHER or the abbreviations? Keep the Sensoraia glossary beside this guide. Its installation terms explain builds, packages, paths and deployment without assuming you already know the tools.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#before-you-start", "title": "Before You Start", "text": "For this task You need Build PC applications Linux, a C/C++ toolchain and the dependencies below Run a simulated flight A working Paparazzi NPS installation and the example aircraft configuration Deploy onboard A compatible Linux compute board; the current ready-made reference is Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W with 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie Capture from hardware Your camera or USB microphone, correct cables and its runtime packages \u003cp>The Pi is the deliberately modest starting point for resource planning, not the definition of MORA. Current ARM64 build and package instructions are for Raspberry Pi OS Trixie on that board. Other boards need matching binaries, runtime libraries, sensor drivers and interface configuration; verify their combined memory and processing capacity with the services you intend to run.\u003c/p> \u003cp>These examples use \u003ccode>~/develop/sensoraia\u003c/code>, SSH user \u003ccode>air\u003c/code>, host \u003ccode>theatre.local\u003c/code> and install directory \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia\u003c/code>. Change them to match your machine. The packaged service currently runs as \u003ccode>air\u003c/code>; if you choose another account, review the service template before packaging.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Connect camera ribbon cables only with power off. For UART, use 3.3 V logic, crossed TX/RX and a common ground.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#build-on-your-pc", "title": "Build On Your PC", "text": ""}, {"location": "getting-started.html#1-prepare-the-source-checkout", "title": "1. Prepare The Source Checkout", "text": "\u003cp>Clone the repository (\u003ccode>make\u003c/code> automatically syncs and checks out the pinned OpenCV \u003ccode>4.14.0\u003c/code> submodule into \u003ccode>ext/opencv\u003c/code> on first build, or pass \u003ccode>--recurse-submodules\u003c/code> to prefetch it during clone):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>git clone https://github.com/OpenUAS/sensoraia.git ~/develop/sensoraia\ncd ~/develop/sensoraia\nmake help\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>All product builds, self-contained Paparazzi demo airframes, flight plans, and simulation launchers are included directly in this repository. \u003ccode>make help\u003c/code> lists all supported build, camera-selection, packaging, and deployment commands.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#2-install-pc-dependencies", "title": "2. Install PC Dependencies", "text": "\u003cp>On a Debian/Ubuntu development PC, start with:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install build-essential cmake meson ninja-build pkg-config \\\n  gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu g++-aarch64-linux-gnu \\\n  git curl ca-certificates gnupg openssl patch python3 python3-venv python3-jinja2 python3-yaml \\\n  libasound2-dev \\\n  rsync openssh-client\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>These are development-PC packages, installed once. The cross-compilers let the same PC build the ARM64 applications as well as native applications. No preinstalled \u003ccode>libcamera-dev\u003c/code>, private camera checkout or manual copying of camera headers is required: the normal build compiles its pinned native camera library from source into the checkout's private build area.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The first build needs Internet access to fetch checked source archives, build-tool Python packages and the Raspberry Pi OS Trixie SDK packages. Those downloads and SDK extraction do not require root access. Later builds reuse the prepared dependencies unless you clean them.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#3-build-native-applications", "title": "3. Build Native Applications", "text": "\u003cp>For the complete suite, run this from the checkout root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make all\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This is also the command to use after \u003ccode>make clean\u003c/code>. It prepares missing dependencies first, then compiles every Sensoraia application for both PC and ARM64. Parallel jobs are selected automatically; you do not need to remember \u003ccode>-j\u003c/code> or individual module targets.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The native camera library, YAML support and OpenCV are built from pinned or bundled sources. OpenCV \u003ccode>4.14.0\u003c/code> lives as a pinned Git submodule at \u003ccode>ext/opencv\u003c/code> in the checkout and is initialized automatically on first \u003ccode>make\u003c/code>. Every application's JPEG codec is built directly from OpenCV's bundled IJG sources; no system JPEG development package or separate JPEG download is required. OpenCV image-codec modules are not added. The ARM64 sysroot is prepared from signed Trixie packages, including camera and ALSA development files. All of this happens on the PC; no running MORA or SSH connection is needed to build.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Success looks like: a final list of every executable followed by:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>Complete Sensoraia suite: 27 executables verified.\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The build exits with an error if a required application is missing. A few completed compiler commands are not the completion report for the whole suite.\u003c/p> Built Application PC Output ARM64 Output USHER \u003ccode>usher\u003c/code> \u003ccode>usher-arm64\u003c/code> SODA \u003ccode>bin/soda\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/soda-arm64\u003c/code> Camera lens tool \u003ccode>bin/camera_lens\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/camera_lens-arm64\u003c/code> PANGEA \u003ccode>bin/pangea\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/pangea-arm64\u003c/code> LWIRcam \u003ccode>bin/lwircam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/lwircam-arm64\u003c/code> LWIR calibration \u003ccode>bin/lwir_calibrate\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/lwir_calibrate-arm64\u003c/code> EARcam \u003ccode>bin/earcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/earcam-arm64\u003c/code> Acoustic heatmap replay \u003ccode>bin/ear_heatmap_replay\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/ear_heatmap_replay-arm64\u003c/code> LOOMcam \u003ccode>bin/loomcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/loomcam-arm64\u003c/code> AIcam native photo worker \u003ccode>bin/aicam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/aicam-arm64\u003c/code> Spottercam \u003ccode>bin/spottercam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/spottercam-arm64\u003c/code> CHDKbridge \u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/chdkbridge-arm64\u003c/code> Diplacam \u003ccode>bin/diplacam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>bin/diplacam-arm64\u003c/code> Acoustic heatmap overlay \u003ccode>bin/ear_heatmap_overlay\u003c/code> PC-only tool \u003cp>AIcam and CHDK camera backends are integrated into USHER. The native AIcam photo worker and CHDKbridge can also be used separately. Building an application does not enable its sensor, install a model or start a service. Choose its guide below for the appropriate hardware, configuration and example workflow.\u003c/p> \u003cp>You should now have: \u003ccode>usher\u003c/code> at the project root and camera applications in \u003ccode>bin/\u003c/code>. Check without opening any hardware:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --version\n./bin/lwircam --version\n./bin/pangea --version\nmake suite-check\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Run \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> again for normal updates; unchanged files and dependencies are reused. To cap parallel jobs, use \u003ccode>make -j4 all\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>make -j1 all\u003c/code> is serial. An intentional full cleanup is:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make clean\nmake all\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>make clean all\u003c/code> is also ordered safely. Cleaning removes generated binaries, SDKs and build caches, so the next build downloads and prepares them again. It does not remove your installed PC prerequisite packages or source checkout. Use \u003ccode>make -B all\u003c/code> when you want to force rebuilding without deleting SDKs.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A build error is not a request to run \u003ccode>make clean\u003c/code>. The useful part is usually the first compiler or dependency error; the last line just reports that Make is disappointed too. See If Something Stops.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#try-it-without-hardware", "title": "Try It Without Hardware", "text": ""}, {"location": "getting-started.html#fly-a-full-simulation-in-paparazzi-center", "title": "Fly A Full Simulation In Paparazzi Center", "text": "\u003cp>With Paparazzi already installed at \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>, run \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> once from the Sensoraia root to link the supplied flight plans, airframes, and unified control panel into \u003ccode>~/paparazzi/conf/\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make native\n./kickstart.sh\ncd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>In Paparazzi Center, select your camera aircraft (such as \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_AICAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LWIRCAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_CHDKCAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_EARCAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LOOMCAM\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Easystar_3_MIMIC\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code>), pick target nps, and click Build. Then open the Session tab, select the matching Sensoraia \\&lt;Camera&gt; NPS session, and click Execute. The session starts USHER for you\u2014do not start local USHER separately. Follow the step-by-step simulation walkthrough.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#start-standalone-local-usher", "title": "Start Standalone Local USHER", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bash examples/paparazzi/run_local_demo.sh --camera spottercam\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Leave the terminal open (or edit \u003ccode>CAMERA = ...\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code>). USHER creates \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> and prints capture diagnostics. Stop it with Ctrl+C when finished; never run it simultaneously with a Center session that already starts USHER.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#connect-a-simulated-aircraft", "title": "Connect A Simulated Aircraft", "text": "\u003cp>Follow Paparazzi integration to install the supplied plans and select a matching NPS session. That guide explains which sessions start USHER for you; use either the session-managed service or the terminal above, never both.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For simulated photographs of real terrain, use MIMIC. It reads your existing aerial tile cache, renders camera views and saves photographs. PANGEA can then map those saved photographs. Missing imagery is reported rather than invented.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Success looks like: a trigger in the simulator, a newly saved JPEG and a completion message in USHER. A GCS photo marker alone means a request was admitted, not necessarily that a file was saved.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#try-the-real-board-on-your-desk", "title": "Try The Real Board On Your Desk", "text": "\u003cp>Once MORA is prepared and deployed, follow the laptop-to-MORA FTDI desk test. Keep the aircraft simulated while using the real board and cable, first with mock capture and then a physical camera. The illustrated guide includes the dedicated Center session.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#prepare-the-reference-pi-board", "title": "Prepare The Reference Pi Board", "text": "\u003cp>The steps below set up the current Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W reference platform. For another compute board, use its supported Linux image and adapt the OS, service, UART and sensor-runtime steps to that board. The board-independent MORA role does not make Raspberry Pi drivers, GPIO numbers or packages portable automatically.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Install 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie and configure networking and SSH.    Follow the board setup guide.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Configure the camera UART on \u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code>; disable the serial login console.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Add the service account to the device groups required by your hardware,    including \u003ccode>dialout\u003c/code> for UART and the USB/audio groups used by your setup.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Install runtime packages for your sensor. For stock AIcam this includes    the OS camera tools; CHDKbridge needs no extra package, only USB device    access for the service account.    The sensor guide gives the relevant commands.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Confirm SSH and architecture from the PC:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'uname -m; ls -l /dev/serial0; df -h /home/air'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Expect \u003ccode>aarch64\u003c/code>, an accessible UART device and room for the release and photographs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#deploy-to-mora", "title": "Deploy To MORA", "text": ""}, {"location": "getting-started.html#1-cross-build-and-package-on-the-pc", "title": "1. Cross-Build And Package On The PC", "text": "\u003cp>After \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> has completed, package the ARM64 applications on the same PC:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-package MORA_INSTALL_DIR=/home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The cross-compilers were installed in the PC prerequisite step. The build's private ARM64 SDK already includes ALSA; you do not need to enable system ARM64 multiarch or install \u003ccode>libasound2-dev:arm64\u003c/code> for this normal workflow. Packaging refreshes ARM64 binaries as needed and prepares the install package. It does not contact the board or restart a service. Keep the same \u003ccode>MORA_INSTALL_DIR\u003c/code> for packaging and deployment.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#2-choose-your-camera-cameraconf-or-camera", "title": "2. Choose Your Camera (\u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> Or \u003ccode>CAMERA=...\u003c/code>)", "text": "\u003cp>Open \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> in the Sensoraia root and set \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> to the sensor you want MORA's \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> to run (\u003ccode>spottercam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>aicam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lwircam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>chdk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>earcam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>diplacam\u003c/code>), or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> directly on the command line without editing any service template:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-package CAMERA=lwircam MORA_INSTALL_DIR=/home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The default \u003ccode>CAMERA = spottercam\u003c/code> starts Spottercam (\u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code>) on the IMX500 AI Camera with \u003ccode>nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code>. All other camera binaries remain installed on MORA so switching cameras later is just changing \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> and redeploying. For smaller single-purpose packages, \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=lwircam\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=diplacam\u003c/code> remain available. AIcam, Spottercam, and LOOMcam share the same physical IMX500 sensor; run only one of them at a time. See Choose a camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#3-upload-and-activate", "title": "3. Upload And Activate", "text": "\u003cp>Safe operation is the end user's responsibility.\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy \\\n  MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local \\\n  MORA_INSTALL_DIR=/home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Deployment stages the files, checks the package and runtime dependencies, backs up the installed release, and activates the service. The command reports the staging and backup paths.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If activation needs a sudo password, use the interactive procedure in Deployment recovery. Use the staging path printed by your own upload, not one from an old example.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#4-confirm-the-installed-release", "title": "4. Confirm The Installed Release", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service'\nssh air@theatre.local 'journalctl -u usher.service -n 50 --no-pager'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Expect the service to be active and the selected sensors to initialize. Make a bench capture, confirm that the JPEG is readable, and inspect its metadata before connecting flight requests. Service startup alone does not confirm capture quality, calibration or sustained operation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To watch subsequent activity:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh -t air@theatre.local 'journalctl -fu usher.service'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Ctrl+C stops log following, not the service.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#choose-your-next-guide", "title": "Choose Your Next Guide", "text": "What you are setting up Guide Raspberry Pi photographs AI Camera Canon USB camera CHDKbridge Thermal capture LWIRcam Real-camera capture throughput Camera Speed Test Thermal lens, mounting and temperature checks LWIR calibration Acoustic search EARcam Gas, particle and radiation sensing possibilities Chemical Vapors and Emissions Vehicle detection Spottercam Maps from saved photos PANGEA PC simulation or MORA in the loop Paparazzi integration"}, {"location": "getting-started.html#if-something-stops", "title": "If Something Stops", "text": "Symptom Try this first Missing or mismatched OpenCV source Run \u003ccode>make opencv-submodule\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code>); \u003ccode>ext/opencv\u003c/code> must be unmodified OpenCV tag \u003ccode>4.14.0\u003c/code> A build tool or PC header is missing Rerun the PC prerequisite-install command above, then \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> Download or archive-signature check fails Check Internet access and the first download/signature error; rerun \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> after resolving it Native camera SDK requested after cleaning Run \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code>; it builds the private native camera dependency from pinned source Missing ARM64 ALSA with a custom SDK The default SDK includes ALSA; omit custom sysroot overrides for the normal \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> workflow, or supply the complete matching SDK Only some applications exist Check the first error and Make's exit status; rerun \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> and require the final complete-suite report Existing SDK or source directory is rejected Keep the reported path and error; do not disable checks or copy headers over it. Use an intentional \u003ccode>make clean\u003c/code> followed by \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> to recreate generated state SSH cannot find MORA Confirm its actual hostname/IP, Wi-Fi and SSH configuration \u003ccode>sudo: a password is required\u003c/code> Use the same upload's staging path and the interactive activation procedure UART is missing or busy Check \u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code>, serial-console configuration and whether another USHER owns it Service runs but no photograph appears Check the sensor flag, camera connection, output directory and first relevant journal error Second local instance refused Stop the existing local USHER; do not delete its endpoint while it is running Geographic result looks wrong Check calibration, mounting, time/pose evidence and ground reference; GPS coordinates alone do not validate the result \u003cp>For capture-specific symptoms and recovery, use the camera reference. Keep the error message, selected camera, application version and a short log when asking for help. Those four details save a surprising amount of guesswork.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html", "title": "Sensoraia Glossary", "text": "\u003cp>You do not need to learn all these words before using Sensoraia. Start with the three names below, then come back whenever a guide uses an unfamiliar term. Each definition explains what the word means here, and why it matters when choosing equipment, running a demo or interpreting a result.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The short version: Sensoraia is the project, MORA is the onboard computer, and USHER is the service that coordinates its sensors. The flight controller still flies the aircraft.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Names and responsibilities | Capture and results | Position and flight | Images and timing | AI and models | Installation | Abbreviations A-Z\u003c/p> \u003cp>Your module: AIcam | CHDKbridge | Diplacam | EARcam | LOOMcam | LWIRcam | PANGEA | Spottercam | MIMIC | Camera Lens\u003c/p> \u003cp>A typical journey: the flight controller requests a capture. USHER asks the selected sensor to do the work, then keeps the observation with its available pose and metadata. A module may also return a detection, position or plan. The flight plan decides whether and how to act on it.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#names-and-responsibilities", "title": "Names And Responsibilities", "text": "\u003cp>Sensoraia\u003c/p> \u003cp>The camera-compute project as a whole: sensor applications, their supporting software and the tools to try them on a development PC or use them onboard. It is not the name of a particular camera or computer.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MORA\u003c/p> \u003cp>The role of the aircraft's onboard companion computer, not a specific board. Sensoraia's initial resource-constrained reference is a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W; another suitable compute board can serve as MORA when its OS, architecture, drivers, interfaces and resources are supported or adapted. It runs sensor and processing software separately from the flight controller. MORA is hardware, not the name of a service to restart.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USHER\u003c/p> \u003cp>The service that coordinates capture and sensor work on MORA, exchanges requests and results with the flight controller, and records information alongside observations. Restarting USHER restarts this software, not the flight controller. You can also run USHER locally on the development PC.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Companion computer\u003c/p> \u003cp>A separate computer that helps the flight controller with jobs such as image processing and sound analysis. In this project, that computer is MORA. \"Companion\" describes its role, not an alternative autopilot.\u003c/p> \u003cp>FC: Flight Controller; Autopilot\u003c/p> \u003cp>The aircraft's flight-control computer. It estimates aircraft state and runs the control and navigation software. Sensoraia can supply observations or targets; the FC and its flight plan remain responsible for flying the aircraft.\u003c/p> \u003cp>GCS: Ground Control Station\u003c/p> \u003cp>The operator's ground-side application for monitoring the aircraft and sending supported commands. Paparazzi's GCS can display telemetry, photographs' event markers and waypoints. A point shown on its map is not automatically a command to fly there.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Module\u003c/p> \u003cp>A part of the system with a particular job, such as EARcam's sound search or LWIRcam's thermal capture. A Sensoraia module belongs to the sensing software; a Paparazzi FC module belongs to the aircraft software. They are not interchangeable installation targets.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Paparazzi\u003c/p> \u003cp>The open-source autopilot system used by the supplied flight examples. It includes aircraft software, simulation and ground tools. Sensoraia connects to it at the communication boundary; Sensoraia is a separate project.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sensor\u003c/p> \u003cp>Equipment that measures something about the world. Here that includes visible and thermal cameras and microphones. Other sensor types are possible extension ideas, not a promise that a driver for every device is already included.\u003c/p> \u003cp>SODA\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sensoraia's saved-image processing application. USHER can hand a completed image to SODA for its selected camera-specific processing, review products or optional lens emulation. SODA is not the camera driver, and it does not mean every image is automatically analysed by a neural network.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#capture-and-results", "title": "Capture And Results", "text": "\u003cp>API: Application Programming Interface\u003c/p> \u003cp>A defined way for software components to request work or exchange information. An API description explains how to interact with a component; it is not the same as the end-user command or the physical cable carrying a message.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Acknowledgement; ACK And NACK\u003c/p> \u003cp>A reply about a request. ACK is an acknowledgement; NACK is a negative acknowledgement. Always check what was acknowledged: accepting a request, saving a file and confirming a waypoint position are different events. An acknowledgement of admission is not proof of completed sensing.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Backend\u003c/p> \u003cp>The implementation selected to do a job. A camera backend handles capture from a particular source; an inference backend selects how a model runs. For example, choosing an ONNX inference backend does not itself choose which physical camera supplies the image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Camera ID And Camera Mask\u003c/p> \u003cp>A camera ID identifies one sensor slot. A mask selects one or more slots by setting bits in a number. The protocol has eight addressable slots, but not every slot has an implemented backend. For example, ID 5 is selected by mask \u003ccode>0x10\u003c/code> (decimal 16), not mask 5. Selecting several cameras does not prove their exposures happen at the same instant.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Capture; Shot; Trigger\u003c/p> \u003cp>A trigger requests an observation. A capture is the sensor work that obtains it. Shot is the camera-oriented name used by the shared interface, including for some non-image measurements. A request can be rejected or fail; counting triggers is therefore not the same as counting saved photographs.\u003c/p> \u003cp>DC_SHOT\u003c/p> \u003cp>A Paparazzi camera-event message used for shot numbering and GCS display. One admitted trigger has one GCS shot number even when it selects several cameras. It is not the UART protocol's message-type number, nor independent proof that every selected camera saved an image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Completion; Saved; Recorded\u003c/p> \u003cp>Completion means a particular stage finished. A saved result concerns an image output; recorded can acknowledge a non-image observation's admission. Neither word alone guarantees a valid geographic target or a finished map. Check the result of the stage you actually need.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Freshness; Stale Result\u003c/p> \u003cp>How recently the information underlying a result was obtained. A newly arrived reply can already be stale if it was computed from an old pose or frame. Freshness limits prevent old information being mistaken for a current target; they do not improve the accuracy of the original measurement.\u003c/p> \u003cp>IPC: Inter-Process Communication; Socket\u003c/p> \u003cp>IPC is how separate running programs exchange information. A socket is one communication endpoint. For example, Diplacam's local worker socket is between software processes on one computer; it is not the FC UART wire protocol or an instruction to change a flight-plan block.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Protocol; Message Type; Payload\u003c/p> \u003cp>A protocol defines how a conversation is encoded and interpreted. A message type identifies the kind of message; its payload carries the associated data. A message-type number is not a camera ID, shot number or waypoint ID, even if two of those numbers happen to be equal.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Session; Sortie\u003c/p> \u003cp>A sortie is one aircraft outing. A software session is a separately identified period of work, such as one search or planning run. A session token helps reject delayed replies from earlier work. A session need not cover an entire flight, and restarting one need not restart the aircraft.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Telemetry\u003c/p> \u003cp>Information reported by the aircraft or another running component, such as position, status or the waypoint coordinates the FC currently holds. It tells you what was reported; receiving telemetry is different from sending a command.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Timeout; Backpressure\u003c/p> \u003cp>A timeout ends a wait that has taken too long; backpressure means a component cannot currently accept more work. A busy camera can reject a request instead of adding it to an ever-growing queue. Retrying is a separate policy: a timeout does not prove a physical exposure never happened.\u003c/p> \u003cp>UART: Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter\u003c/p> \u003cp>The serial communication interface used between the FC and MORA. TX sends, RX receives and GND provides the common electrical reference. For the documented 3.3 V connection, TX connects to the other device's RX. A compatible connector shape does not establish compatible voltage levels.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Valid; Invalid; Unavailable\u003c/p> \u003cp>Valid means the stated checks for a particular result passed. Invalid means it cannot be used as that result; unavailable means the required information was not supplied. An unavailable temperature is not zero degrees, and a valid computation is not an accuracy certificate.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Worker; Asynchronous Processing\u003c/p> \u003cp>A component that carries out a job separately from the request-handling loop. Asynchronous means the requester need not wait in place until all work is done; the result arrives later. It does not mean the result is immediate, nor that unlimited jobs can be queued.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#position-and-flight", "title": "Position And Flight", "text": "\u003cp>AGL: Above Ground Level\u003c/p> \u003cp>Height above the terrain underneath the aircraft, not height above sea level. An aircraft 50 m above a hill is at 50 m AGL even when the hill itself is high above the sea. AGL matters when estimating a camera footprint or a sound source's distance.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Attitude: Roll, Pitch And Yaw\u003c/p> \u003cp>The aircraft's orientation. Roll is banking, pitch is nose-up or nose-down tilt, and yaw is rotation around the vertical axis. Camera projection needs orientation as well as position: a banked camera generally sees different ground from a level camera at the same coordinates.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Coordinates; Local Origin\u003c/p> \u003cp>Numbers that locate a point within a declared reference system. Latitude and longitude use angles; a local east/north map uses distances from a chosen origin. An east offset of 20 m has no geographic meaning until you know that origin. Keep both the reference system and the units with the numbers.\u003c/p> \u003cp>ENU And NED\u003c/p> \u003cp>Two ways to name local coordinate directions: East, North, Up and North, East, Down. Their component order and vertical sign differ. In NED, a positive down value points downward, not upward. Neither is the aircraft's forward/right body frame.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Fillet turn\u003c/p> \u003cp>A rounded corner joining two straight flight legs. Rather than stopping or turning sharply at their meeting point, the aircraft follows a connecting circular arc. This changes which ground positions a sound-search pass samples.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Flight Plan; Block; Stage\u003c/p> \u003cp>The mission instructions interpreted by the autopilot. In Paparazzi, a block groups a mission activity and a stage is a step within it. Updating a target waypoint's coordinates does not, by itself, select a new block or restart the current stage.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Geolocation; Geotag\u003c/p> \u003cp>Geolocation estimates a geographic position, for example where an image pixel meets the ground. A geotag stores geographic information with an image. The aircraft's position in EXIF is not automatically the position of every object in that photograph.\u003c/p> \u003cp>GNSS And GPS\u003c/p> \u003cp>Global Navigation Satellite System is the general name for satellite navigation systems. Global Positioning System is one such system; \"GPS\" is also commonly used informally for the aircraft's satellite-derived position. Coordinate precision in a file does not establish position accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Heading And Course\u003c/p> \u003cp>Heading describes where the aircraft's nose points. Course describes the direction of travel over the ground. Wind can make them different, so course is not a substitute for camera yaw when projecting an image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>HOME; HOLD\u003c/p> \u003cp>Named waypoints in the example flight plans. HOME is the plan's home reference; HOLD is a configured waiting location where provided. They are not necessarily the aircraft's current position. Entering a HOLD block does not automatically mean hovering exactly where a failure happened.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MSL: Mean Sea Level; Ellipsoid Height; Geoid\u003c/p> \u003cp>Different references for altitude. MSL is a sea-level-based reference; ellipsoid height uses a mathematical Earth surface. A geoid model relates these references. For a simple example, ground at 30 m MSL and an aircraft at 80 m MSL give 50 m AGL. Do not subtract an MSL ground height from an ellipsoid aircraft height without the appropriate conversion.\u003c/p> \u003cp>NPS: New Paparazzi Simulator\u003c/p> \u003cp>Paparazzi's flight-simulation environment. It runs the aircraft software with a simulated aircraft so you can exercise a complete command-and-result loop without a physical flight. A successful simulation is not a measurement of real sensor accuracy or onboard processing speed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Pose\u003c/p> \u003cp>Position and orientation together. A useful sensor pose also says when it applies and which coordinate conventions it uses. The pose at request time may differ from the pose during exposure because the aircraft keeps moving.\u003c/p> \u003cp>UTM: Universal Transverse Mercator\u003c/p> \u003cp>A family of projected coordinate systems that express position in metres within numbered zones. UTM is convenient for local distance calculations, but an easting/northing pair must retain its zone and hemisphere.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Waypoint; Target\u003c/p> \u003cp>A named geographic point available to the flight plan. A sensing target may be stored as a waypoint, but detecting something and commanding the aircraft to fly toward it are separate actions. The flight plan chooses the navigation behaviour and flight altitude.\u003c/p> \u003cp>WGS84: World Geodetic System 1984\u003c/p> \u003cp>The Earth reference used for the latitude/longitude coordinates in these workflows. It provides a common geographic reference, not a guarantee that a reported position is correct to any particular number of metres.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Units: m, mm, Hz, fps, ms, us And ns\u003c/p> \u003cp>Metres and millimetres measure distance; 1 m is 1,000 mm. Hertz, Hz, counts cycles or updates per second; fps counts frames per second. Milliseconds, microseconds and nanoseconds are fractions of a second: 1 s is 1,000 ms, 1,000,000 us or 1,000,000,000 ns. A camera's advertised frame rate is not a promise of the saved-photo or inference rate.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#images-and-timing", "title": "Images And Timing", "text": "\u003cp>Calibration And Validation\u003c/p> \u003cp>Calibration estimates parameters or corrections using reference measurements. Validation checks performance on independent observations. A calibration file existing, or a fitting program finishing, is not the same as demonstrating accuracy on new measurements.\u003c/p> \u003cp>EXIF: Exchangeable Image File Format\u003c/p> \u003cp>Structured metadata that can be stored inside a photograph, including supported location, camera and exposure information. Sensoraia also preserves specific module-owned evidence records. Editing software may discard metadata; keep original files when you need their measurement history. More about Sensoraia's EXIF.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Exposure; Shutter Time; Rolling Shutter\u003c/p> \u003cp>An exposure is the interval over which a sensor gathers light. Shutter time is its duration. With a rolling shutter, different image rows are exposed at different times. A moving aircraft can therefore introduce blur or geometric skew even when one pose is stored for the whole image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Footprint; FOV: Field Of View\u003c/p> \u003cp>FOV is the angular extent of a camera's view; the footprint is the area of ground that view covers at a particular position, height and orientation. A wider lens or greater AGL generally covers more ground. Horizontal, vertical and diagonal FOV are different measurements, not interchangeable versions of one number.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Frame; Sequence Number\u003c/p> \u003cp>One image from a camera stream. A sequence number identifies its place in that stream and helps associate results with the correct observation. Camera sequence numbers and FC shot numbers are different identifiers; matching numeric values alone does not prove two records belong together.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Ground Plane; Nadir\u003c/p> \u003cp>A ground plane is a flat surface used to simplify image projection. Nadir means directly downward. A flat-ground calculation cannot account for every roof, tree or slope; a downward-looking camera can still move away from nadir when the aircraft banks.\u003c/p> \u003cp>JPEG, PNG, RGB And Grayscale\u003c/p> \u003cp>JPEG is a commonly compressed photographic image format; PNG is a lossless image format often used for diagrams and masks. RGB means red, green and blue colour channels; grayscale stores intensity without colour. These describe image storage or appearance, not whether the pixels are calibrated temperatures. JPEG expands to Joint Photographic Experts Group; PNG to Portable Network Graphics.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Latency; Jitter\u003c/p> \u003cp>Latency is delay, for example between requesting a photo and its exposure. Jitter is variation in that delay. File arrival time includes later processing and transfer, so it is not automatically the exposure time.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Metadata; Provenance\u003c/p> \u003cp>Metadata describes an observation: for example its pose, time, sensor or model identity. Provenance records where it came from and how it was processed. These help distinguish physical observations, simulated inputs and derived results without guessing from a filename.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Observation; Request Pose; Observation Pose\u003c/p> \u003cp>An observation is what a sensor measured, with its relevant context. Request pose describes the aircraft when the capture was requested; observation pose describes the estimated aircraft pose at sensing time. The latter requires timing evidence. Missing timing must not be silently relabelled as a synchronized observation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Pixel; Resolution\u003c/p> \u003cp>A pixel is one sample in an image grid. Image resolution commonly means its width and height in pixels; it is not the same as the detail the optics resolve or the accuracy of a geographic estimate. Enlarging an image creates more output pixels, not new measured detail.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sidecar; Embedded Data\u003c/p> \u003cp>A sidecar is a separate file associated with an image, such as an analysis INI file. Embedded data lives inside the image file itself. Keep required sidecars with their original image; do not assume every workflow writes them or that an image editor preserves every embedded record.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Synchronization; Clock Alignment\u003c/p> \u003cp>Relating events or clocks so the system can estimate which aircraft pose belongs to a measurement. This includes limits on timing uncertainty, not just putting timestamps on both files. At 10 m/s, a 0.1 s timing error alone corresponds to about 1 m of travel.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Timestamp; UTC; Monotonic Time\u003c/p> \u003cp>A timestamp says when something happened according to a particular clock. UTC, Coordinated Universal Time, is a shared civil-time reference. Monotonic time measures elapsed time without ordinary wall-clock jumps. Monotonic values from different computers cannot simply be subtracted as though their clock origins were the same.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Uncertainty, Precision And Accuracy\u003c/p> \u003cp>Uncertainty describes how well a value is known. Precision concerns repeatability or, in file output, the number of digits shown. Accuracy is closeness to an independent reference. More decimal places, a sharper image or a high model score do not by themselves establish better location accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#ai-and-models", "title": "AI And Models", "text": "\u003cp>AI, ML And Neural Network\u003c/p> \u003cp>Artificial Intelligence is the broad field of software performing tasks such as recognition. Machine Learning learns behaviour from examples. A neural network, or NN, is one kind of learned model. In Sensoraia, a network's job depends on its contract: detecting objects, classifying sound or proposing measurements are different tasks.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Class; Classification\u003c/p> \u003cp>A class is a label such as \"vehicle\" or an acoustic category. Classification chooses or scores categories for an input. It does not necessarily say where an object is in an image, and it does not automatically produce a geographic target.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Confidence; Score; Threshold\u003c/p> \u003cp>A model's score expresses support for an output according to that model. A threshold decides which scores to accept. Do not read 0.8 as an 80% chance of a correct result unless the model's calibration establishes that interpretation. Computational-validity flags and geographic accuracy are different quantities again.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CPU And Neural Accelerator\u003c/p> \u003cp>The Central Processing Unit runs general-purpose programs. A neural accelerator is specialized hardware for compatible model computations. Sensoraia's ONNX baseline runs on the CPU; an IMX500 RPK uses the AI Camera's neural processor. A model file alone does not make an accelerator available.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Dataset; Ground Truth\u003c/p> \u003cp>A dataset is a collection of examples. Ground truth is the reference answer associated with an example, such as a labelled object or a known synthetic field value. Training and evaluation need suitable labels, rights and representative inputs; saved imagery is not automatically a ready-to-use training dataset.\u003c/p> \u003cp>False Positive And False Negative\u003c/p> \u003cp>A false positive reports something that is not there; a false negative misses something that is. Adjusting a score threshold changes this trade-off. One successful demonstration does not measure how often either occurs in different light, terrain, sound or weather.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Inference And Training\u003c/p> \u003cp>Training learns model parameters from examples. Inference runs the already trained model on a new input. Selecting an ONNX model and analysing a photo performs inference; it does not train or automatically improve that model.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Model; Graph; Checkpoint\u003c/p> \u003cp>A learned model combines computations with trained parameters. An exported graph describes those computations in a runtime-readable form. A checkpoint stores a particular training state. Models are not interchangeable just because their filenames have the same extension.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Model Descriptor; Model Contract\u003c/p> \u003cp>The descriptor states which graph and tensor layout an application expects, including preprocessing and a graph hash. The wider contract says what the inputs and outputs mean. A vehicle detector is not a visual-return model, and a generic feature vector is not a navigation direction. Choose a compatible model bundle.\u003c/p> \u003cp>ONNX And ONNX Runtime; ORT\u003c/p> \u003cp>Open Neural Network Exchange is the model format. ONNX Runtime, often abbreviated ORT, is the engine that executes compatible graphs. Sensoraia applications share native runtime support but select their own models and settings. ONNX support does not imply support for every custom operator.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Preprocessing; Normalization; Letterbox\u003c/p> \u003cp>Preparing input exactly as a model expects. Normalization rescales values; letterbox resizes an image while preserving its aspect ratio and pads the remaining area. Colour order and interpolation also matter: a plausible-looking image is not enough to establish the right numerical input.\u003c/p> \u003cp>FP32, INT8 And Quantization\u003c/p> \u003cp>FP32 uses 32-bit floating-point numbers. INT8 uses 8-bit signed integers. Quantization represents values at reduced numerical precision, usually with scales and offsets. It may reduce model cost, but speed and accuracy depend on the export and runtime. An INT8 filename alone is not proof of compatibility, acceleration or unchanged quality.\u003c/p> \u003cp>RPK\u003c/p> \u003cp>The model-package file used by the IMX500 camera path. It must suit the camera and application contract; it is not a general-purpose CPU model. An \u003ccode>.onnx\u003c/code> and an \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code> are different deployment choices, not extensions to rename.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Tensor; Shape; NCHW And NHWC\u003c/p> \u003cp>A tensor is a structured array of numbers passed to or from a model. Shape specifies its dimensions. For image batches, NCHW means batch, channels, height, width; NHWC means batch, height, width, channels. The same numbers arranged differently can mean a completely different input.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#installation-and-operation", "title": "Installation And Operation", "text": "\u003cp>ABI: Application Binary Interface\u003c/p> \u003cp>The low-level rules that let a compiled program use libraries and operating system facilities. Two ARM64 files can still be incompatible if their runtime requirements differ. Matching the processor name alone is not a deployment compatibility check.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Architecture: x86-64, ARM64 And AArch64\u003c/p> \u003cp>The processor instruction family a binary runs on. ARM64 and AArch64 refer to the 64-bit Arm target used by MORA; x86-64 is the development-PC target in the supplied builds. The production source is shared, but the compiled binaries are different.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Build; Cross-compile; Deploy\u003c/p> \u003cp>To build is to turn source code into runnable binaries. To cross-compile is to build for another target, such as compiling ARM64 on the PC. To deploy is to upload and activate a prepared release on MORA. Building or packaging does not deploy it, and deployment can restart USHER. Follow the installation path.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CLI; Option; Flag; Environment Variable\u003c/p> \u003cp>Command-Line Interface means operating a program through typed commands. An option or flag, such as \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code>, changes that invocation. An environment variable, such as \u003ccode>AIRCRAFT\u003c/code>, supplies context inherited by programs. Neither automatically rewrites an application's settings file.\u003c/p> \u003cp>INI, JSON, CSV And XML\u003c/p> \u003cp>Text formats for different kinds of information. INI is an initialization- file format used for settings; JSON, JavaScript Object Notation, stores structured values; CSV, Comma-Separated Values, stores rows of data; XML, Extensible Markup Language, uses named elements, including Paparazzi flight plans. A JSON file does not require a JavaScript runtime.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Firmware\u003c/p> \u003cp>Software running inside a device such as a camera or flight controller. Uploading a Sensoraia application to MORA does not replace Canon firmware, install CHDK on its SD card or flash a new autopilot image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Headless; SSH\u003c/p> \u003cp>Headless operation uses no locally attached display or keyboard. Secure Shell, SSH, provides a remote terminal. A command typed in an SSH session runs on the remote computer; a command typed in your ordinary PC terminal runs on the PC. This distinction matters for build and deployment commands.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Log; Diagnostic; Exit Status\u003c/p> \u003cp>A log records what software reports while running. A diagnostic explains a condition or failure. Exit status is the final numeric result of a command, normally zero for success. Output text alone may describe an intermediate stage; inspect the saved result and final status too.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Native\u003c/p> \u003cp>Compiled machine-code execution, here primarily C and C++. In a build command, \"native build\" also means a build for the current PC rather than a cross-build. Native does not mean real-camera input, hardware acceleration, no dependencies or demonstrated whole-board memory headroom.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Package; Release\u003c/p> \u003cp>A prepared collection of application binaries and required settings, models, runtime libraries and notices. A release identifies a particular collection for distribution or deployment. A package on the PC is not already activated on MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Absolute Path; Relative Path; Working Directory\u003c/p> \u003cp>An absolute path starts at the filesystem root, such as \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia/bin/earcam\u003c/code>. A relative path needs a reference directory. Shared model-settings paths are relative to their INI file; ordinary command arguments may instead be relative to the shell's working directory. Moving a settings file can therefore change what its paths select.\u003c/p> \u003cp>RAM; CMA; MiB\u003c/p> \u003cp>Random Access Memory is working memory shared by the operating system, applications and device buffers. The Pi Zero 2 W reference board has 512 MB total; other MORA boards may have different capacities. Linux's Contiguous Memory Allocator, CMA, reserves contiguous memory for devices such as cameras. MiB, mebibytes, counts bytes in powers of two. A small executable file does not imply a small runtime memory requirement.\u003c/p> \u003cp>SDK; Toolchain; Sysroot\u003c/p> \u003cp>A Software Development Kit supplies build-time components for a target. A toolchain includes its compiler and related tools; a sysroot provides target headers and libraries. Sensoraia prepares these on the development PC for cross-compilation. They are not the application runtime package deployed to MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Service; systemd; Journal\u003c/p> \u003cp>A service runs under operating-system supervision rather than only inside an interactive terminal. On MORA, systemd manages \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code>; its journal contains service logs. Editing a service configuration is different from rebuilding the application or changing a camera's firmware.\u003c/p> \u003cp>SHA-256; Hash; Checksum\u003c/p> \u003cp>A hash is a fingerprint computed from file contents; SHA-256 is Secure Hash Algorithm 256-bit. A checksum comparison can reveal changed or mismatched files. It does not establish trustworthy authorship by itself: an attacker could replace both an untrusted file and its untrusted expected hash.\u003c/p> \u003cp>USB, UVC, PTP And CSI\u003c/p> \u003cp>Universal Serial Bus, USB, connects devices such as microphones and thermal cameras. USB Video Class, UVC, and Picture Transfer Protocol, PTP, are different device protocols carried over USB. The Camera Serial Interface, CSI, is the ribbon-cable camera connection used by the AI Camera. These are not substitutes for the FC-to-MORA UART link.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#aicam", "title": "AIcam", "text": "\u003cp>AIcam is the visible-camera path using the Raspberry Pi AI Camera. Spottercam and LOOMcam can use the same physical camera for different processing jobs. AI Camera setup and operation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>IMX500\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sony's image sensor with an integrated neural processor, used in the Raspberry Pi AI Camera. Its hardware can run a compatible RPK while capturing images. Ordinary image capture does not automatically enable a particular detector.\u003c/p> \u003cp>libcamera; rpicam-still\u003c/p> \u003cp>libcamera is the camera-control framework used by the native camera workers. rpicam-still is a separate Raspberry Pi camera application used by the stock capture path. They are not two names for the same executable. A physical camera still needs one capture owner at a time.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sensor Mode; Crop; Binning\u003c/p> \u003cp>A sensor mode chooses how the camera reads its image. A crop selects part of the sensor; binning combines samples to produce a lower-resolution readout. Changing these can change image geometry, so a calibration for one mode must not be assumed valid for another merely by matching the filename.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#chdkbridge", "title": "CHDKbridge", "text": "\u003cp>CHDKbridge controls supported Canon cameras from MORA or the PC. Canon-camera setup and commands.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CHDK: Canon Hack Development Kit\u003c/p> \u003cp>A separately installed software add-on running inside a supported Canon camera. It adds scripting and control functions. CHDK must match the camera model and firmware; installing Sensoraia on MORA does not install CHDK on the camera.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CHDKbridge\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sensoraia's native controller that communicates with camera-side CHDK over USB/PTP. It runs on MORA or the PC, not inside the Canon camera. USHER's CHDK backend invokes it for the selected camera work.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Direct Download\u003c/p> \u003cp>The optional CHDK capture path that transfers JPEG data directly over USB when the camera port supports it. Ordinary capture saves the photo on the camera's SD card and downloads that file. CHDK support alone does not prove the direct-JPEG capability is available.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Optical Zoom; Normalized Zoom\u003c/p> \u003cp>Optical zoom changes the lens's magnification. CHDKbridge's normalized 0..255 control maps onto the camera's available zoom steps; 128 is roughly the midpoint of that control range, not a guaranteed 2x magnification. This is different from cropping or enlarging pixels digitally.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Playback Mode; Lens Retraction\u003c/p> \u003cp>Playback is the camera mode used for reviewing images rather than taking them. Requesting it can let the powered Canon camera retract its lens. Mode confirmation does not measure physical lens position or retraction time; a later capture can extend the lens again.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#diplacam", "title": "Diplacam", "text": "\u003cp>Diplacam proposes where to measure next using accumulated knowledge of a field, rather than following only a fixed coverage pattern. The measurement-to-plan guide.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Belief; Mean; Variance\u003c/p> \u003cp>The planner's current estimate of the field and its uncertainty. The mean is the estimated value at each grid cell; variance describes uncertainty in that estimate. Belief is not the complete field truth: it changes as measurements arrive.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CMA-ES: Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy\u003c/p> \u003cp>A numerical search method that improves candidate plans by evaluating and adapting a population of proposals. Diplacam offers it as an explicitly selected comparison mode, not a silent substitute for a failed ONNX model. It is unrelated to Linux camera-memory CMA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>DDIM: Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model\u003c/p> \u003cp>A sampling method that repeatedly refines a noisy candidate using a diffusion model. Diplacam runs twenty native denoising steps. One ONNX call predicts noise for one step; it is not the entire eight-point planner by itself.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Diffusion Planner\u003c/p> \u003cp>A learned planner that turns an initial noisy candidate into proposed measurement locations, conditioned on the current belief and aircraft position. \"Diffusion\" here describes the model's generation process, not physical spreading of a measured substance.\u003c/p> \u003cp>GP: Gaussian Process; Posterior\u003c/p> \u003cp>A statistical model that relates nearby field values and maintains an estimate with uncertainty. After incorporating a measurement, the updated estimate is the posterior. Measuring one area can change expectations nearby, but does not turn unmeasured cells into independently observed truth.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Informative Path Planning\u003c/p> \u003cp>Choosing measurement locations to learn useful information efficiently, rather than merely visiting evenly spaced positions. The research objective combines expected information and travel cost. Whether a particular model achieves useful plans needs evaluation in the intended conditions.\u003c/p> \u003cp>P1-P8; Plan Generation; Snapshot\u003c/p> \u003cp>The eight waypoints refreshed by the Diplacam example. A generation identifies one completed native plan; a snapshot keeps its eight points together during retrieval. Publishing eight ordinary GCS waypoint moves is not an atomic eight-point update of the FC.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Scalar Field; Synthetic Observation\u003c/p> \u003cp>A map with one numerical value at each location. Diplacam's supplied CSV stands in for sensor returns: only the current footprint is sampled into the GP belief. It is not an RGB image, a full spectral-image cube or an already implemented driver for a physical sensor.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Training Range; Predicted Z\u003c/p> \u003cp>The conditions represented during model training. Diplacam's bundled model uses XYZ internally and was trained for 10..40 m heights; the current demo commands 2 m and publishes only XY. Predicted Z is discarded as a flight command. Correct software execution at 2 m does not establish planning quality outside the model's training range.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#earcam", "title": "EARcam", "text": "\u003cp>EARcam searches by sound: it associates microphone measurements with aircraft positions to estimate where an alarm source is located. Continue with the sound-search guide.\u003c/p> \u003cp>ALSA: Advanced Linux Sound Architecture\u003c/p> \u003cp>The Linux sound system that supplies microphone samples to EARcam. An ALSA device name selects an audio input; it does not select an acoustic detection algorithm.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Band-pass filter\u003c/p> \u003cp>A filter that keeps a chosen range of sound frequencies and reduces sounds outside it. EARcam uses this to concentrate on the alarm's frequency range rather than treating all microphone noise as equally useful.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Contrast: EARcam tone contrast\u003c/p> \u003cp>A comparison between the strongest detected tone and the energy across the selected frequency band. A distinct alarm tone has stronger contrast than broad noise. This is an audio quality check, not visual image contrast.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Decibels: dB, dBFS And dB SPL\u003c/p> \u003cp>A logarithmic way to compare signal levels. The reference matters: dBFS means decibels relative to a digital recording's maximum level; dB SPL means sound-pressure level relative to a standard physical pressure. A microphone's dBFS reading is not automatically a calibrated dB SPL reading. Keep the microphone gain consistent when comparing EARcam measurements.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Envelope\u003c/p> \u003cp>A smoothed description of how loud a signal is over time. It bridges short gaps between an alarm's pulses so one brief quiet interval does not make the source appear to vanish.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Gauss-Newton\u003c/p> \u003cp>A repeated-improvement method for fitting a mathematical model to measurements. EARcam uses a fitted sound model to estimate a source position from readings taken at different aircraft positions. It does not simply choose the coordinates of the single loudest reading.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Goertzel filter\u003c/p> \u003cp>An efficient way to measure how much of a particular tone is present in a sound recording. EARcam examines several nearby frequencies to accommodate variation in the alarm tone.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Hampel filter And MAD: Median Absolute Deviation\u003c/p> \u003cp>A method for spotting measurements that differ unusually strongly from nearby ones. MAD measures typical spread around the median, so one extreme spike does not set the scale for everything else. EARcam uses this kind of robust filtering to reduce the effect of isolated bad readings.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Huber weights\u003c/p> \u003cp>A way to reduce the influence of measurements that disagree strongly with a model, without giving up on the whole collection. In EARcam's position fit, this helps prevent a few unusual sound readings from dominating the answer.\u003c/p> \u003cp>motionSCOUT\u003c/p> \u003cp>MSA's personal motion-alarm device, used as the sound source in the EARcam search example. Its pulsed alarm gives the detector a recognizable tone pattern to look for. It is the target sound source, not a component running on MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Sound picture\u003c/p> \u003cp>The JPEG heat map produced after an EARcam search. It makes the sound-search result easy to inspect and associate with flight metadata. It is a computed visualization, not a photograph taken by a microphone.\u003c/p> \u003cp>PCM: Pulse-Code Modulation; Sample Rate\u003c/p> \u003cp>A representation of recorded sound as regularly spaced numerical samples. The sample rate counts samples per second, not how often a position estimate is produced. An audio model must receive the declared rate and window length; changing only a file's label does not resample its audio.\u003c/p> \u003cp>RMS: Root Mean Square\u003c/p> \u003cp>A measure of signal level across a window of samples. EARcam's raw RMS level and an optional classifier's probability describe different things. A loud sound need not be the desired alarm, and a high classifier score is not a calibrated sound-pressure measurement.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Star Refinement\u003c/p> \u003cp>An acoustic-search pattern with passes in several directions around a candidate location. The different viewing positions help refine the sound source fit; it is not a new type of microphone. The flight plan still owns the actual path and its constraints.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#loomcam", "title": "LOOMcam", "text": "\u003cp>LOOMcam is the experimental visual-return application. Its model integration and bounded target policy are present; a trained homing network is not included. Visual-return settings and workflow.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Features; Embedding\u003c/p> \u003cp>Numbers produced by a network to describe an input. An embedding arranges descriptions so they can be compared in the intended feature space. Features from different models may not be comparable. A feature vector alone does not specify a return direction or a geographic position.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Homing; Visual Return\u003c/p> \u003cp>Using visual information to help identify a direction toward a known home region.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Reference Photographs; Outbound Phase\u003c/p> \u003cp>Images retained during the outward part of the example flight for possible later visual comparison. References carry their available capture and model evidence. Keeping these photos is not proof that a model can reliably match them from a different viewpoint.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Return Vector; Vector Frame\u003c/p> \u003cp>A declared model output describing a direction toward the return spot. Its frame states whether components mean Earth \u003ccode>north_east\u003c/code>, aircraft \u003ccode>body_forward_right\u003c/code> (rotated into Earth coordinates using the photo's EXIF IMU roll/pitch and magnetometer heading), or \u003ccode>simulated\u003c/code> (an explicitly labelled homeward vector seeded by the photo's neural features for testing before a model is trained). LOOMcam checks and converts the declared frame; it never guesses the meaning from two raw numbers.\u003c/p> \u003cp>T1; Look-ahead; HOME Protection Radius\u003c/p> \u003cp>LOOMcam's example return waypoint, initially at HOME. Look-ahead places a target along an accepted homing ray; the protection radius (\u003ccode>60 m\u003c/code> by default) locks \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> at HOME once the aircraft arrives so it can settle into a smooth \u003ccode>80 m\u003c/code> standby orbit without chasing a point underneath its belly. These checks bound target updates; they do not demonstrate visual homing accuracy by an untrained model.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#lwircam", "title": "LWIRcam", "text": "\u003cp>LWIRcam captures supported thermal cameras. Measurement and geolocation depend on the camera's available data and the enabled analysis features. Capture guide | Thermal calibration guide\u003c/p> \u003cp>LWIR: Long-Wave Infrared\u003c/p> \u003cp>The infrared wavelength region used by these thermal cameras. A displayed thermal image represents detected infrared information; its colour palette is not visible-light colour, and brightness is not automatically a calibrated temperature reading.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Radiometric; Temperature Plane; Image-only\u003c/p> \u003cp>Radiometric data supports numerical temperature-related measurements. A temperature plane contains those per-pixel values separately from the display image. Supported Tiny1C capture preserves this plane; the supported TDK image-only mode does not provide it. A colourful thermal JPEG alone cannot supply missing temperature samples.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Kelvin64\u003c/p> \u003cp>The preserved Tiny1C temperature encoding: kelvin multiplied by 64. For a valid sample, divide by 64 to obtain kelvin, then subtract 273.15 for Celsius. Reserved invalid codes are not cold or hot temperatures and must not be converted into ordinary measurements.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Emissivity; Reflected Temperature\u003c/p> \u003cp>Emissivity describes how effectively a surface emits thermal radiation compared with an ideal emitter. Reflected temperature accounts for thermal radiation reflected from the surroundings. A shiny surface can show reflected heat rather than its own temperature; visual brightness cannot resolve that ambiguity on its own.\u003c/p> \u003cp>FFC And NUC\u003c/p> \u003cp>Flat-Field Correction and Non-Uniformity Correction address uneven response across a thermal sensor. A camera may use a shutter/reference event as part of its correction. This is different from lens calibration and does not by itself establish absolute-temperature accuracy. Do not assume a software readback records every correction event.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Intrinsics; Extrinsics; Boresight\u003c/p> \u003cp>Intrinsics describe the lens and sensor geometry, including focal lengths, image centre and distortion. Extrinsics describe how the camera is mounted relative to the aircraft. Boresight concerns the viewing-axis alignment. Both lens geometry and mounting matter when placing a hotspot on a map.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Palette; Display Window; Percentiles\u003c/p> \u003cp>A palette converts displayed levels into colours. A display window chooses the temperature range mapped into those levels. Percentiles can choose a range from the distribution of valid values. These can make a scene easier to inspect without changing the preserved temperature samples.\u003c/p> \u003cp>ROI: Region Of Interest\u003c/p> \u003cp>A selected part of an image or sensor plane. LWIRcam can measure a rectangular ROI, point or line using native sensor coordinates. Rotating the displayed JPEG does not rotate those measurement coordinates; check the coordinate convention before selecting a region.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Hotspot; Ground Projection\u003c/p> \u003cp>A region meeting the thermal detector's selection conditions. Ground projection estimates where its image position intersects the assumed ground using calibration and observation pose. It is an estimated location, not proof that the region is a fire or that a surveyed error requirement has been met.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Correction Profile; Hold-out Check\u003c/p> \u003cp>A correction profile records measured corrections for a specified thermal device and configuration. A hold-out check uses reference measurements not used to fit those corrections. Lens, mounting and temperature checks solve different problems; one successful calibration does not replace the others.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#pangea", "title": "PANGEA", "text": "\u003cp>PANGEA, Photo-Assisted Neighboring Geographic Edge Assembly, turns saved photographs and their supported positioning information into photo maps. It is a separate processing job, not a prerequisite for every capture. Photo-map workflows.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Georeferenced\u003c/p> \u003cp>Associated with a geographic coordinate system so image positions can be placed on a map. A georeferenced output can still have errors from pose, timing, lens assumptions or terrain; the presence of coordinates is not a survey-accuracy certificate.\u003c/p> \u003cp>GSD: Ground Sampling Distance\u003c/p> \u003cp>The ground distance represented by an image pixel, often expressed in centimetres per pixel. A map at 10 cm/pixel does not automatically have 10 cm positioning accuracy or recover detail finer than its source images.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Mosaic; Orthomap\u003c/p> \u003cp>A mosaic combines several images into one visual map. An orthomap places imagery in a map-like, top-down geometry. PANGEA's reference-plane workflows do not reconstruct every terrain height, so elevated objects can still be displaced or show seams.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Seam; Blending; Exposure Compensation\u003c/p> \u003cp>A seam decides where overlapping source images contribute. Blending smooths their visual transition; exposure compensation reduces brightness differences. These improve appearance but do not correct all geometric misalignment or establish absolute colour/temperature calibration.\u003c/p> \u003cp>SIMPLE And GREAT\u003c/p> \u003cp>Names of PANGEA's two completed-survey modes. SIMPLE uses a simpler compositing approach; GREAT adds graph-cut seam selection and multiband blending. They describe processing choices, not formal accuracy grades. The separate live photo-map workflow has its own input and resource rules.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Coverage; Gap Filling\u003c/p> \u003cp>Coverage says which ground was represented by accepted photographs. Gap filling makes output visually complete using supported source-colour completion. Filled pixels are not extra photographs or newly measured terrain. An output with no blank pixels need not have complete observed coverage.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Incremental Map; Provisional Revision; Finalize\u003c/p> \u003cp>An incremental map updates as accepted photos arrive. A provisional revision is a coherent intermediate product, not a complete survey. Finalizing ends input collection and produces the finished session output; it does not independently validate geographic accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Manifest; Checkpoint; Resume\u003c/p> \u003cp>A manifest identifies inputs or packaged files. A checkpoint records completed work and the identities needed to reuse it. Resume continues a matching interrupted job rather than starting from zero. Editing committed inputs can invalidate that match; a checkpoint is not permission to mix unrelated surveys.\u003c/p> \u003cp>XYZ Tiles; Tile Pyramid; Zoom Level\u003c/p> \u003cp>A web-map layout addressing square image tiles by zoom level and X/Y indices. A tile pyramid stores several zoom levels for efficient viewing. Higher zoom shows a finer grid; it does not create finer source detail. XYZ here means tile addresses, not a three-dimensional point or elevation model.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#mimic", "title": "MIMIC", "text": "\u003cp>MIMIC is Map-tile Imagerie Modeling in Interactive Camera-sim: the map-based camera simulator used in the PC examples. Make camera views from cached imagery.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Map Cache; Map Tile\u003c/p> \u003cp>A map tile is one small image from a map provider. The cache is the local collection already downloaded by the GCS. MIMIC uses the configured cached aerial imagery to render a camera view; displaying the aircraft in the GCS does not automatically prove every tile in its camera footprint is available.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Mock; Fake Camera; Simulated Detection\u003c/p> \u003cp>A mock replaces a particular component for a test. A fake camera can supply saved or rendered images while the real native model still runs. A simulated detection instead fabricates a detector result under an explicit demo policy. These are different substitutions; a mock JPEG does not mean every later processing stage is fake.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Rendered Camera View\u003c/p> \u003cp>An image generated from the simulated camera's pose and ground imagery. Turning or banking the aircraft changes what it sees. MIMIC's ground texture is not a physical exposure, and visible aerial imagery rendered for a thermal path is not a temperature measurement.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MORA-in-the-loop; Camera-in-the-loop\u003c/p> \u003cp>In MORA-in-the-loop, a simulated flight exchanges requests with USHER on the real board. Camera-in-the-loop includes supported physical capture hardware. These test different boundaries; real capture paired with simulated pose does not establish real-flight geolocation accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Random Seed\u003c/p> \u003cp>A starting value that makes a pseudo-random demo repeatable under the same conditions. It helps compare software behaviour across runs. Seeded random targets are still invented targets, not observations made by a trained detector.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#camera-lens", "title": "Camera Lens", "text": "\u003cp>The optional lens emulator adds declared optical and shutter effects to supported MIMIC imagery while retaining the clean source. Lens profiles and examples.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Lens Distortion; Fisheye\u003c/p> \u003cp>Lens geometry that bends the relation between a scene direction and an image position. A fisheye deliberately uses a wide-angle projection with strong distortion. Correct projection needs that lens's model, not just a wider crop of a pinhole image.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Pinhole Model; Ray\u003c/p> \u003cp>A simplified geometric camera model. A ray is an imagined line from the camera through an image pixel; intersecting it with the assumed ground locates that pixel's footprint. The model describes geometry, not measured focus, lens blur or actual shutter timing.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Lens Profile; Derivative Image\u003c/p> \u003cp>A lens profile declares the emulation's optical and exposure parameters. A derivative is the processed image made from an original. Labelling an emulated photo preserves its meaning; it does not turn assumed parameters into a calibrated physical camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#spottercam", "title": "Spottercam", "text": "\u003cp>Spottercam detects objects, with the supplied application focused on vehicles. Its image detections and geographic target updates are separate steps. Vehicle-search guide.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Bounding Box; Detection\u003c/p> \u003cp>A bounding box encloses a detected object in image coordinates. Detection reports a class, box and score. It does not by itself locate that object on Earth; geographic projection also needs the corresponding camera geometry, pose and ground assumptions.\u003c/p> \u003cp>IoU And NMS\u003c/p> \u003cp>Intersection over Union measures overlap between two boxes: overlap area divided by their combined area. Non-Maximum Suppression reduces competing boxes for the same apparent object. These operate on image detections, not on the aircraft's navigation waypoints.\u003c/p> \u003cp>T1-T5; Best-Five Targets\u003c/p> \u003cp>Five target waypoints in the Spotter demo, initially at HOME. The native store fills empty slots, then replaces the lowest score only with a strictly higher one. These are not LOOMcam's single return target T1, and moving them does not automatically interrupt the survey.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Zero Detections; Analysis Failure\u003c/p> \u003cp>Zero detections means a model ran successfully without accepted objects. Analysis failure means the result could not be produced or trusted. They are not interchangeable. A genuine empty result must not trigger fabricated detections; the explicit random spotting policy belongs only to the simulation harness's documented fallback cases.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "glossary.html#abbreviations-a-z", "title": "Abbreviations A-Z", "text": "\u003cp>Each link leads to the explanation and its practical meaning above. Names such as MORA, USHER and SODA are project names; their roles are more useful than an invented expansion of their letters.\u003c/p> Term Meaning Or Place To Start ABI Application Binary Interface ACK / NACK Acknowledgement / negative acknowledgement AGL Above Ground Level AI / ML / NN Artificial Intelligence / Machine Learning / neural network ALSA Advanced Linux Sound Architecture API Application Programming Interface ARM64 / AArch64 The 64-bit Arm build target CHDK Canon Hack Development Kit CLI Command-Line Interface CMA Linux Contiguous Memory Allocator CMA-ES Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy CPU Central Processing Unit CSI Camera Serial Interface CSV Comma-Separated Values dB / dBFS / dB SPL Decibels; the digital or physical reference matters DDIM Denoising Diffusion Implicit Model ENU / NED East/North/Up; North/East/Down EXIF Exchangeable Image File Format FC Flight Controller FFC / NUC Flat-Field Correction / Non-Uniformity Correction FOV Field Of View FP32 / INT8 32-bit floating-point / 8-bit signed integer fps Frames per second GCS Ground Control Station GNSS / GPS Global Navigation Satellite System / Global Positioning System GP Gaussian Process GSD Ground Sampling Distance Hz Hertz; cycles or updates per second INI / JSON Settings format / JavaScript Object Notation IoU / NMS Intersection over Union / Non-Maximum Suppression IPC Inter-Process Communication JPEG / PNG / RGB Image formats and red/green/blue colour channels LWIR Long-Wave Infrared MAD Median Absolute Deviation MiB / RAM Mebibytes / Random Access Memory MIMIC Map-tile camera simulation ms / us / ns Milliseconds / microseconds / nanoseconds MSL Mean Sea Level NCHW / NHWC Image-tensor dimension orders NPS New Paparazzi Simulator ONNX / ORT Open Neural Network Exchange / ONNX Runtime PANGEA Photo-Assisted Neighboring Geographic Edge Assembly PCM / RMS Pulse-Code Modulation; Root Mean Square PTP / UVC Picture Transfer Protocol / USB Video Class ROI Region Of Interest RPK The IMX500 model package SDK Software Development Kit SHA-256 Secure Hash Algorithm 256-bit SSH Secure Shell TX / RX / GND Transmit / receive / ground UART Universal Asynchronous Receiver/Transmitter USB Universal Serial Bus UTC Coordinated Universal Time UTM Universal Transverse Mercator WGS84 World Geodetic System 1984 XML Extensible Markup Language XYZ Map-tile addresses, not height data"}, {"location": "licensing.html", "title": "Licensing", "text": "\u003cp>Sensoraia's software, build scripts and documentation are copyright OpenUAS and licensed under the Apache License 2.0. Read the complete license for its terms.\u003c/p> \u003cp>All current software and modules within the Sensoraia project exclusively use functionality licensed under the Apache License 2.0; no other licensed code is used across the application suite.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "licensing.html#dependencies-and-models", "title": "Dependencies And Models", "text": "\u003cp>Third-party libraries, source files, firmware, neural-network models and external projects keep their own licenses and notices. The project's Apache license does not replace those terms. Preserve the notices supplied with a source checkout or release package when redistributing it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>JPEG encoding and decoding use the IJG JPEG sources bundled with OpenCV, not the TurboJPEG API or codec. The release includes the IJG license and copyright notices in \u003ccode>licenses/jpeg/README\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>This software is based in part on the work of the Independent JPEG Group.\u003c/p> \u003cp>CHDK firmware is prepared separately by the camera owner; it is not included in Sensoraia's release package. Paparazzi is also a separate project. Review their terms if you distribute them with your own system.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "licensing.html#no-operational-certification", "title": "No Operational Certification", "text": "\u003cp>Sensoraia is provided under its license terms, not as certified flight, life-safety, thermal-measurement or navigation equipment. You are responsible for configuring and validating the complete hardware and software system for its intended operation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html", "title": "LOOMcam: Visual-Return Homing", "text": "\u003cp>LOOMcam captures outbound reference photographs on the way out and turns downward return-leg photographs into a course ray back toward HOME. On every accepted return view, T1 is updated along the predicted homing ray while the Flight Controller stays in full command of the flight plan, altitude, and landing sequence.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A trained homing network is not included. In the PC simulation, LOOMcam runs its selected ONNX model on real MIMIC camera views and can synthesize a clearly labelled, bounded homeward vector from the photo's neural features so you can fly and test the entire capture, EXIF, ONNX, and waypoint-update loop before your neural network has earned its pilot's license. Vehicle-detector outputs or generic features are never silently treated as real navigation vectors.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Visual-return terms | Try Sensoraia with Paparazzi | Paparazzi first-flight walkthrough | AI Camera setup\u003c/p> \u003cp>For PC flights, select the simulated camera profile before launching Paparazzi Center. The LOOM session now enables the nominal AIcam lens/shutter profile by default; \u003ccode>LOOM_CAMERA_PROFILE=none\u003c/code> restores the clean pinhole camera.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#how-the-visual-return-loop-works", "title": "How the Visual-Return Loop Works", "text": "\u003cp>Everything in the LOOMcam pipeline has a single, clear job\u2014nobody tries to fly the airplane from inside a camera process:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>MORA (\u003ccode>usher\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code>) is a stateless Flight Controller helper. It   takes periodic ground photos when commanded, stamps the Flight Controller's   Baro altitude, IMU roll/pitch, and Magneto heading into EXIF, runs ONNX   inside \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code>, and returns a suggested \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> target. MORA holds zero   flight-plan state.\u003c/li> \u003cli>USHER (\u003ccode>usher\u003c/code>) handles the UART wire protocol, camera triggers, and EXIF   tagging with zero embedded ONNX Runtime.\u003c/li> \u003cli>The Autopilot (Paparazzi) owns the mission blocks (\u003ccode>Outbound\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Return\u003c/code>,   \u003ccode>Standby\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Land\u003c/code>), validates incoming \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> updates, and flies the aircraft.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> Mission Phase Camera Command What MORA &amp; \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> Do What the Autopilot Does 1. Outbound \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> (recording on) Captures downward JPEGs, stamps Baro height, IMU tilt, and Magneto yaw into EXIF, and links up to 256 reference photos into \u003ccode>references/session-&lt;token&gt;/\u003c/code>. Flies from \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>TURN\u003c/code> along the outbound leg while triggering a photo every 3 seconds with wings level. 2. Return \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code> (recording off) Evaluates each return view in RAM without archiving extra reference copies, runs ONNX in \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code>, rotates the vector into Earth \u003ccode>(North, East)\u003c/code>, and proposes \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code>. Flies wings-level toward \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code>, locks onto \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> inside the 60 m gate, and enters a calm 80 m standby orbit. \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#why-ray-intercept-an-80-m-orbit-beats-a-short-carrot", "title": "Why Ray-Intercept + an 80 m Orbit Beats a Short Carrot", "text": "\u003cp>If you place a waypoint carrot just 50 m ahead of a fixed-wing aircraft cruising at 16 m/s, the aircraft covers that distance in 3.1 seconds. By the time the next camera frame is processed, the plane has already overrun the point and rolled into a 35\u00b0 bank trying to chase its own tail. Worse yet, a belly-mounted camera banked at 35\u00b0 is staring sideways at the horizon instead of looking at the terrain underneath the aircraft.\u003c/p> \u003cp>LOOMcam avoids the \"dog-chasing-its-tail\" spiral in two ways:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Attitude-gated capture (\u003ccode>return_max_tilt_deg=25\u003c/code>): When a turn or gust    tilts the aircraft beyond 25\u00b0 of roll or pitch, \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> skips steering for    that frame rather than trusting a skewed, oblique view of the countryside.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Ray-intercept projection &amp; 80 m orbit: Instead of chasing a short carrot    under the nose, \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> is projected along the homing course ray to intercept    the target area, and the flight plan transitions into a smooth 80 m    standby circle once inside the 60 m home-protection radius. Wings stay    level on the approach, the camera keeps a clear view of the ground, and the    plane orbits calmly over home until you command landing.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#what-gets-stamped-into-every-photos-exif", "title": "What Gets Stamped Into Every Photo's EXIF", "text": "\u003cp>When the Flight Controller triggers a shot, USHER pairs the image with the freshest autopilot pose and writes standard EXIF fields before \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> inspects the frame:\u003c/p> Datum Aircraft Sensor EXIF Tag Why \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> Needs It Height (\u003ccode>alt_m\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>agl\u003c/code>) Barometer / navigation altitude \u003ccode>GPSAltitude\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>ImageDescription\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>agl=...\u003c/code>) Scales ground-footprint geometry and logs capture height Roll &amp; Pitch (\u003ccode>phi\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>theta\u003c/code>) IMU attitude estimator \u003ccode>ImageDescription\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>roll=... pitch=...\u003c/code>) Rejects banked frames (\u003ccode>&gt; 25\u00b0\u003c/code>) and corrects body-to-horizontal tilt Heading (\u003ccode>psi\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>yaw_deg\u003c/code>) Magnetometer / AHRS heading \u003ccode>GPSImgDirection\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>True North\u003c/code>) + \u003ccode>ImageDescription\u003c/code> Rotates body \u003ccode>(forward, right)\u003c/code> vectors into Earth \u003ccode>(North, East)\u003c/code> Capture Position Autopilot navigation state \u003ccode>GPSLatitude\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GPSLongitude\u003c/code> Anchors outbound references and bounded step checks"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#run-a-manual-simulated-flight-on-your-pc", "title": "Run a Manual Simulated Flight on Your PC", "text": "\u003cp>You can fly the complete outbound-and-return loop at your desk without a physical airplane or windburn. Paparazzi's NPS flies the simulated fixed-wing aircraft, MIMIC renders realistic downward camera photographs from your cached Valkenburg satellite tiles at the aircraft's exact pose, and native \u003ccode>usher\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> process every frame.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-1-build-for-pc-simulation-prepare-the-ground-imagery", "title": "Step 1: Build for PC Simulation &amp; Prepare the Ground Imagery", "text": "\u003cp>For this local NPS flight, no physical camera or camera SDK is needed. Run this complete command from your Sensoraia checkout:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cd ~/develop/sensoraia\nmake -j4 usher soda loomcam CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\u003c/code> is an option on the \u003ccode>make\u003c/code> command, not a Paparazzi setting or a flight-time switch. It leaves out physical-camera capture support but keeps LOOM's saved-photo processing. It does not turn off the simulated camera: USHER renders new photographs from cached ground imagery at the simulated aircraft's current position and attitude, then passes those saved photographs to LOOM. Keep this option on subsequent \u003ccode>make ... loomcam\u003c/code> commands when rebuilding for PC-only simulation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Before flying, prepare the aerial imagery:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>While online, open PprzGCS, select aerial/satellite imagery, and display    the Valkenburg demo route at zoom 18. Pan over the whole outbound and    return route, plus the surrounding ground visible to the downward camera,    so those tiles are cached.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Confirm that the cache contains zoom-18 JPEGs:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>find ~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE/18 -name '*.jpeg' | head\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The default cache is \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map\u003c/code>. Confirming that tiles exist does not prove every camera footprint is covered; missing tiles can still reject a photo during flight, so cache more of the surrounding area if that happens. The Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS session uses this cache automatically; do not start another USHER manually. It does not download imagery during flight or substitute a fixed test picture when tiles are missing. The pictures show the imagery currently stored in your cache, not a live satellite feed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If your cache is elsewhere, add \u003ccode>--mock-map-cache /absolute/path/to/map\u003c/code> to the \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> command line in Paparazzi Center's Session tab. See MIMIC: prepare the map cache for more about the simulated camera.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Only if you also need a physical camera connected to this PC, use the camera-enabled build instead:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make imx500-native-sdk\nmake -j4 usher soda loomcam CAMERA_CAPTURE=1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>That alternative adds hardware capture support; it does not select the simulation's ground imagery. MORA deployment is covered separately below.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-2-install-sensoraia-links-into-paparazzi", "title": "Step 2: Install Sensoraia Links Into Paparazzi", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root (with Paparazzi installed at \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This links the Sensoraia flight plans, demo airframes, and unified control panel into \u003ccode>~/paparazzi/conf/\u003c/code> (see the Paparazzi setup walkthrough).\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-3-build-start-the-session-in-paparazzi-center", "title": "Step 3: Build &amp; Start the Session in Paparazzi Center", "text": "\u003cp>Choose the camera profile first; Center passes its environment to the session-managed USHER.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Launch Paparazzi Center from your Paparazzi directory:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi\n./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>(If a MORA FTDI cable is plugged into your computer and you want a purely PC-only flight, start Center with \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE=\"\" ./paparazzi\u003c/code>. Without that override, Sensoraia's cable-first detection automatically routes the session to USHER on MORA.)\u003c/p> \u003cp>In Paparazzi Center:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Ensure configuration \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code> and    control panel \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> are    selected.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Select aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LOOMCAM\u003c/code> (or your own fixed-wing aircraft using    \u003ccode>flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_loom_cam_demo.xml\u003c/code>), select target    \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code>, and click Build.\u003c/li> \u003cli>In the Session tab, select \u003ccode>Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS\u003c/code> and click    Execute.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-4-fly-outbound-return-land-in-gcs-classic", "title": "Step 4: Fly Outbound, Return &amp; Land in \u003ccode>GCS Classic\u003c/code>", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Wait for GPS: In \u003ccode>GCS Classic\u003c/code>, wait a few seconds for live telemetry and    a valid 3D GPS fix in Holding point.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Click \u003ccode>Takeoff\u003c/code>: The aircraft climbs to 100 m nominal altitude and    enters Start demo (\u003ccode>LOOM outbound\u003c/code>) automatically.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Watch the Outbound Leg (\u003ccode>OUT1\u003c/code> $\\rightarrow$ \u003ccode>OUT2\u003c/code>):    - Entering Session start sends camera \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> over the simulated UART.    - During Outbound, downward photographs are captured periodically and      hard-linked into \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/loom-reference/session-&lt;token&gt;/\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Watch the Return Leg (\u003ccode>Return phase\u003c/code> $\\rightarrow$ \u003ccode>Return\u003c/code>):    - Entering Return phase sends camera \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code> to switch USHER's native LOOM      worker from reference recording to return-phase evaluation.    - In Return, each saved photograph is evaluated by \u003ccode>bin/loomcam\u003c/code> and      correlated \u003ccode>USHER_CAM_POINT_UPDATE\u003c/code> messages are processed through      \u003ccode>cam_point_to_waypoint(USHER_CAMERA_AICAM, WP_T1)\u003c/code> while the aircraft      orbits before proceeding into the automatic Land sequence.    - Stop all session programs in Paparazzi Center after landing before starting      another run.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-5-inspect-your-saved-photos-reference-links", "title": "Step 5: Inspect Your Saved Photos &amp; Reference Links", "text": "\u003cp>After the flight, inspect USHER's native output folders:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>\u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code> \u2014 every captured JPEG stamped with EXIF altitude,   roll, pitch, and magnetometer heading. With emulation enabled,   \u003ccode>o000006.jpg\u003c/code> is the retained clean view and \u003ccode>o000006.lens.jpg\u003c/code> is the   camera-profile view actually submitted to LOOM.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/loom-reference/session-&lt;token&gt;/\u003c/code> \u2014 the immutable   outbound reference photographs hard-linked during the \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> leg. These   link the emulated JPEGs when a profile is selected, not the clean views.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>(Because the bundled \u003ccode>nn_loom_v001.rpk\u003c/code> is a feature-only assignment rather than a trained homing network, native \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> reports uncalibrated/invalid return directions unless configured with a compatible ONNX homing assignment or \u003ccode>return_vector_frame=simulated\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code>, leaving \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> safely unchanged rather than inventing a direction.)\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#select-the-simulated-camera-profile", "title": "Select the Simulated Camera Profile", "text": "\u003cp>The local Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS session defaults to \u003ccode>resources/camera_lens_aicam.json\u003c/code>: nominal AIcam field of view, Brown distortion, 2 ms exposure, rolling shutter, motion blur, chromatic aberration, and configured vibration, residual shading, optical softness, sensor noise and luma sharpening. Output matches USHER AIcam's 2028x1520 quality-90 JPEG mode. This is an illustrative starting point, not a measured calibration of your flight camera.\u003c/p> Selection Set before starting Center Result Default AIcam No override Nominal AIcam profile Your camera \u003ccode>LOOM_CAMERA_PROFILE=/absolute/path/flight-camera.json\u003c/code> Validated custom profile No emulator \u003ccode>LOOM_CAMERA_PROFILE=none\u003c/code> Original clean pinhole JPEG, no extra render \u003cp>For example, prepare your own profile from the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cp resources/camera_lens_aicam.json \"$HOME/loom-flight-camera.json\"\n# Edit that copy, then validate it:\n./bin/camera_lens --validate-profile \"$HOME/loom-flight-camera.json\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>In Paparazzi Center's Session tab (with Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS stopped), update \u003ccode>--mock-lens resources/camera_lens_aicam.json\u003c/code> on the \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> command line to \u003ccode>--mock-lens /home/you/loom-flight-camera.json\u003c/code> (or remove \u003ccode>--mock-lens resources/camera_lens_aicam.json\u003c/code> for a clean pinhole baseline) and click the play button (or Session -&gt; Save to keep your choice). Relative profile paths resolve from the Sensoraia root because \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> enters its own directory at startup. Keep the profile unchanged for the whole sortie; after editing it, start a fresh session with fresh references. This only affects PC map simulation; a MORA session uses its real camera, without synthetic effects.\u003c/p> \u003cp>When managing USHER yourself instead of using Center:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --local --test --mock-map --loomcam \\\n  --mock-lens \"$HOME/loom-flight-camera.json\" --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Omit \u003ccode>--mock-lens\u003c/code> to disable emulation in a manually managed USHER. Add \u003ccode>--mock-map-cache /absolute/path/to/map\u003c/code> for a non-default cache. Never start this alongside the session-managed peer.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#match-the-camera-that-produced-your-training-photos", "title": "Match the Camera That Produced Your Training Photos", "text": "\u003cp>Start with measured parameters for the saved JPEG mode, not just the sensor's advertised maximum resolution:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Set \u003ccode>horizontal_fov_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vertical_fov_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>model\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>distortion\u003c/code>   from calibration in that mode. Brown coefficients are \u003ccode>[k1,k2,p1,p2,k3]\u003c/code>;   fisheye uses \u003ccode>[k1,k2,k3,k4]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>diagonal_fov_deg\u003c/code>. Use   OpenCV's calibration conventions.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Set \u003ccode>output_width\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>output_height\u003c/code> together to match the real JPEG   dimensions (for example 2028 and 1520, if that is your capture mode).   Omitted or paired \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> values preserve MIMIC's clean-view dimensions.   Rendering uses the requested output grid directly, before ONNX's independent   model-input resize. Do not downsize here just to conceal inference cost.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Match exposure duration (\u003ccode>exposure_s\u003c/code>), shutter/readout, JPEG quality,   and realistic speed/height. Keep \u003ccode>height_override_m=null\u003c/code> so the photo's   flight AGL is authoritative. \u003ccode>f_number\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>focal_length_mm\u003c/code> record lens   metadata; they do not simulate depth of field.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Match RGB/BGR, scaling, and resize separately in the   ONNX assignment. A lens profile is not a   neural model configuration, nor a PANGEA camera-calibration INI.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Compare batches with \u003ccode>./bin/camera_lens --measure /data/flight/*.jpg\u003c/code>. Follow Calibrate Against Real Captures to compare noise, detail, shading, brightness and JPEG statistics, tune the profile and verify a held-out real flight. The supplied profile has not been calibrated against real AIcam flight captures.\u003c/p> \u003cp>See the complete profile settings and realism limits for units, ranges, coefficient conventions, performance controls, and calibration guidance. The Raspberry Pi AI Camera product information describes the nominal hardware, not your installed lens calibration.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#what-reaches-the-neural-network", "title": "What Reaches the Neural Network", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cached imagery + capture pose -&gt; clean JPEG + flight EXIF\n                             -&gt; camera_lens(profile, same pose/cache)\n                             -&gt; saved .lens.jpg + profile/hash provenance\n                             -&gt; outbound reference link OR return ONNX input\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>USHER runs the existing native renderer once, before the saved-image callback. LOOM archives and reads that exact derivative; SODA receives the same path without applying a second lens pass. Pose, GPS, AGL, and shot identity remain attached. The derivative's EXIF records the effective profile, its SHA-256, shutter/exposure, rendering assumptions, and source geometry. The clean file remains available for paired comparisons. Reference filenames still end in \u003ccode>.jpg\u003c/code>, but their inode is the derivative's inode.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A missing tile, invalid profile, busy renderer, timeout (90 s maximum), or write failure rejects that simulated observation: no clean-image fallback, no stale derivative reuse, no reference admission or new LOOM result. Read \u003ccode>CAMERA_LENS\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>IMAGE_LENS\u003c/code> diagnostics and USHER's \u003ccode>lens-emulated analysis input\u003c/code> line. The Flight Controller remains in charge.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The renderer reprojects cached terrain rather than stretching only the original photo, so a wider profile needs a wider cached region. It uses bounded strips, a bounded tile cache, and one renderer job at a time; it does not add OpenCV to USHER or LOOM. Profile settings \u003ccode>samples\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>threads\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>memory_budget_mb\u003c/code>, and output resolution control cost. Capture workers wait for the derivative, so large images/many samples can miss the flight plan's 3-second photo cadence. Check the renderer's \u003ccode>elapsed_s\u003c/code> and USHER's timing at your real resolution; the timeout is a safety ceiling, not a real-time throughput promise. Lower odd \u003ccode>samples\u003c/code> only after comparing blur convergence. See resource guidance. Adaptive exposure sampling and verified ray-grid interpolation keep the full-effect default near 0.60 s/photo on the development PC; this is not a whole-pipeline or onboard timing guarantee.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Satellite imagery, flat terrain, configured vibration/rates, and relative sRGB exposure cannot establish real-flight neural accuracy. Saved-image noise/shading/sharpening are approximations, not a reproduction of the Bayer sensor or complete camera ISP. Automatic exposure/white balance, physical defocus, and atmospheric or 3D-scene effects are not reproduced. Compare held-out real-flight JPEGs with simulation before calling a profile representative. The simulated homeward vector mode remains only an integration aid, even with lens emulation enabled.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#choose-the-model-vector-frame-loomcam_settingsini", "title": "Choose the Model &amp; Vector Frame (\u003ccode>loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code>)", "text": "\u003cp>Configure \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>modules/loom_cam/loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code> (or your own copy). ONNX requires a compatible \u003ccode>.onnx\u003c/code> graph and descriptor; IMX500 uses a compatible \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code>. Paths are resolved relative to the settings file. The bundled \u003ccode>models/nn_loom_v001.rpk\u003c/code> is a feature-only assignment; its presence does not establish trained homing behavior.\u003c/p> \u003ccode>return_vector_frame\u003c/code> How \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> Interprets the Output \u003ccode>none\u003c/code> Extracts features and logs observations only; never moves \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> \u003ccode>north_east\u003c/code> First output component is North, second is East \u003ccode>body_forward_right\u003c/code> First component is aircraft forward, second is right; rotated into Earth \u003ccode>(North, East)\u003c/code> using the photo's EXIF IMU roll/pitch and Magneto heading \u003ccode>simulated\u003c/code> Runs the real \u003ccode>.onnx\u003c/code> graph, then synthesizes a slightly noisy body-frame homeward vector seeded by the photo's neural features and projects it through the 3D IMU/Magneto rotation (ideal for testing before a model is trained) \u003cp>A two-value vector has no measured confidence estimate; a three-value output supplies \u003ccode>(vector_0, vector_1, confidence)\u003c/code>. Vector magnitude in \u003ccode>0..1\u003c/code> scales the lookahead step down as the aircraft approaches the target spot, while magnitudes at or above \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> step by the full \u003ccode>return_lookahead_m\u003c/code> ahead of the aircraft. Photos taken beyond \u003ccode>return_max_tilt_deg\u003c/code> (roll or pitch) are rejected before steering. Match the declared RGB/BGR order, scaling, resize, and nearest-neighbor interpolation to your training pipeline; see ONNX model selection.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Example \u003ccode>loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code> for a compatible ONNX assignment:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\ninput_source=live\nmodel=models/return.onnx\nmodel_descriptor=models/return.ini\nonnx_threads=1\ninference_timeout_ms=2000\nreturn_vector_frame=body_forward_right\nreturn_home_radius_m=60\nreturn_lookahead_m=50\nreturn_max_step_m=5\nreturn_min_confidence=0.6\nreturn_max_tilt_deg=25\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Live operation steps from each photograph's aligned pose\u2014or from hardware-bound camera frames using barometer height and magnetometer heading when GPS alignment is unavailable. Optional \u003ccode>return_home_latitude\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>return_home_longitude\u003c/code> add destination hemisphere and arrival bounds when a fixed coordinate is known.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#prepare-fly-a-real-flight-with-loomcam-on-mora", "title": "Prepare &amp; Fly a Real Flight with LOOMcam on MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Taking LOOMcam into the air on MORA (Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W) with the Raspberry Pi AI Camera (IMX500) uses the exact same native C11/C++17 binary pipeline\u2014cross-compiled on your PC and deployed cleanly over SSH.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-1-mount-the-camera-connect-the-flight-controller-uart", "title": "Step 1: Mount the Camera &amp; Connect the Flight Controller UART", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Mount the Raspberry Pi AI Camera looking straight down (nadir) with    an unobstructed view beneath the fuselage.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Connect the Flight Controller UART to MORA (\u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code> at \u003ccode>115200\u003c/code> baud,    3.3 V UART logic, crossed \u003ccode>TX\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>RX\u003c/code>, and common ground).\u003c/li> \u003cli>In your aircraft's airframe XML, ensure \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart\u003c/code> has    \u003ccode>DIGITAL_CAM_UART_POSE_STREAM=1\u003c/code> enabled so MORA receives live Baro    altitude, IMU roll/pitch, and Magneto heading with every trigger.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-2-prepare-your-loomcam_settingsini", "title": "Step 2: Prepare Your \u003ccode>loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code>", "text": "\u003cp>Copy \u003ccode>modules/loom_cam/loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code> to a flight configuration file and point \u003ccode>model\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>model_descriptor\u003c/code> to your trained return network.\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Keep \u003ccode>return_vector_frame=none\u003c/code> for initial data-collection flights where you   only want to record outbound/return imagery and verify inference timing.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Switch to \u003ccode>return_vector_frame=body_forward_right\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>north_east\u003c/code>) only   after validating your model on representative aerial views from your flying   site.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-3-select-cameraloomcam-for-mora", "title": "Step 3: Select \u003ccode>CAMERA=loomcam\u003c/code> For MORA", "text": "\u003cp>AIcam, Spottercam, and LOOMcam are alternative owners of the same physical IMX500 camera (Spottercam is the default when \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> is left unchanged). Set \u003ccode>CAMERA = loomcam\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> at the Sensoraia root, or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=loomcam\u003c/code> directly when packaging and deploying.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-4-package-deploy-to-mora", "title": "Step 4: Package &amp; Deploy to MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Use the standard MORA deployment workflow with \u003ccode>CAMERA=loomcam\u003c/code> and pass your settings file via \u003ccode>MORA_LOOMCAM_SETTINGS\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 mora-deploy \\\n  CAMERA=loomcam \\\n  MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local \\\n  MORA_LOOMCAM_SETTINGS=/absolute/path/loomcam_settings.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This cross-compiles the ARM64 binaries on your PC, configures \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> to start \u003ccode>usher --loomcam\u003c/code>, installs the graph, descriptor, and settings under \u003ccode>share/loomcam/\u003c/code> on MORA, and restarts \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code>. (To build the package without contacting MORA, run \u003ccode>make -j32 mora-package CAMERA=loomcam MORA_LOOMCAM_SETTINGS=/absolute/path/loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code>.)\u003c/p> \u003cp>Confirm the service is running on MORA:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service'\nssh air@theatre.local 'journalctl -u usher.service -n 40 --no-pager'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-5-optional-ftdi-desk-flight-before-going-to-the-field", "title": "Step 5: Optional FTDI Desk Flight Before Going to the Field", "text": "\u003cp>Before flying the aircraft, you can connect your laptop's FTDI cable to MORA (5 V power, common ground, crossed 3.3 V \u003ccode>TX\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>RX\u003c/code>; see the FTDI desk-test guide) and start the Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS session in Paparazzi Center. With the cable attached, the simulator automatically uses your real MORA board (\u003ccode>execution: MORA\u003c/code>) instead of a local PC peer.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#step-6-what-happens-during-the-real-flight", "title": "Step 6: What Happens During the Real Flight", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Takeoff &amp; Standby: Take off normally and verify stable telemetry, GPS,    barometer altitude, and magnetometer heading in \u003ccode>pprzgcs\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Outbound Leg (\u003ccode>START\u003c/code>): Entering the outbound block sends \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> to    USHER on MORA. Native USHER creates a fresh, exclusive    \u003ccode>references/session-&lt;token&gt;/\u003c/code> directory for the sortie and links up to 256    downward reference photographs with full EXIF pose tags.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Return Leg (\u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code>): Entering the return block sends \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code>, locking the    outbound reference set for the sortie while periodic captures continue. Each    level return photo (\u003ccode>&lt;= 25\u00b0\u003c/code> tilt) is evaluated in RAM by \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> without    writing extra reference copies to the SD card.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Approaching \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> &amp; Landing: Accepted return vectors step \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code>    toward the return spot, bounded within 1200 m and gated inside    \u003ccode>return_home_radius_m\u003c/code> (60 m). Once at the return circle, the aircraft    orbits \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> calmly at 80 m radius until you command Land.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#keep-references-separate-troubleshoot", "title": "Keep References Separate &amp; Troubleshoot", "text": "\u003cp>Use a clean reference directory for each sortie, and never mix different model feature spaces or edit hard-linked originals.\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Per-sortie reference caps: Each \u003ccode>START\u003c/code> creates a new \u003ccode>session-&lt;token&gt;\u003c/code>   directory under \u003ccode>references/\u003c/code>, capped at 256 hard links per session and   64 top-level sessions. Once at the 256-photo cap, photos are still   captured and evaluated normally, with no further reference links added.   Existing sorties are never silently deleted; archive or remove old sessions   deliberately before storage runs low.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Strict startup and confirmation: Missing, feature-only, or incompatible   model assignments are rejected at startup. Rejected vectors never move \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code>.   Missing waypoint confirmation does not commit a proposed target, and its   confirmation channel enforces a four-second deadline.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul>"}, {"location": "loom-cam.html#if-return-updates-stop", "title": "If Return Updates Stop", "text": "What You See Why It Happens What to Do A shot during a turn produces no \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> move Roll or pitch exceeded \u003ccode>return_max_tilt_deg\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>25\u00b0\u003c/code>) Normal! Wait for the aircraft to roll wings-level on the straight leg \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> stops updating near \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> Aircraft is inside \u003ccode>return_home_radius_m\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>60 m\u003c/code>) Normal! \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> holds at \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> so the aircraft can fly a steady 80 m standby orbit No \u003ccode>T1\u003c/code> updates on straight, level legs \u003ccode>return_vector_frame=none\u003c/code>, confidence below threshold, or model/descriptor mismatch Read the helper or journal diagnostic; check \u003ccode>loomcam_settings.ini\u003c/code> and model paths Capture rejected with \u003ccode>--mock-map\u003c/code> Missing zoom-18 tile in the local \u003ccode>PprzGCS\u003c/code> cache Pan over the missing area at zoom 18 in \u003ccode>PprzGCS\u003c/code> so \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE/18\u003c/code> is populated \u003cp>When updates stop for any reason, the Flight Controller retains its existing target and normal flight-plan behavior\u2014the absence of a visual update is never treated as a new fix. Use the mission's normal Standby or Land controls to manage the flight.\u003c/p> \u003cp>PC simulation demonstrates end-to-end integration, not visual homing accuracy. Before relying on a model onboard, validate it on independent representative views, check camera calibration and timing, and verify MORA's combined memory and CPU headroom.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html", "title": "Calibrate Your Thermal Camera", "text": "\u003cp>A hotspot's map position depends on more than the camera's GPS tag. Measure the lens, measure how the camera is mounted, then check the combination on independent targets. Keep temperature verification as a separate task.\u003c/p> \u003cp>First thermal capture | Calibration terms\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#1-know-what-you-are-calibrating", "title": "1. Know What You Are Calibrating", "text": "Part What You Measure Lens/intrinsics How a native image pixel corresponds to a camera direction Mounting/extrinsics How that direction relates to aircraft axes Position and timing Where the camera was during the observation Temperature response How measured thermal values compare with independent references \u003cp>Changing resolution, focus, lens or bracket can invalidate the corresponding calibration. A fitted file is not an independent field-accuracy measurement.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#2-gather-ordinary-materials", "title": "2. Gather Ordinary Materials", "text": "\u003cp>Use a flat rigid board, materials that give clear thermal contrast, measured square spacing, a tape measure, a square and a level. You also need a laptop for fitting and separate folders for fitting and checking photographs.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Remove the propeller before working around the installed aircraft. Keep the camera in its final bracket for the mounting measurement.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#3-make-a-target-the-camera-can-see", "title": "3. Make A Target The Camera Can See", "text": "\u003cp>Test a small patch first. Ordinary printed black and white may look nearly identical in thermal imagery. Contrasting surface materials/emissivity and reflections can make the grid visible; inspect the actual camera image before constructing a large target. Avoid glare, uneven heating and warped surfaces.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#make-a-small-trial-patch", "title": "Make A Small Trial Patch", "text": "\u003cp>Put a matte tape square beside a smooth aluminium-foil-tape square on one flat backing. Look at their boundary with the actual camera from the intended distance and a modest tilt. Gentle, uniform warmth behind the board can help; keep liquids and heaters clear of electronics and follow their instructions. Do not use flames, boiling water or a heat gun to obtain contrast.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Foil reflects the thermal surroundings. Move your body and other warm objects away from the reflected view; the foil's apparent brightness is not a temperature reference. Continue only when edges remain sharp at several angles. Wrinkled foil, thick overlaps and a bowed board are reasons to improve the patch before making a full grid.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>The commands below use 7 by 6 internal corners: that means 8 by 7 squares. They also use an example 50 mm square pitch. Measure your real pitch and substitute it; the fitter cannot know that a handmade target is slightly different from its intended dimensions.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Add a uniform border at least half a square wide. Measure the finished grid pitch across several squares in both directions and divide by their count: five pitches spanning 250 mm means 50 mm per square. Measure grid-line to grid-line, not the remaining exposed foil width. Rebuild a crooked grid or one with unequal horizontal/vertical pitch rather than averaging the error.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Choose a size that fills useful parts of the image while remaining sharp. If corners are not visible or stable, fix the target/contrast before taking a large collection of photos.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Keep the intended flight focus. If a small indoor board is blurred, use a larger board farther away instead of refocusing just for calibration. Roughly 10-20 pixels per square is a useful starting size; inspect the native image at 100% and retain the complete grid and border in each fitting view.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#4-freeze-the-camera-setup", "title": "4. Freeze The Camera Setup", "text": "\u003cp>Keep native dimensions, focus and optics fixed. Save original thermal JPEGs, not resized screenshots, rotated exports or annotated previews. Do not change the bracket between mounting measurement and use.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Record the camera serial, mode, dimensions, lens/focus and relevant gain state. A gain label you type is a record of your intention, not hardware readback.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a standalone capture with USHER and other camera owners stopped, use a new filename on the camera computer:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>mkdir -p calibration/photos\nbin/lwircam calibration/photos/view01.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Take the next view under a new name. Copy originals to the laptop without resizing or re-encoding them; copy matching raw companions too if recorded. Do not use mock imagery or display-rotated exports for the real lens fit.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#5-take-a-useful-set-of-photos", "title": "5. Take A Useful Set Of Photos", "text": "\u003cp>Collect at least 15 usable fitting views with varied tilt, position, distance and in-plane rotation. Let corners visit the image edges as well as the centre. Keep the full grid visible, flat and sharp.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Set aside 5-8 genuinely new views for checking. Do not fit with those same images or delete difficult views merely to improve a score. Replace an image only when you can identify its problem, and retain your reason.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#6-fit-the-lens-on-the-laptop", "title": "6. Fit The Lens On The Laptop", "text": "\u003cp>The native calibration application runs on the development PC. Build it from the Sensoraia root; the project reuses its static OpenCV calibration modules and bundled JPEG codec, so no separate calibration package is needed:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j\"$(nproc)\" lwir_calibrate\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Keep fitting photos in \u003ccode>calibration/photos\u003c/code>, independent checks in \u003ccode>calibration/check\u003c/code> and mounting views in \u003ccode>calibration/mount\u003c/code>. Then run:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwir_calibrate lens \\\n  --images calibration/photos --check calibration/check \\\n  --columns 7 --rows 6 --square-mm 50 \\\n  --output calibration/tiny1c-lens.yml\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The normal ARM64 build also creates a MORA-compatible copy, included in the release package for optional manual calibration work. It is not started by the USHER service, and packaging does not upload or activate it:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j\"$(nproc)\" arm64\nbin/lwir_calibrate-arm64 --help\nmake -j\"$(nproc)\" mora-package MORA_INSTALL_DIR=/home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The package is assembled on the development PC; deployment is a separate command. The utility remains a standalone tool and is not part of USHER's startup command. Use your actual board dimensions and measured pitch. A new output name is required for a separate attempt. Inspect the corner overlays, detected/skipped file list and independent-check errors, not only the summary number.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Reprojection error is in pixels, not metres of GPS error. Correct-looking overlays and comparable independent checks are necessary; no universal RMS threshold guarantees geographic accuracy. A good centre-only fit does not validate the image edges.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#7-measure-the-mounting-without-guessing", "title": "7. Measure The Mounting Without Guessing", "text": "\u003cp>Secure the aircraft over a horizontal target, using its defined body-axis reference. A convenient fuselage surface may not be that reference. Align board columns with aircraft right and rows toward the tail. Resolve any disagreement between the FC's level reference and the physical datum first.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>With the camera in its final bracket:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwir_calibrate mount \\\n  --image calibration/mount/mount01.jpg \\\n  --lens calibration/tiny1c-lens.yml \\\n  --columns 7 --rows 6 --square-mm 50 \\\n  --output calibration/tiny1c-mounted.yml\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Inspect the numbered corners: 0 is the near-nose, aircraft-left inner corner; 1 is next toward aircraft right; 7 starts the next row toward the tail for this seven-column board. A checkerboard can be detected in the opposite order. Use \u003ccode>--reverse-corners\u003c/code> only for the identified 180-degree reversal, with a new output name. Do not guess signs or rotations until a result looks plausible.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#8-check-distances-before-checking-gps", "title": "8. Check Distances Before Checking GPS", "text": "\u003cp>Use target positions and distances not used in fitting. Compare predicted directions/ground offsets with measured physical references across the image, including its edges. Recheck with a separately collected set.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a local geometry check, secure the aircraft over a level patch with propulsion disconnected. Measure camera height and use a plumb line to mark its ground projection. Put independently measured targets forward, right, left and near the usable image edges. Check directions before comparing small distance errors, then repeat with known changed heading/attitude. Use the actual stationary pose/height rather than old flight metadata.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Checkerboard fitting does not require hotspot detection. A checkerboard can correctly produce zero detected hotspots; do not heat a household target dangerously to force a detection. End-to-end hot-target checks need a suitable controlled target. Tape measurements check relative geometry, while absolute GPS error needs an independently surveyed reference and its uncertainty.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>A systematic offset suggests mounting/reference error; growing edge error suggests lens or mode mismatch. Before blaming GPS, check the image axes, target geometry, ground plane and observation timing.\u003c/p> Error Pattern Investigate First Grows toward image edges Lens fit, crop or wrong image dimensions Similar offset that rotates with heading Mounting alignment Changes with speed/turn direction Observation timing or motion blur Slowly drifting displacement of every target Position reference or GNSS bias Changes over sloping terrain AGL and the flat-ground assumption \u003cp>These are clues, not unique diagnoses; change one thing at a time.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#9-install-the-profile-deliberately", "title": "9. Install The Profile Deliberately", "text": "\u003cp>Retain the original fit and reports. Set \u003ccode>verified: 1\u003c/code> only after reviewing the lens, corner ordering, mounting and independent geometry checks. It is a review gate, not certification of GPS or field accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The runtime profile uses original native dimensions, fitted \u003ccode>fx/fy/cx/cy\u003c/code>, distortion coefficients and the camera-to-body rotation. Do not substitute a cropped display matrix or pre-undistort the image: the runtime projects the calibrated ray itself.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Copy the reviewed profile to MORA and select it in the actual USHER launch configuration, for example:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>--lwir-calibration /home/air/sensoraia/calibration/tiny1c-mounted.yml\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use your installed account/path. Changing a command in an SSH terminal does not update an already running service. Apply configuration changes through the deployment procedure.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To inspect a copy of an existing flight-tagged photo:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --geolocate calibration/check-shot.jpg \\\n  --calibration calibration/tiny1c-mounted.yml\nexiftool -b -UserComment calibration/check-shot.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The geolocation command updates the JPEG, so work on a copy. Preserve any raw companion too. Missing/unreviewed calibration or unsuitable pose evidence must not be treated as a valid target position. ExifTool is optional inspection software, not a runtime requirement.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#10-keep-temperature-checks-separate", "title": "10. Keep Temperature Checks Separate", "text": "\u003cp>Lens fitting does not calibrate temperature. Begin by checking the camera's existing temperature response rather than replacing factory corrections without evidence.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use stable references with known uncertainty and suitable surfaces. Allow camera and references to reach a stable condition, record gain/range and repeat measurements at several temperatures. Reflections and emissivity matter: a shiny surface can show reflected surroundings rather than its own temperature.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A contact probe and matte comparison patch can help triage a problem, but are not automatically a traceable absolute-temperature reference. For such claims use suitable calibrated references and include their uncertainty.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If measured corrections are necessary, fit only within the verified camera, gain and range, then test independent intermediate/hold-out references. Keep raw observations and prior results. Do not divide Celsius by emissivity or extrapolate a profile beyond its support range.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#11-check-the-whole-flight-measurement", "title": "11. Check The Whole Flight Measurement", "text": "\u003cp>Onboard geolocation also depends on aircraft position, real AGL, attitude, mounting, exposure timing and terrain. Compare independent ground targets under representative conditions before relying on a metre-level claim. Simulation, successful fitting and printed decimal places cannot replace that check.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#12-quick-troubleshooting", "title": "12. Quick Troubleshooting", "text": "Symptom Check Few detected boards Thermal contrast, sharpness, complete pattern and correct corner counts Large edge errors Edge coverage, focus, dimensions and lens model Mount rotated/reversed Physical board axes and numbered corner order Good fit, poor ground locations Mounting, timing, height/reference system and terrain Good geometry, poor temperatures Independent thermal references, gain, emissivity and reflections"}, {"location": "lwir-calibration.html#13-save-a-reusable-calibration-pack", "title": "13. Save A Reusable Calibration Pack", "text": "\u003cp>Keep originals, fitting/check sets, board dimensions, overlays, reports, reviewed profiles, camera/mode identity and independent reference results together. If you change the optics or installation, retain the old pack and make a new one rather than silently reusing it.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html", "title": "LWIRcam: Capture Thermal Images", "text": "\u003cp>LWIRcam saves images from supported USB thermal cameras. With supported Tiny1C data it also retains temperature samples and can measure selected regions. Start with one clean capture before changing rendering or analysis.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Thermal terms | Calibrate your camera\u003c/p> Camera Available Data Tiny1C combined mode 256 x 192 display image and a separate 256 x 192 temperature plane TDK IRcamera \u003ccode>5474:5411\u003c/code> 640 x 512 display image; no temperature plane in this supported mode"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#build-on-the-pc", "title": "Build On The PC", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 lwircam\nbin/lwircam --features\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For an ARM64 release use the installation workflow. \u003ccode>--features\u003c/code> reports the capture backend (\u003ccode>capture=v4l2\u003c/code>, native Linux V4L2 with the kernel \u003ccode>uvcvideo\u003c/code> driver) and which optional analysis/mock features are built. The development PC supplies Linux headers; no compiler or development packages are needed on MORA. Hotspot geolocation and ONNX classification need analysis enabled consistently for USHER and its worker, for example \u003ccode>LWIR_ENABLE_ANALYSIS=1\u003c/code> when building the selected release. Ordinary image/temperature capture does not require it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To deploy the full Sensoraia suite on MORA with LWIRcam selected as the active service, set \u003ccode>CAMERA = lwircam\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> or run:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy CAMERA=lwircam\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Or, to deploy a minimal LWIR-only package (excluding the other camera workers and models):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy MORA_PROFILE=lwircam\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Both configure \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> to start \u003ccode>usher --lwircam\u003c/code>. For an NPS flight using the physical thermal camera and UART, follow the laptop-to-MORA desk test with aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LWIRCAM\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>USHER_CAMERA_MASK_LWIR\u003c/code>). The connected FTDI cable selects MORA automatically. The capture-only build does not return hotspot navigation points.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#arm64-cpu-profiles", "title": "ARM64 CPU Profiles", "text": "\u003cp>The normal ARM64 build targets Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W. Select the intended CPU when cross-compiling on the development PC:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make lwircam-arm64 ARM64_BOARD=pi-zero2w\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003ccode>ARM64_BOARD\u003c/code> CPU Tuning \u003ccode>pi-zero2w\u003c/code> Cortex-A53; default for MORA \u003ccode>radxa-zero3w\u003c/code> Cortex-A55 \u003ccode>orange-pi5b\u003c/code> Cortex-A76/A55 combination \u003ccode>generic\u003c/code> Portable ARMv8-A baseline \u003cp>Choose one profile for the board that will run the binary. Tuned builds are not interchangeable with every ARM64 processor. Rendering and temperature statistics already use native NEON fast paths where supported. CPU tuning does not activate a GPU, hardware JPEG encoder or NPU, and does not qualify a board's OS image or camera drivers. Use a matching target sysroot when required and measure on the actual board. Continue to build on the PC and deploy binaries, not compilers or development packages, to MORA.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Tiny1C's combined stream supplies 25 independent frames per second in the tested mode. Faster processing cannot create additional camera frames. Measure camera wait, rendering, JPEG encoding, metadata and durable storage separately; using more threads is not automatically faster for a 256x192 image. Keep resource headroom for the whole Pi Zero 2 W's shared 512 MB, not only the capture worker.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#native-v4l2-capture", "title": "Native V4L2 Capture", "text": "\u003cp>Native capture opens only supported USB camera identities and image-capture endpoints, not arbitrary video or metadata devices. Automatic discovery checks \u003ccode>/dev/video0\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>/dev/video63\u003c/code> and requires exactly one supported image endpoint. Multiple cameras are an error, never an arbitrary first-device choice. Select the actual serial or an actual stable \u003ccode>/dev/v4l/by-id/\u003c/code> image-device link:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --device /dev/v4l/by-id/ACTUAL-CAMERA-IMAGE-LINK \\\n  --native-raw --stats /absolute/path/native-thermal.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>An explicit path can also select a higher-numbered device. If both path and serial are provided, they must identify the same camera. The config key \u003ccode>device=/dev/v4l/by-id/ACTUAL-CAMERA-IMAGE-LINK\u003c/code> selects the worker device when USHER is given that LWIR config. Device selection is rejected for offline replay.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The driver requires exact YUYV dimensions, tightly packed rows and full frame length. Tiny1C image-only 256x192 negotiation, padded rows, partial/error frames, duplicate/backward sequences and stale qualified monotonic buffers cannot become successful captures. It owns two to four bounded MMAP buffers, requeues each dequeued frame before success and releases partial startup resources on failure. Eight-frame qualification is only for the first startup of a newly seen USB camera instance. It is not repeated for later photos, a quiet interval, rejected frames, a sequence gap, reopen or a worker restart. A private volatile record under \u003ccode>/run/lock\u003c/code> remembers completion for the service user, keyed to the host boot and USB enumeration identity. The kernel does not expose a sensor power-cycle counter: a genuinely new USB enumeration is treated as new startup. An inaccessible startup record is an explicit error, never a reason to silently repeat the wait.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Later requests select the next usable fresh frame without rebuilding a frame count. Older queued frames are skipped, never returned as that request's photo. Missing, corrupt or neutral-fill frames may still delay capture until valid data arrives; that is not an artificial warmup delay. Optional scene/settling checks still apply when explicitly selected, but never add an eight-frame wait after startup. The configured warmup time also applies only to initial startup. Images are copied only when needed for settling or a selected capture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Polling is event-driven, with short cancellation intervals and monotonic deadlines; kernel/driver stalls cannot be preempted by a userspace deadline.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Fatal queue/protocol failures block further use of that session until it is closed and reopened. Cleanup retains any mapping the kernel failed to unmap, so a repeated close can release it; reopening cannot overwrite that ownership. Descriptor-close errors are not blindly retried, because Linux may already have released the descriptor number. These checks reduce long-running failure risks; they do not guarantee that a USB cable, camera or kernel driver cannot fail. Validate the intended installation with a sustained soak test before relying on unattended all-day operation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Real Tiny1C transitions can produce a full-sized neutral fill: every byte pair in both planes is \u003ccode>00 80\u003c/code>, displaying black while falsely converting to a uniform 238.85 C. Live capture rejects that exact whole-frame pattern and any entirely invalid temperature plane, and waits for good frames or the existing deadline. A real \u003ccode>0x8000\u003c/code> temperature in a useful frame is not rejected. Offline replay remains structural evidence, not fresh capture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The supplied service includes the \u003ccode>video\u003c/code> group and camera-specific video-node udev rules. The kernel \u003ccode>uvcvideo\u003c/code> driver must be bound, and other camera owners must be stopped; reconnect the device if another USB tool previously detached the kernel driver and the normal video nodes do not reappear. Permission, busy, unplug and driver errors are failures, not permission to use an old frame.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Native \u003ccode>--device-state\u003c/code> uses Linux USBFS control requests for the existing Tiny1C read-only mailbox protocol. It opens the matched USB node only for the snapshot, verifies the USB-instance identity, and closes it afterward without claiming interfaces or detaching \u003ccode>uvcvideo\u003c/code>. This requires read/write permission on the USB device node as well as normal video-node access. Image-only TDK still has no temperature/state support.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Host dequeue/arrival time and the kernel's monotonic buffer timestamp/flags are preserved separately. Neither is automatically qualified as exposure time, even when the kernel marks a timestamp as start-of-exposure. The kernel may overwrite buffer metadata during requeue; accepted-frame clock evidence is snapshotted before returning buffer ownership. Revalidate measured latency when changing USB topology or kernel drivers; previous lag measurements must not silently be reused for attitude-sensitive projection. Saved JPEG temperature bytes remain exact and native; the kernel timestamp is additional evidence, not calibration.\u003c/p> \u003cp>LWIRcam captures directly through Linux V4L2 and USBFS, with no external UVC or USB user-space library dependencies. Host fault tests and cross-compilation alone do not qualify a real camera: verify a fresh full-height JPEG, its complete thermal plane and an exact raw companion on the target.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#first-camera-jpeg", "title": "First Camera JPEG", "text": "\u003cp>Stop other owners of the camera, connect it and use a new filename:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam /absolute/path/first-thermal.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Open the JPEG and check focus, orientation and the expected scene. With multiple devices, choose the actual serial using \u003ccode>--serial SERIAL\u003c/code>. Permission errors need device-access setup, not repeated captures.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Native Tiny1C capture must report \u003ccode>native V4L2 YUYV 256x384 transport, 256x192 image and temperature planes\u003c/code>. The 384 transport rows contain the full 192-row image followed by the full 192-row temperature plane, not a cropped or resized sensor image. Its other advertised \u003ccode>256x192\u003c/code> UVC mode is image-only and must never be split into two 96-row planes. LWIRcam rejects that mode and non-native live Tiny1C dimensions. An unoriented JPEG without a legend is 256 x 192; its embedded temperature plane always remains 256 x 192. A raw combined companion is 196608 bytes.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For the TDK mode, preserve the native 640 x 512 dimensions. It is image-only: colourful pixels do not provide temperatures, statistics or thermal calibration.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#measurements-and-threshold-coverage", "title": "Measurements And Threshold Coverage", "text": "\u003cp>These examples require a supported Tiny1C temperature plane:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --stats /absolute/path/statistics.jpg\nbin/lwircam --roi 32 24 128 96 --thresholds 15 45 /absolute/path/region.jpg\nbin/lwircam --point 128 96 --average-3x3 /absolute/path/spot.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>ROI, point and line coordinates refer to the original sensor image: origin at the top left, X right and Y down. Check the actual native dimensions. Out-of-range selections are rejected rather than silently clipped.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Thresholds specify an inclusive temperature band for diagnostic coverage, not a command to change sensor gain. The printed min/max, averages and coverage describe the selected valid samples; they are not independent temperature calibration. Printed decimal places are not measured accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#display-orientation-and-acceleration", "title": "Display Orientation And Acceleration", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --rotate 90 --mirror /absolute/path/oriented.jpg\nbin/lwircam --window 15 45 --palette iron --legend /absolute/path/fixed-window.jpg\nbin/lwircam --percentiles 2 98 --palette white-hot /absolute/path/contrast.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Rotation is clockwise, followed by mirror/flip. These change the displayed view, not the raw temperature coordinates. A fixed window makes successive views easier to compare; percentiles emphasize the current scene's contrast. Neither creates additional sensor detail or changes preserved temperatures.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#adjust-image-contrast", "title": "Adjust Image Contrast", "text": "\u003cp>If the picture looks flat or grey, higher contrast can make small temperature differences easier to see. These software controls draw the JPEG from the temperature plane; they do not change camera gain or temperature calibration. There is no separate \u003ccode>--contrast\u003c/code> option. Use a new output filename for each capture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Start with automatic contrast:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --percentiles 2 98 /absolute/path/contrast.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This spreads the middle 96% of the scene's temperature samples from black to white. The coldest 2% appear black and the hottest 2% appear white. Try \u003ccode>--percentiles 5 95\u003c/code> for stronger contrast, with more extremes shown as solid black or white. Automatic contrast adapts to each picture, so the same temperature can have a different brightness in the next picture.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For USHER, add or update these settings in the LWIR config passed with \u003ccode>--lwir-config\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>percentile_low=2\npercentile_high=98\npalette=white-hot\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For consistent brightness between pictures, use a fixed temperature range instead:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --window 20 35 /absolute/path/fixed-contrast.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>With the default white-hot palette, 20 C becomes black and 35 C becomes white. Temperatures below or above that range also appear black or white. Choose a range that suits the scene. In a USHER config, use \u003ccode>window_low_c=20\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>window_high_c=35\u003c/code> instead of the two percentile settings. Use either automatic percentiles or a fixed window, not both in the same command or config.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The saved temperature samples stay unchanged, even where the picture becomes solid black or white. Higher contrast adds no sensor detail and does not make temperature readings more accurate. For Spottercam, keep the chosen rendering mode and settings consistent between model training and operation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#jpeg-quality-and-speed", "title": "JPEG Quality And Speed", "text": "\u003cp>Use the generic Camera Speed Test to measure saved-image throughput on the deployed board. Sensor frame rate, fastest single capture and sustained saved-image rate are distinct metrics.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Normal captures use JPEG quality 95 with 4:4:4 sampling. It is usually a useful balance between picture detail, file size and encoding work. Quality 100 is also lossy; it is not a way to preserve exact original image pixels. Keep the raw companion when those original bytes are needed.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Select quality 100 for comparison, or set another value from 1 to 100:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --jpeg-quality 100 /absolute/path/quality-comparison.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For USHER, put \u003ccode>jpeg_quality=95\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>jpeg_quality=100\u003c/code> in its LWIR config. The command-line option overrides the config for a standalone capture. The selected quality and 4:4:4 sampling are recorded in the JPEG's capture evidence. Existing zero-initialized library callers retain their quality-100 default; the capture worker explicitly selects 95.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The embedded temperature plane remains exact at every JPEG quality. Its 98304 bytes also limit total-file savings: only the displayed image becomes more compressed. Lower quality can soften small details, especially at this sensor resolution. Keep quality, rendering and palette consistent between Spottercam training and operation, and evaluate small-object detection before changing a deployed model's input policy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A paired development-PC test on the same recorded Tiny1C frame measured about 0.93 ms per JPEG at quality 100 and 0.73 ms at 95, excluding USB and storage. Total file size fell from about 124 kB to 111 kB while all temperature bytes remained identical. These are PC measurements, not Pi or other-board speed claims. File publication still includes synchronization and no-overwrite checks; those safeguards are not removed to inflate throughput. A separate live PC run with raw companions, statistics and durable writes enabled saved about 12.36 photos/s at quality 100 and 12.51 at 95. The encoder's speedup therefore must not be presented as the same percentage gain for the whole capture pipeline.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#preserve-raw-measurements", "title": "Preserve Raw Measurements", "text": "\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --native-raw /absolute/path/baseline.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The raw companion is \u003ccode>baseline.jpg.raw\u003c/code>. Keep it with the JPEG when you need the exact combined frame. The supported thermal JPEG also retains its temperature plane; a screenshot cannot replace it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For offline replay use \u003ccode>--raw\u003c/code> with the recorded dimensions and a new output. Replay exercises stored data, not a fresh camera exposure.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For example, for a recorded 256-by-192 Tiny1C combined frame:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --raw /absolute/path/baseline.jpg.raw --size 256 192 /absolute/path/replay.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#read-only-device-state", "title": "Read-Only Device State", "text": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>--device-state\u003c/code> requests supported Tiny1C state information alongside capture. The legacy mailbox reader was implemented, not just a stub; native capture now uses the same field parser with Linux \u003ccode>USBDEVFS_CONTROL\u003c/code>, not libusb. Transfers are limited to 64 bytes and 50 ms each within the 250 ms mailbox budget. Cancellation, short replies, device replacement, permission failures and transfer errors produce explicit statuses without invalidating the saved image. Firmware fields may legitimately be unsupported, busy, invalid or unavailable; successful host tests do not establish readback on every firmware. Unavailable fields remain unavailable. It does not write firmware settings, prove physical gain or exposure accuracy, or provide a complete correction history. The image-only TDK mode does not support this Tiny1C readback.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On 2026-10-30, native USBFS readback was exercised on a PC-attached Tiny1C reporting firmware \u003ccode>Tiny_V3.14_M\u003c/code>. The first snapshot returned 16 fields, including gain selection, firmware, shutter, temperatures, emissivity and image-processing settings. Twenty further state-enabled captures all passed exact raw/embedded-temperature comparison while retaining five process file descriptors and four video mappings. This is PC evidence, not a MORA readback or endurance qualification.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The 250 ms budget does not guarantee all 20 fields in one snapshot. On this camera, later AGC fields and mirror/flip were timed out or skipped, and some following snapshots found the mailbox still busy. These statuses are retained; the driver does not extend the budget or pretend that missing readings were measured. Readback is after capture, not frame-atomic, and does not establish gain, shutter timing or correction settings at exposure. To change settings, use the separate explicit sensor controls; capture with \u003ccode>--device-state\u003c/code> never writes to the camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#explicit-sensor-controls", "title": "Explicit Sensor Controls", "text": "\u003cp>Tiny1C hardware controls are standalone operations, not capture options. Stop any service or other process owning the camera first. Only one control is accepted per invocation; it cannot be combined with an output image, replay, capture-server, analysis, correction or rendering options. Nothing in normal capture or configuration-file loading automatically applies these writes.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Read and record original values before changing them:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/lwircam --get-control gain\nbin/lwircam --get-control emissivity-128\nbin/lwircam --get-control agc-mode\nbin/lwircam --get-control agc\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Select a particular camera with \u003ccode>--device /dev/videoN\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--serial SERIAL\u003c/code>. The normal native device identity, exclusive ownership and USB permissions apply: the account needs read/write access to the Tiny1C USB node, which the supplied \u003ccode>99-tiny1c.rules\u003c/code> grants to the \u003ccode>plugdev\u003c/code> group. These commands use Linux USBFS directly.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#what-each-control-does", "title": "What Each Control Does", "text": "Command Values What It Changes Use It When \u003ccode>--get-control NAME\u003c/code> \u003ccode>gain\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>emissivity-128\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>agc-mode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>agc\u003c/code> Nothing; reads the current firmware value Recording settings before a change, or checking state after an uncertain result \u003ccode>--set-gain low\\|high\u003c/code> \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>high\u003c/code> The sensor's physical measurement range. High gain gives finer temperature resolution over roughly everyday temperatures; low gain covers much hotter scenes at coarser resolution Scenes saturate (pixels pinned at the maximum) in high gain, or you need finer detail and the scene is not hot \u003ccode>--set-emissivity-128 N\u003c/code> 1-128 The emissivity the firmware assumes when converting radiation to temperature, in units of 1/128 (128 = 1.0, 122 \u2248 0.95, 64 = 0.5) Measuring a known surface type: most organic matter, water and painted or matte surfaces are about 0.95; bare polished metal is much lower \u003ccode>--set-agc-mode N\u003c/code> 0-5 The vendor's automatic contrast algorithm for the camera's own grey image plane Tuning the camera-supplied image appearance; it does not change the temperature plane \u003ccode>--set-agc 0\\|1\u003c/code> 0 off, 1 on Whether that automatic image contrast is applied at all Wanting a stable, non-adapting camera image for comparison between frames \u003ccode>--ffc\u003c/code> none Triggers one flat-field correction (vendor OOC/B update); the internal shutter briefly closes to re-measure the sensor's offsets The image shows banding, fixed patterns or drift, typically after warm-up or a large ambient temperature change \u003cp>Gain and emissivity affect the reported temperatures. The vendor defines AGC mode and AGC on/off as image-processing settings for the camera's own grey image; the temperature plane is not expected to change, but this has not been independently measured. LWIRcam's own rendering windows, palettes and percentile views are independent of both. \u003ccode>--set-gain\u003c/code> is the physical sensor setting, unlike \u003ccode>--gain-label\u003c/code>, which only names the gain recorded in metadata and correction-profile matching. During FFC the camera may briefly deliver shutter-closed or repeated frames; avoid triggering it while taking a capture you need.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#output-and-exit-status", "title": "Output And Exit Status", "text": "\u003cp>On success the command exits with status 0 and prints one line:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>LWIR_CONTROL_OK control=gain operation=read value=1\nLWIR_CONTROL_OK control=emissivity-128 operation=write value=122 readback=122 verified=1\nLWIR_CONTROL_OK control=ffc operation=trigger acknowledged=1 verified=0\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For gain, \u003ccode>value=1\u003c/code> means high and \u003ccode>value=0\u003c/code> means low. A write succeeds only when the firmware reads back the requested value. FFC reports acknowledgement only: the camera completed the request, but correction quality is not measured.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On failure the command exits with status 1 and reports the control, a status (\u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>busy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>io_error\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>invalid\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unsupported\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>cancelled\u003c/code>) and whether a command was \u003ccode>submitted\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>submitted=0\u003c/code> means the camera was not changed. \u003ccode>submitted=1\u003c/code> means it may have changed even though completion was not confirmed; read the value back before deciding what to do next. Invalid values or combinations exit with status 2 before the camera is touched.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Each operation first acquires a fresh valid stream frame, without repeating the initial-power-instance-only eight-frame qualification; the camera's control channel does not reliably answer before streaming frames. Property setters send one command and then require matching targeted readback. This confirms firmware readback, not physical temperature accuracy or exposure-time settings. Standalone targeted reads bypass the short all-fields snapshot budget.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Ordinary setters and targeted reads have a one-second mailbox budget; gain and FFC have five seconds. FFC includes a three-second correction interval, then checks mailbox completion. These intervals apply only to explicit controls, never the normal capture hot path. The usual process supervisor still bounds the complete invocation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>After any transfer error, timeout, cancellation or mismatched readback, inspect the reported \u003ccode>submitted\u003c/code> flag. Never automatically replay a write or FFC. Use \u003ccode>--get-control\u003c/code> to inspect current settings, then explicitly decide whether to restore a previously recorded value. FFC is not flash calibration programming; this API issues no save-to-flash, firmware-update or factory-reset commands. Do not assume firmware settings are persistent across power cycles.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Changing physical gain or emissivity affects measurement interpretation. Keep correction profiles and gain labels consistent and validate against independent references. These APIs do not attach exposure-qualified setting history to later photographs. No new flight-controller control protocol is introduced.\u003c/p> \u003cp>On 2026-10-30, PC-attached Tiny1C hardware tests changed and read back gain high/low, emissivity 128/64, AGC mode 0/1 and AGC enable 1/0, restoring each recorded original afterward. One FFC request was acknowledged. A following JPEG passed exact raw/embedded-temperature comparison with all 49,152 native temperature samples valid. Other firmware versions and MORA runtime remain separate validation requirements.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#measured-correction-profile", "title": "Measured Correction Profile", "text": "\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>--corrections\u003c/code> only with a profile established for the matching camera, gain label, dimensions and temperature range. An operator-supplied gain label is not physical gain readback. Keep the original samples and validate any correction on independent references.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Start with the temperature-check procedure, not arbitrary example coefficients. Display palettes and lens calibration cannot replace temperature verification.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#correction-file-format", "title": "Correction File Format", "text": "\u003cp>The flat INI uses these fields. Replace the identity/range and any fitted values with your camera's measured, independently checked configuration:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>format=LWIR_CORRECTIONS_V1\nserial=YOUR_CAMERA_SERIAL\ngain=YOUR_GAIN_LABEL\nwidth=256\nheight=192\nscale_ppm=1000000\noffset_mk=0\nrange_min_raw=18000\nrange_max_raw=22000\nbad_pixel_count=0\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This shows an identity scale/offset and example support range, not a measured correction. Raw units are kelvin times 64. The width/height, serial and gain label must match the input, and the support range must stay within the temperatures you verified. Unsupported readings are marked rather than extrapolated. List measured bad pixels only when you have established them; do not copy another camera's defect coordinates.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Apply a reviewed file with \u003ccode>--corrections /absolute/path/corrections.ini\u003c/code> and its matching \u003ccode>--gain-label\u003c/code>. Original samples remain available; display correction does not write firmware calibration tables. Save the file and independent checks together so the result can be reproduced.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#use-with-usher", "title": "Use With USHER", "text": "\u003cp>For a reviewed live bench configuration:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --lwircam --lwir-config /absolute/path/thermal.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>A small capture policy might contain:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>format=LWIR_CONFIG_V1\nwidth=256\nheight=192\nwarmup_ms=5000\ntimeout_ms=20000\npalette=iron\nlegend=1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Those dimensions are a Tiny1C example, not the TDK stream. Use the actual camera mode. Select a new directory/session for new observations and keep service changes separate from standalone camera checks.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#geographic-hotspots", "title": "Geographic Hotspots", "text": "\u003cp>Use the calibration workshop to measure lens geometry and mounting and check independent targets. Geographic analysis also needs usable observation pose/timing and the appropriate analysis build profile. A detected hot region is not automatically a fire or a surveyed location.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#optional-onnx-classification", "title": "Optional ONNX Classification", "text": "\u003cp>Choose the assignment in \u003ccode>lwircam_settings.ini\u003c/code>, then use \u003ccode>--check-model\u003c/code> before \u003ccode>--analyze\u003c/code> on an existing image. Models requiring temperature values cannot use an image-only TDK frame. Classification is separate from numerical temperature measurement and calibrated hotspot projection. Choose compatible model inputs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "lwircam.html#troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Problem Check No camera or access denied Power, USB cable, selected serial and device permissions Temperature option rejected Whether this mode supplies a supported temperature plane Unexpected measurement location Native coordinates versus rotated display Changing brightness Fixed window versus scene-dependent percentile rendering Coordinates unavailable Analysis feature, reviewed calibration and valid pose evidence Implausible temperature Independent references, emissivity, reflections, gain state and calibration \u003cp>Before combining sensors on MORA, check repeated capture, storage, timing and total board resource use with the intended services running.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html", "title": "MIMIC: Fly With A Simulated Camera", "text": "\u003cp>MIMIC turns cached aerial imagery into camera views for a simulated flight. The aircraft's position and attitude determine what appears in each photo. USHER saves the JPEG and its available flight information just as it would for a camera observation.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Prepare a simulated flight | Simulation terms | Photo maps\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#from-tiles-to-a-photo", "title": "From Tiles To A Photo", "text": "\u003cp>The cache supplies pixels; NPS supplies pose. Banking or turning the aircraft changes the next camera view. Panning the GCS map does not move the camera. The renderer uses a flat ground model, not reconstructed buildings or terrain.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#quick-start", "title": "Quick Start", "text": "\u003cp>Use a working fixed-wing Paparazzi NPS aircraft. Start with the shared demo setup; no MORA or camera is required.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#1-prepare-the-map-cache", "title": "1. Prepare The Map Cache", "text": "\u003cp>In PprzGCS, display aerial imagery at zoom 18 over the example route and enough surrounding ground to cover the camera footprint. The default cache is \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map\u003c/code>, with the configured zoom-18 JPEG layout (\u003ccode>--mock-map-cache DIR\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> selects another existing cache):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>find ~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map -path '*/18/*/*.jpeg' | head\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Missing tiles stop capture. MIMIC does not download missing terrain or substitute blank/generated fields. Prepare the cache while online before expecting an offline flight to work.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#2-prepare-your-aircraft", "title": "2. Prepare Your Aircraft", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 usher soda pangea camera-lens\n./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> installs the Sensoraia flight plans, demo airframes and unified control panel into your Paparazzi \u003ccode>conf/\u003c/code> directory.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#a-complete-simulated-flight", "title": "A Complete Simulated Flight", "text": "\u003cp>Start Paparazzi Center (\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi &amp;&amp; ./paparazzi\u003c/code>), select \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code>, aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_MIMIC\u003c/code> (or your own aircraft using \u003ccode>flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_mimic_pangea_demo.xml\u003c/code>), and target nps, then click Build. In the Session tab choose \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code> and Sensoraia MIMIC PANGEA NPS, then click Execute. It starts \u003ccode>Simulator\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Data Link\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Server\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>PprzGCS\u003c/code> and native \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> together.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Wait for valid GPS and live telemetry, then select Takeoff. The example collects images on suitable survey legs near its nominal 100 m altitude. Look for newly saved camera JPEGs and a PANGEA map, not merely GCS shot dots. Let the normal landing finish and stop the session programs afterwards.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>A photo map is made from saved camera photographs, rather than joining the input tiles directly.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>The companion prints the fresh map directory. Original photos use the normal USHER output location. Preserve both for comparison. The session can be repeated with the same configuration; a new map is created for each run.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#try-a-single-photograph", "title": "Try A Single Photograph", "text": "\u003cp>For an existing NPS setup where you manage the other programs yourself:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --local --mock-map --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Do not start this alongside a Center session that already owns USHER. Select the AIcam capture mask and use the GCS photo control once the aircraft is in a suitable simulated pose. Wait for completion before retrying.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For optical blur, exposure and shutter experiments, use the optional lens emulator. It retains the clean MIMIC photograph. The LOOM NPS session passes \u003ccode>--mock-lens resources/camera_lens_aicam.json\u003c/code> on its \u003ccode>USHER\u003c/code> command line by default; edit that \u003ccode>--mock-lens\u003c/code> argument in the Session tab to use a custom profile or remove it to disable emulation. The selected derivative, not the clean source, reaches LOOM's reference archive and neural input.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#camera-geometry-and-limits", "title": "Camera Geometry And Limits", "text": "\u003cp>The camera is fixed to the simulated aircraft. Height, heading, bank and pitch affect its footprint. The clean renderer uses an aircraft-centred 3-by-3 tile neighbourhood; a view extending beyond it may be rejected.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The default field of view is illustrative. These visible map pixels are not thermal temperatures or a physical exposure. Check the module's model and input requirements before treating the pictures as representative evaluation data.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "mimic.html#when-a-photo-is-missing", "title": "When A Photo Is Missing", "text": "Symptom Check Cache check fails Zoom, imagery layout and coverage around the entire route GCS dot but no JPEG USHER's completion/error message and output permissions Rejected view during a turn Bank, height and footprint size relative to available tiles No new map Saved-photo availability and PANGEA's diagnostics Endpoint already in use Stop the previous session rather than starting duplicate peers"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html", "title": "Choose And Run An AI Model", "text": "\u003cp>Sensoraia can run compatible ONNX models on the CPU, using the same native inference support across its sensor applications. You choose a separate model for each job: a vehicle detector, sound classifier and sampling planner do not interpret the same outputs.\u003c/p> \u003cp>AI terms explained | Spottercam | Diplacam\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html#choose-a-model", "title": "Choose A Model", "text": "\u003cp>Start with a model exported for the application you want to use. Keep its graph, descriptor, labels and any deployment metadata together. The filename extension alone does not establish compatibility.\u003c/p> Application The Model's Job Spottercam Return image boxes, classes and scores LOOMcam Provide features or an explicitly declared return vector; a trained homing model is not included EARcam Classify the declared microphone sample window LWIRcam Classify declared display pixels or supported temperature samples Diplacam Predict one denoising step used by the native measurement planner \u003cp>Each application reads its own settings file, such as \u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_settings.ini\u003c/code>. A typical ONNX assignment is:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>inference_backend=onnx\nmodel=models/assignment/model.onnx\nmodel_descriptor=models/assignment/model.ini\nonnx_threads=1\ninference_timeout_ms=2000\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Paths in these settings are relative to the INI file, not your terminal. Use \u003ccode>--settings /absolute/path/settings.ini\u003c/code> to select another assignment. Keep the supplied descriptor with the graph: it records the expected input preparation, tensor layout and graph identity.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Spottercam and LOOMcam can also use a compatible IMX500 \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code> with the physical AI Camera. \u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\u003c/code> selects ONNX for \u003ccode>.onnx\u003c/code> or IMX500 for \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code> on hardware; during PC simulation or saved-image CPU analysis, Spottercam automatically probes sibling \u003ccode>vehicle_yolo11n_int8.onnx\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vehicle_yolo11n_fp32.onnx\u003c/code> exports in \u003ccode>models/\u003c/code> when \u003ccode>model\u003c/code> points to \u003ccode>models/nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code>. CPU inference does not require the camera's neural processor.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html#check-the-assignment-before-capture", "title": "Check The Assignment Before Capture", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root, for Spottercam:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 spottercam CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\nbin/spottercam --check-model --settings /absolute/path/spottercam_settings.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>\u003ccode>CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\u003c/code> builds without physical camera support. It still runs real model inference. Omit that switch when you need live camera capture. The model check does not take a photograph or establish detection accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html#try-a-saved-picture", "title": "Try A Saved Picture", "text": "\u003cp>Use an original image representative of the model's intended camera input:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/spottercam --analyze /absolute/path/photo.jpg \\\n  --settings /absolute/path/spottercam_settings.ini --sequence 1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Inspect the result beside the original and read any reported rejection. Ordinary analysis leaves the input unchanged and writes the application's result sidecar. The module guide explains its result fields and limitations.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Saved images, MIMIC views and supported live cameras can feed the same model processing. Their provenance remains different: a real neural result from a simulated image is still not a real-flight measurement.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html#understand-empty-results-and-errors", "title": "Understand Empty Results And Errors", "text": "Result What To Do Model accepted, no objects found Keep the empty result; do not invent targets Descriptor/hash mismatch Restore the graph and descriptor from the same export Unsupported tensor layout or preprocessing Obtain a compatible export; do not change labels merely to silence the check Runtime library unavailable Use the matching packaged runtime for this processor and release Inference timeout or failure Read the diagnostic and stop relying on fresh model results until resolved \u003cp>Explicit PC demos can use labelled random observations under their documented fallback rules. That is a simulation choice, not normal production error handling. A present invalid model must not silently become a random detector.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "onnxruntime.html#put-the-assignment-on-mora", "title": "Put The Assignment On MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Build and package on the PC using the deployment workflow. The release needs matching runtime libraries, selected model files and their settings. Review the module's package selection before activation; a successful local image run does not measure MORA's shared memory use or sustained processing rate.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Evaluate the model on independent examples resembling its intended use. Scores are not automatically calibrated probabilities, and changing the camera, height, lighting or sound environment can change performance.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html", "title": "PANGEA: Make Maps From Photographs", "text": "\u003cp>PANGEA combines supported saved photographs into a georeferenced photo map. Start with the included teaching images, then try photos from a MIMIC flight or a camera with the required pose and calibration information.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Mapping terms | MIMIC flight | GCS map tiles\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>The map is assembled from the six saved camera views.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#try-it-without-a-flight", "title": "Try It Without A Flight", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root, build PANGEA and copy the teaching images into your own example directory:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 pangea\nmkdir -p \"$HOME/Pictures/pangea-example/photos\"\ncp modules/pangea/docs/images/pangea-photo-*.jpg \"$HOME/Pictures/pangea-example/photos/\"\nbin/pangea --input \"$HOME/Pictures/pangea-example/photos\" \\\n  --output \"$HOME/Pictures/pangea-example/map-01\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use a new output name for a separate experiment. Wait for the map-published message, then open \u003ccode>map-01/latest/map.jpg\u003c/code>. The originals are unchanged. No flight, physical camera or online map connection is needed for this example.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#make-a-map-during-a-mimic-flight", "title": "Make A Map During A MIMIC Flight", "text": "\u003cp>The MIMIC Center session starts capture and mapping together and prints a new map directory. PANGEA uses saved camera views, not direct stitching of the source provider tiles.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a setup where you manage the other simulation components yourself:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --local --mock-map --pangea \"$HOME/Pictures/pangea-flight-01\" --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Do not run that command beside a session already owning USHER. A missing map does not necessarily mean capture failed; inspect the saved originals and the mapper's own diagnostic.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#use-your-own-camera-photos", "title": "Use Your Own Camera Photos", "text": "\u003cp>For original USHER JPEGs with a matching measured camera profile:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --input /absolute/path/photos --output /absolute/path/new-map \\\n  --camera-profile /absolute/path/camera.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Add \u003ccode>--watch\u003c/code> if the directory will receive more completed photos. Otherwise the command finishes its backlog and exits. One camera, resolution and calibration belong together in a map.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The profile declares delivered image dimensions, lens geometry and mounting. Use the supplied \u003ccode>examples/pangea/aicam-2028.ini\u003c/code> only as an illustrative starting format, not measured calibration. Generic GPS-only JPEGs may lack the attitude/height evidence required by this workflow.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#prepare-the-camera-profile", "title": "Prepare The Camera Profile", "text": "\u003cp>Copy \u003ccode>examples/pangea/aicam-2028.ini\u003c/code> to your own \u003ccode>camera.ini\u003c/code>. Its fields describe the delivered JPEG, not just the sensor's advertised size:\u003c/p> Field What To Supply \u003ccode>filename_prefix\u003c/code> Original camera filename prefix, normally \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>c\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>l\u003c/code> \u003ccode>width\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>height\u003c/code> Actual JPEG dimensions in this mode \u003ccode>fx\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fy\u003c/code> Focal lengths in image pixels, not millimetres \u003ccode>cx\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cy\u003c/code> Principal point in the delivered image \u003ccode>mount_roll_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mount_pitch_deg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mount_yaw_deg\u003c/code> Measured mounting relative to the aircraft \u003cp>Keep \u003ccode>schema=PANGEA_CAMERA_V1\u003c/code>. The supplied numbers are a nominal example; substitute measured geometry and mounting for your setup. Changed crop, resolution or optics can need a different profile. Use original upright photos, not pre-undistorted, cropped or annotated review copies.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a live supported camera launcher, choose the actual backend and profile:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --aicam --pangea /absolute/path/new-map \\\n  --pangea-camera /absolute/path/camera.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This is real capture. Review sensor ownership and service selection first. The visual-return LOOM profile is not this launcher's real-photo mapping mode.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#completed-survey-workflow", "title": "Completed-Survey Workflow", "text": "\u003cp>Use a validated capture manifest or survey CSV with the supplied \u003ccode>examples/pangea/survey.ini\u003c/code> format. Set input and new output paths for your survey, then check it before running:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --check /absolute/path/map.ini\nbin/pangea --run /absolute/path/map.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> Choice Use It For SIMPLE A simpler reference-plane composite on a desktop GREAT Seam selection, blending and completed XYZ tile output \u003ccode>runtime.profile=auto\u003c/code> Let the application select the supported resource profile \u003cp>The bounded profile supports GREAT XYZ output; SIMPLE and orthomap exports use a desktop profile. Do not force a desktop workload onto a small board. Choose the tile zoom explicitly; higher zoom does not create source detail.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#incremental-great", "title": "Incremental GREAT", "text": "\u003cp>This workflow publishes provisional XYZ revisions as completed photos arrive, then produces a final tile pyramid. It is distinct from the ordinary live photo-map watcher and can run independently of USHER.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Copy \u003ccode>examples/pangea/great-aicam.ini\u003c/code> to your own \u003ccode>session.ini\u003c/code>. Set:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>\u003ccode>input.photo_directory\u003c/code> to the original USHER photo directory.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>input.camera_profile\u003c/code> to your matching camera profile.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Start/end latitude/longitude to the region the photos actually cover.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>mosaic.reference_plane_amsl_m\u003c/code> to ground height in the same vertical   reference as the admitted camera altitude; do not mix MSL and ellipsoid heights.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>output.directory\u003c/code> to a new map session.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Keep \u003ccode>runtime.profile=low_memory\u003c/code> for this AI-camera directory workflow, including when trying it on a desktop. The completed-survey profile choices above are a different input path.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The example chooses \u003ccode>pose_policy=recorded\u003c/code> for ordinary request-pose metadata. Choose \u003ccode>exposure\u003c/code> only when native capture provides qualifying observation- time evidence. Exposure mode rejects missing evidence rather than silently using a request pose. Neither choice certifies accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#follow-completed-photos", "title": "Follow Completed Photos", "text": "\u003cp>Start capture separately, then run mapping in another terminal:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --follow-great /absolute/path/session.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This directory mode accepts completed top-level AI-camera \u003ccode>a*.jpg\u003c/code> originals with suitable metadata, not LOOM references or arbitrary GPS-only exports. Do not edit published originals while mapping. PANGEA reports rejected files and resource delays; starting a watcher does not promise an immediate revision.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#timing-controls", "title": "Timing Controls", "text": "\u003cp>The incremental follower reads these settings from the session INI:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>[live]\nupdate_seconds=30\nbatch_photos=4\ncpu_percent=20\npause_file=mapping.pause\nfinal_file=capture.complete\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>After the initial revision, changed input becomes eligible for another render when either four photos have accumulated or 30 seconds have elapsed since the last successful render. These are scheduling thresholds, not completion deadlines: validation, preparation and rendering still need time to finish. \u003ccode>cpu_percent\u003c/code> limits average process CPU use relative to one core, not the percentage of the whole computer. Its accepted range is 1..100.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Marker paths are relative to the INI directory. Create the pause file to defer processing, then remove it to resume. Create the final file only after capture and photo publication have finished. Pause and low-memory waits remain cancellable; a final marker does not override a pause.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The ordinary \u003ccode>--watch\u003c/code> photo-map path instead checks for two seconds of stable file identity using a monotonic clock, then publishes changed maps on its ten-second export cadence. A future-dated photo does not postpone admission; an explicit \u003ccode>--since\u003c/code> cutoff still filters source timestamps.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#finish-or-resume", "title": "Finish Or Resume", "text": "\u003cp>Stop capture and let pending tagging/publication finish. Stop the follower with Ctrl+C, then finalize the unchanged session:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --finalize-great /absolute/path/session.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For an interrupted run, repeat \u003ccode>--follow-great\u003c/code> with unchanged inputs/settings. Stopping PANGEA does not stop USHER. A finalized session is closed; choose a new output directory for another flight. Directory input uses GREAT's update/follow/finalize commands, not the completed-manifest \u003ccode>--run\u003c/code> path.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#create-map-tiles-for-a-gcs", "title": "Create Map Tiles For A GCS", "text": "\u003cp>Ground control stations display slippy Web-Mercator (\u003ccode>EPSG:3857\u003c/code>) pyramids of \u003ccode>256 x 256\u003c/code> tiles addressed as \u003ccode>{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>x\u003c/code> increasing eastward and \u003ccode>y\u003c/code> increasing southward.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The quick \u003ccode>--input ... --output ...\u003c/code> replay command above writes one stitched \u003ccode>latest/map.jpg\u003c/code> (plus \u003ccode>latest/map.vrt\u003c/code> for GIS tools), not a slippy tile tree. To generate GCS-ready \u003ccode>{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code> tiles from a directory of area photos, use \u003ccode>processing.mode=great\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>output.format=xyz\u003c/code>. PANGEA projects each admitted photo onto a UTM working grid, selects graph-cut seams, blends overlapping views across four frequency bands, resamples into Web Mercator, and writes clean \u003ccode>256 x 256\u003c/code> JPEGs without EXIF or XMP overhead.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#choose-tile-zoom-levels-and-staging-output", "title": "Choose Tile Zoom Levels And Staging Output", "text": "\u003cp>One XYZ run builds up to three consecutive zoom levels (\u003ccode>min_zoom\u003c/code> within two levels below \u003ccode>zoom\u003c/code>), deriving each lower zoom from aligned \u003ccode>2 x 2\u003c/code> child tiles so seams never jump when zooming in the GCS:\u003c/p> Zoom Ground Pixel (Equator) Ground Pixel (~52\u00b0N) Typical GCS Use \u003ccode>17\u003c/code> ~1.19 m ~0.74 m Approach and circuit overview \u003ccode>18\u003c/code> ~0.60 m ~0.37 m Standard flight-plan view; also read by MIMIC \u003ccode>19\u003c/code> ~0.30 m ~0.18 m Maximum built-in zoom of standard GCS satellite layers \u003ccode>20\u003c/code> ~0.15 m ~0.09 m Near-native low-altitude detail with a custom local GCS layer \u003cp>Pick \u003ccode>zoom\u003c/code> to match your camera's actual ground sample distance rather than oversampling beyond optical detail. Always set \u003ccode>output.directory\u003c/code> to a new staging directory first\u2014never directly to a populated GCS cache\u2014because PANGEA refuses to overwrite existing \u003ccode>{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code> files.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#example-1-directory-of-just-captured-usher-camera-photos", "title": "Example 1: Directory Of Just-Captured USHER Camera Photos", "text": "\u003cp>Suppose you have just flown over an area with USHER AIcam and copied the folder of saved \u003ccode>a*.jpg\u003c/code> originals to \u003ccode>$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/photos\u003c/code>, together with your matching \u003ccode>camera.ini\u003c/code> (Prepare The Camera Profile).\u003c/p> \u003cp>Create \u003ccode>$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/gcs-camera-tiles.ini\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>examples/pangea/great-aicam.ini\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>[input]\nphoto_directory=/home/you/Pictures/area-flight-01/photos\ncamera_profile=/home/you/Pictures/area-flight-01/camera.ini\npose_policy=recorded\n\n[start]\nlat=52.0052\nlon=4.3574\n\n[end]\nlat=52.0034\nlon=4.3616\n\n[processing]\nmode=great\n\n[runtime]\nprofile=low_memory\n\n[mosaic]\nreference_plane_amsl_m=0.2\n\n[tiles]\nzoom=19\nmin_zoom=17\n\n[output]\ndirectory=/home/you/Pictures/area-flight-01/xyz-session\nformat=xyz\n\n[live]\ncpu_percent=100\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Key settings for a folder of photos you already captured:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>\u003ccode>[start]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[end]\u003c/code>: Opposite latitude/longitude corners bounding the   area your photos cover.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>mosaic.reference_plane_amsl_m\u003c/code>: Ground height of the surveyed field in   the same vertical reference as the flight controller's navigation   altitude.\u003c/li> \u003cli>\u003ccode>runtime.profile=low_memory\u003c/code>: Required for the AI-camera   \u003ccode>photo_directory\u003c/code> input path even on a desktop PC. Setting \u003ccode>cpu_percent=100\u003c/code>   under \u003ccode>[live]\u003c/code> removes the background 20%-of-one-core throttle when running   post-flight on a workstation.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Because all photos are already in the directory, you do not need to run the \u003ccode>--follow-great\u003c/code> watcher. Run one of these single-pass commands from the Sensoraia root:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Irregular flight path or partial bounding-box coverage \u2014 publish every   strictly observed, 100%-covered \u003ccode>256 x 256\u003c/code> tile and omit unphotographed   corners cleanly without gap invention:\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --update-great \"$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/gcs-camera-tiles.ini\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cul> \u003cli>Complete lawnmower box coverage \u2014 finalize the session with graph-cut   seams, multiband blending and source-derived gap completion across the entire   \u003ccode>[start]\u003c/code>..\u003ccode>[end]\u003c/code> rectangle:\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --finalize-great \"$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/gcs-camera-tiles.ini\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Both commands publish the slippy tile pyramid at:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/xyz-session/latest/xyz/{17,18,19}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#example-2-completed-survey-directory-with-manifest-or-csv", "title": "Example 2: Completed Survey Directory With Manifest Or CSV", "text": "\u003cp>When your photo directory contains a completed capture manifest (\u003ccode>*.capture.ini\u003c/code>, which already supplies \u003ccode>[start]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[end]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>source_csv\u003c/code>) or a validated survey CSV (\u003ccode>*.csv\u003c/code>) beside the JPEGs, use the desktop-capable survey runner based on \u003ccode>examples/pangea/survey.ini\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Create \u003ccode>$HOME/Pictures/area-survey-01/gcs-survey-tiles.ini\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>[input]\ncapture_manifest=/home/you/Pictures/area-survey-01/photos/photos_session.capture.ini\n\n[processing]\nmode=great\n\n[runtime]\nprofile=auto\n\n[tiles]\nzoom=20\nmin_zoom=18\n\n[output]\ndirectory=/home/you/Pictures/area-survey-01/xyz-staging\nformat=xyz\njpeg_quality=95\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>If you use \u003ccode>source_csv=/path/to/photos.csv\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>capture_manifest\u003c/code>, add \u003ccode>[start]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[end]\u003c/code> corner coordinates; on a desktop PC (\u003ccode>profile=auto\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>desktop\u003c/code>), you can also add \u003ccode>complete_only=1\u003c/code> under \u003ccode>[tiles]\u003c/code> so flight lines that miss the corners of your \u003ccode>[start]\u003c/code>..\u003ccode>[end]\u003c/code> box publish all complete tiles and record omitted edge tiles instead of stopping.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Validate the inputs and tile plan first, then render across your PC's CPU cores:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/pangea --check \"$HOME/Pictures/area-survey-01/gcs-survey-tiles.ini\"\nbin/pangea --run \"$HOME/Pictures/area-survey-01/gcs-survey-tiles.ini\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The tile folders (\u003ccode>18/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>19/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>20/\u003c/code>) are written directly inside \u003ccode>xyz-staging/\u003c/code>, and the coverage report (\u003ccode>dataset.json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tiles.csv\u003c/code>) is saved beside it under \u003ccode>pangea_exports/xyz_&lt;session&gt;/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#place-and-use-the-tiles-in-paparazzi-gcs-pprzgcs", "title": "Place And Use The Tiles In Paparazzi GCS (PprzGCS)", "text": "\u003cp>PprzGCS (\u003ccode>pprzgcs\u003c/code>) stores its local map cache by default under \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map\u003c/code> (configured by \u003ccode>map/tiles_path\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>~/.config/paparazzi/pprzgcs/settings.ini\u003c/code>, or in \u003ccode>pprzgcs --configure\u003c/code> under Map \u2192 Tiles path). Inside that directory, each map provider uses:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/&lt;DIR&gt;/{z}/{x}/{y}&lt;format&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>PprzGCS always checks the local disk cache before making any HTTP request: any \u003ccode>{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code> file present on disk is displayed immediately, is never overwritten from the Internet, and works completely offline (\u003ccode>pprzgcs --maps_no_http\u003c/code>).\u003c/p> \u003cp>Choose one of the two cache placements below depending on how you fly.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#option-a-recommended-dedicated-local-layer-in-pprzgcs", "title": "Option A (Recommended): Dedicated Local Layer In PprzGCS", "text": "\u003cp>Placing your PANGEA pyramid in its own cache subdirectory (\u003ccode>map/PANGEA/\u003c/code>) is the cleanest setup:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Your existing \u003ccode>GOOGLE\u003c/code> satellite cache stays untouched.\u003c/li> \u003cli>You can toggle Pangea Survey on/off and adjust its opacity slider right   over the satellite base map in PprzGCS's Layers panel.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Setting \u003ccode>addr=\"\"\u003c/code> prevents HTTP requests for unmapped surrounding tiles.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Setting \u003ccode>zoomMax=\"20\"\u003c/code> (or up to \u003ccode>22\u003c/code>) lets PprzGCS display sharp zoom-20+   tiles directly, whereas the built-in \u003ccode>Google\u003c/code> layer clamps at \u003ccode>zoomMax=\"19\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003col> \u003cli>Copy only the numeric \u003ccode>{z}/\u003c/code> zoom folders into \u003ccode>map/PANGEA/\u003c/code> (from    \u003ccode>xyz-staging/\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>--run\u003c/code>, or from \u003ccode>xyz-session/latest/xyz/\u003c/code> for    \u003ccode>--update-great\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>--finalize-great\u003c/code>):\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>mkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/PANGEA\"\nrsync -av \"$HOME/Pictures/area-survey-01/xyz-staging/\" \\\n  \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/PANGEA/\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>(For Example 1's camera-directory session, replace the source path with    \u003ccode>\"$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/xyz-session/latest/xyz/\"\u003c/code> \u2014 keep the trailing    slash so \u003ccode>17/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>18/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>19/\u003c/code> land directly inside \u003ccode>map/PANGEA/\u003c/code> without    session metadata files.)\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Register the local layer in your user \u003ccode>tile_sources.xml\u003c/code>. PprzGCS loads    \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/tile_sources.xml\u003c/code> ahead of its system    defaults (you can also open both folders from PprzGCS via File \u2192 Open app    directory and File \u2192 Open user directory). Copy the default    \u003ccode>tile_sources.xml\u003c/code> into your user directory once if it is not there yet:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>mkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs\"\ncp -n /usr/share/pprzgcs/tile_sources.xml \\\n  \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/tile_sources.xml\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Edit \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/tile_sources.xml\u003c/code> and add your    \u003ccode>Pangea Survey\u003c/code> \u003ccode>&lt;source&gt;\u003c/code> at the top of \u003ccode>&lt;sources&gt;\u003c/code> (PprzGCS assigns    higher initial Z-order to earlier entries, so placing it above \u003ccode>Google\u003c/code>    draws your fresh survey tiles on top of the satellite background    automatically):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;sources&gt;\n &lt;source\n     name=\"Pangea Survey\" dir=\"PANGEA\"\n     addr=\"\"\n     zoomMin=\"18\" zoomMax=\"20\"\n     tileSize=\"256\"\n     format=\".jpeg\"\n /&gt;\n &lt;!-- keep existing Google, OpenStreetMap, etc. entries below --&gt;\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Match \u003ccode>zoomMin\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>zoomMax\u003c/code> to the zoom levels you generated (for example    \u003ccode>17\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>19\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>18\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>20\u003c/code>). If you do not add a custom \u003ccode>200 x 100\u003c/code> PNG    at \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/pictures/map_thumbnails/Pangea Survey.png\u003c/code>,    PprzGCS uses its default layer thumbnail automatically.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Start PprzGCS and enable the layer: launch \u003ccode>pprzgcs\u003c/code> (add    \u003ccode>--maps_no_http\u003c/code> for strictly offline field operation), open the Layers    panel, enable Pangea Survey alongside Google, and zoom in to your    surveyed area.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#option-b-drop-in-merge-into-pprzgcss-google-cache", "title": "Option B: Drop-In Merge Into PprzGCS's \u003ccode>GOOGLE\u003c/code> Cache", "text": "\u003cp>PprzGCS's built-in Google layer uses \u003ccode>dir=\"GOOGLE\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>zoomMin=\"0\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>zoomMax=\"19\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tileSize=\"256\"\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>format=\".jpeg\"\u003c/code>\u2014the exact tile geometry and \u003ccode>.jpeg\u003c/code> extension PANGEA writes. Merging zoom \u003ccode>17..19\u003c/code> tiles into \u003ccode>map/GOOGLE/\u003c/code> requires zero XML configuration and also feeds zoom \u003ccode>18\u003c/code> directly into Sensoraia's MIMIC simulated camera (\u003ccode>GOOGLE/18/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code>).\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Generate your tiles with \u003ccode>zoom=19\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>min_zoom=17\u003c/code> into a staging folder    (built-in \u003ccode>Google\u003c/code> does not request zoom \u003ccode>20\u003c/code>+).\u003c/li> \u003cli>Optionally back up \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE\u003c/code> if you want    to preserve prior cached satellite imagery over the same coordinates, then    merge the numeric zoom directories:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>mkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE\"\nrsync -av \"$HOME/Pictures/area-flight-01/xyz-session/latest/xyz/\" \\\n  \"$HOME/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE/\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003col> \u003cli>Restart \u003ccode>pprzgcs\u003c/code> if it was already open (PprzGCS keeps already-loaded    tiles in memory for the current window session). Outside your photographed    patch and at coarser zooms (\u003ccode>0..16\u003c/code>), PprzGCS seamlessly surrounds your    updated survey area with the rest of your \u003ccode>GOOGLE\u003c/code> cache.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#map-files", "title": "Map Files", "text": "\u003cp>Open the printed \u003ccode>latest/map.jpg\u003c/code> for a quick view. Geographic companion files, coverage information and export reports describe how the map is placed and which input contributed. Use those records when importing into GIS tools.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For the ordinary live/replay workflow:\u003c/p> File Use It For \u003ccode>latest/map.jpg\u003c/code> Quick visual view of the current map \u003ccode>latest/map-mask.pgm\u003c/code> Distinguish photographed coverage from missing ground \u003ccode>latest/map.vrt\u003c/code> Open the georeferenced map with transparent gaps in QGIS/GDAL \u003ccode>latest/map.jgw\u003c/code> JPEG world-file positioning; it does not identify a CRS by itself \u003ccode>latest/map.json\u003c/code> Geometry, input counts and source/profile information \u003ccode>done/*.json\u003c/code> Find accepted/rejected source records and their reasons \u003cp>Black in the JPEG can mean missing coverage or real black image content; use the coverage mask or VRT to distinguish them. Map pixel spacing is not a measurement of geographic accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>XYZ tiles are addressed by zoom/X/Y, not three-dimensional coordinates. A visually complete export can contain source-derived gap filling; it is not proof every output pixel was independently photographed. Neither SIMPLE nor GREAT reconstructs building heights or all terrain relief.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A completed GREAT export is a tile pyramid with a \u003ccode>dataset.json\u003c/code> report; PANGEA does not currently package it as one GeoTIFF. The ordinary live/replay map workflow separately provides the \u003ccode>latest/map.vrt\u003c/code> GIS view described above. For an interrupted survey, keep inputs and settings unchanged and rerun the same command to reuse a matching checkpoint. Changed inputs or calibration need a new job. Do not edit originals midway through a resumed survey.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "pangea.html#troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Problem Check Photos rejected Required pose/height information, image orientation and camera profile No published map yet Wait for export, not just an assembled-photo message Doubled roof edges or seams Pose timing, calibration, terrain relief and overlap Existing output refused Use a new directory or resume the matching unchanged job Resource limit reached Use the appropriate resource profile and inspect the diagnostic Working input changed during checksum Stop edits or copying into published inputs; resume only with the original unchanged files Cannot hash or publish a working file Check that storage is available and the input is a regular file, not a pipe or symlink New tiles do not appear in PprzGCS Confirm numeric \u003ccode>{z}/{x}/{y}.jpeg\u003c/code> folders sit directly under \u003ccode>map/&lt;DIR&gt;/\u003c/code>, check \u003ccode>zoomMin\u003c/code>..\u003ccode>zoomMax\u003c/code>, enable the layer, and restart \u003ccode>pprzgcs\u003c/code> \u003cp>Reference-plane maps can misplace elevated objects. Attractive blending and fine pixel spacing do not demonstrate surveyed geographic accuracy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html", "title": "Use The Raspberry Pi AI Camera", "text": "\u003cp>The Raspberry Pi AI Camera is a visible-light camera built around Sony's IMX500 sensor. Sensoraia can use it for ordinary photographs, compatible vehicle detection or experimental visual return. Choose one profile at a time.\u003c/p> \u003cp>AI Camera terms | Spottercam | LOOMcam\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#connect-the-hardware", "title": "Connect The Hardware", "text": "\u003cp>Power off before connecting or removing the ribbon cable. Use the cable matching the board's connector; the Pi Zero family needs the smaller Zero-compatible connection. Seat the cable squarely and close the latch. Check your board's camera/display connector documentation rather than assuming similar-looking sockets have the same role.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#prepare-raspberry-pi-os", "title": "Prepare Raspberry Pi OS", "text": "\u003cp>The current reference setup uses 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie and its camera tools, drivers and matching resources. MORA is not limited to a Pi, but using this camera on another board depends on a compatible camera stack and requires that board's own integration and validation. On the reference Pi:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install imx500-all\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use the distribution's camera documentation if that package is unavailable. Review OS/kernel updates as board maintenance: compatibility needs checking with the actual installed camera stack, not a dated version copied from a guide.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#check-camera-discovery", "title": "Check Camera Discovery", "text": "\u003cp>Stop USHER and any other camera client before a standalone check:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>rpicam-hello --version\nrpicam-hello --list-cameras\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Look for the IMX500. With no local display, take a photo without preview:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>rpicam-still --nopreview --timeout 2000 --output ai-camera-test.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Open the JPEG on your PC and check that it is sharp, correctly exposed and oriented as expected. Use the actual reported camera index when multiple devices are present. A nonempty file alone does not prove image quality.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#choose-a-sensoraia-profile", "title": "Choose A Sensoraia Profile", "text": "Profile Use It For \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code> Ordinary photographs using the OS camera tool \u003ccode>--aicam --native-photo\u003c/code> Native photographs without a model or \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code> Compatible vehicle detection and same-image results \u003ccode>--spottercam --native-photo\u003c/code> Native photographs with Spottercam detection disabled \u003ccode>--loomcam\u003c/code> Experimental visual-return observations and bounded target policy \u003cp>All three use the same physical camera. Do not start two owners, and do not run a standalone camera application beside an active USHER capture profile. Installing camera packages alone does not select a model or enable detection.\u003c/p> \u003cp>An IMX500 model uses a compatible RPK. A CPU ONNX model is a separate choice; see choose a model. Renaming an unrelated model does not make its outputs meaningful to the application.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#deploy-and-select-a-profile", "title": "Deploy And Select A Profile", "text": "\u003cp>The full MORA package installs all camera workers ready to run. You do not need to hand-edit systemd service templates to switch between them\u2014simply set \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> at the Sensoraia root (or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> on the \u003ccode>make\u003c/code> command line):\u003c/p> Desired IMX500 MORA Mode \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> / Command-Line Selection Resulting Service Command Plain AI Camera photos (native libcamera, no model) \u003ccode>CAMERA=aicam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>usher --aicam --native-photo\u003c/code> Spottercam (default: IMX500 + \u003ccode>nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code>) \u003ccode>CAMERA=spottercam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>usher --spottercam\u003c/code> LOOMcam (visual-return homing) \u003ccode>CAMERA=loomcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>usher --loomcam\u003c/code> \u003cp>(Advanced: if you specifically want OS \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>--spottercam --native-photo\u003c/code>, you can customize \u003ccode>ExecStart\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>usher.service.in\u003c/code> directly.)\u003c/p> \u003cp>From your PC checkout, build, upload, and activate the AI Camera profile in one step:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-deploy CAMERA=aicam MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local\nssh air@theatre.local 'systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service'\nssh air@theatre.local 'journalctl -u usher.service -n 50 --no-pager'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Adjust the SSH target and \u003ccode>MORA_INSTALL_DIR\u003c/code> for your installation. Deployment rebuilds the ARM64 package on the PC, stages it on MORA, checks dependencies and restarts USHER. The packaged service uses its configured UART; send a camera-2 request from the connected flight controller and inspect the saved image under the service account's \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code>. Filenames begin with \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>, and existing photographs are not overwritten. See the complete deployment procedure for setup, password handling and recovery.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#native-photographs", "title": "Native Photographs", "text": "\u003cp>For photographs without object recognition, select either native photo mode:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --aicam --native-photo\n./usher --spottercam --native-photo\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Both use the same libcamera capture and JPEG code. The AIcam selection starts \u003ccode>bin/aicam\u003c/code>; the Spottercam selection starts \u003ccode>bin/spottercam\u003c/code> without a model. Neither starts \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code>, loads a model or produces detection results. USHER also skips SODA analysis for these photo-only captures. The IMX500's previously retained network is cleared from volatile sensor state; its model file is unchanged and a later detection session loads it again.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Each request waits for a new frame and saves a 2028x1520 JPEG at quality 90 with 4:2:0 sampling and sensor timing evidence. The camera and storage determine the available rate. Use the camera speed test to measure your setup. A failed native capture is not replayed through another backend. \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code> cannot be combined with mock capture or detector settings.\u003c/p> Option Purpose \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code> Select ordinary AI Camera photographs; without \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code>, use \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code> Select the same IMX500 for vehicle detection, or photo-only capture when combined with \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code> Use the native libcamera worker without a model, detector, SODA or \u003ccode>rpicam-still\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--speedtest\u003c/code> Take one warm-up and ten timed photographs, report the observed rate, then exit \u003ccode>USHER_AI_CAM_TIMING=1\u003c/code> Log native acquisition and JPEG-save times; useful for diagnosing capture latency \u003cp>If the native worker fails during a request, that request fails without an automatic replay. A later request can start a replacement worker after the old one has exited. Check the service journal and storage space if failures repeat.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a standalone photograph, stop other camera owners and choose a new absolute output path:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./bin/aicam --probe\n./bin/aicam --capture /home/air/Pictures/aicam-photo.jpg\n./bin/spottercam --photo-only --probe\n./bin/spottercam --photo-only --capture /home/air/Pictures/spotter-photo.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Run these from \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia\u003c/code> on MORA, after stopping USHER if it owns the camera. \u003ccode>--probe\u003c/code> checks that a frame is available without saving a JPEG; \u003ccode>--capture\u003c/code> saves one new file and exits. Restore the service afterward. The target needs its compatible libcamera runtime, IMX500 driver and Raspberry Pi camera tuning files; no model is needed for native photo mode.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a short throughput measurement, follow the camera speed test, selecting either \u003ccode>./usher --aicam --native-photo --speedtest\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>./usher --spottercam --native-photo --speedtest\u003c/code>. It stops and restores the service manually around the standalone measurement and retains the test photographs.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#capture-through-usher", "title": "Capture Through USHER", "text": "\u003cp>After the board/sensor check, select the intended backend in your reviewed service configuration and follow deployment. For a separately managed live bench session with no other owner:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --aicam --debug\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Connect the prepared FC/UART integration and request one photo. Check both the saved JPEG and its available flight metadata. Use the capture guide for service diagnostics and result interpretation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "raspberry-pi-ai-camera.html#if-something-does-not-work", "title": "If Something Does Not Work", "text": "\u003cp>Check cable seating and power before software changes. A busy-camera error often means another application owns it. For model failures, inspect the module's descriptor/export requirements rather than switching randomly between RPK, ONNX and camera-tool configuration files.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Camera mode, crop, delivered dimensions and mounting affect geometry. A nominal product field of view is not a calibration of your installed camera.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html", "title": "Prepare a Pi Zero 2 W for USHER", "text": "\u003cp>This guide covers the Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W reference board and its current 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie setup. MORA means the onboard compute role, so a different suitable board may be used; its OS, UART, drivers, runtime packages and deployment need board-specific setup. These Pi instructions are not universal.\u003c/p> \u003cp>When this guide says OS image, it means the single operating-system file you flash onto a microSD card\u2014not a camera JPEG\u2014and flashing simply means writing that operating system onto the card so MORA can boot from it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Use the official Raspberry Pi Imager to write Raspberry Pi OS to a microSD card. The steps below cover first access, the camera UART and network settings you may need away from the desk.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Before flashing, make sure the selected Wi-Fi network is reachable by a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W. It supports 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi; use WPA2-Personal or a WPA3-Personal network verified to work with the selected image and access point. Avoid enterprise authentication and legacy TKIP.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>In Imager, select Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS    Trixie headless image, and the intended microSD card. Confirm its capacity and name    before continuing: writing erases the selected card.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Open OS customization. Set a hostname, a non-default user, the Wi-Fi network    and country, then enable SSH. Prefer public-key authentication; paste    the public key from the development PC into Authorized keys.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Write the card and wait for Imager's verification to succeed. Eject it,    insert it into MORA, and power the board.\u003c/li> \u003cli>From the development PC, confirm the configured account and network work:\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This example uses the project's default account and hostname. Substitute the    username and hostname you configured in Imager if they differ. The \u003ccode>.local\u003c/code>    name requires mDNS support on the development PC and local network; if it    does not resolve, use the board's DHCP address from the router instead.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For Imager-specific screens and recovery options, see the official Raspberry Pi getting-started documentation.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html#fast-sd-card-backup-cloning-and-auto-expanding-restore", "title": "Fast SD-Card Backup, Cloning, And Auto-Expanding Restore", "text": "\u003cp>Configuring Raspberry Pi OS, Wi-Fi, SSH keys, UART overlays, device groups, and camera tools from scratch takes real effort\u2014and microSD cards at an airfield occasionally get lost, soaked, or corrupted right before flight day. Sensoraia ships two Linux PC helpers in \u003ccode>build-support/\u003c/code> that turn your finished \"golden\" MORA card into a small archive you can clone onto spare cards in minutes:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>Why this saves so much time: A plain whole-card \u003ccode>dd\u003c/code> reads every single   empty gigabyte on a 32 GB, 64 GB, or 128 GB card. Instead,   \u003ccode>build-support/fast_sd_backup.sh\u003c/code> inspects the partition table (\u003ccode>sfdisk\u003c/code>),   reads only up to the last sector actually used by your partitions, shows a   live progress bar and ETA (\u003ccode>pv\u003c/code>), and compresses across all CPU cores in   parallel (\u003ccode>xz -1 -T0\u003c/code>). When restoring or cloning onto a new card,   \u003ccode>build-support/fast_sd_restore_and_expand.sh\u003c/code> streams the image straight to   hardware and automatically expands the EXT4 root partition to 100% of the   destination card (\u003ccode>parted\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>e2fsck\u003c/code> + \u003ccode>resize2fs\u003c/code>) before you even eject   it\u2014no extra resize dance on MORA needed!\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Install the standard Linux disk utilities once on your PC if needed (\u003ccode>sudo apt install pv xz-utils parted e2fsprogs util-linux\u003c/code>), shut MORA down cleanly, and insert its microSD card into your PC.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Back up your configured MORA card (confirm the whole-disk device name    such as \u003ccode>/dev/sdX\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/dev/mmcblk0\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>lsblk\u003c/code>\u2014never a partition like    \u003ccode>/dev/sdX1\u003c/code>):\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN,MOUNTPOINTS\nsudo bash build-support/fast_sd_backup.sh /dev/sdX /path/to/mora-golden.img.xz\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003col> \u003cli>Clone or restore onto another microSD card (at least as large as the    used partition boundary; this erases the entire target card, so double-    check \u003ccode>lsblk\u003c/code> so your laptop's own SSD stays safe and happy):\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,MODEL,TRAN,MOUNTPOINTS\nsudo bash build-support/fast_sd_restore_and_expand.sh \\\n  /path/to/mora-golden.img.xz /dev/sdY\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Wait until the script prints \u003ccode>==&gt; Restore and expansion complete!\u003c/code> before removing the card. Keep two cloned cards in your flight box and future-you will thank present-you. (For flashing a brand-new blank OS for the very first time, Raspberry Pi Imager remains the easiest starting point.)\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html#configure-uart-for-usher", "title": "Configure UART for USHER", "text": "\u003cp>On MORA, disable Bluetooth and dedicate the full UART to USHER. Run the following on MORA, then reboot:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo nano /boot/firmware/config.txt\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Under the existing \u003ccode>[all]\u003c/code> section in \u003ccode>config.txt\u003c/code>, add:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>enable_uart=1\ndtoverlay=disable-bt\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Save the file, then open \u003ccode>raspi-config\u003c/code> to turn off the Linux login prompt on that serial port (an autopilot and a Linux login prompt make terrible conversation partners):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo raspi-config\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>In \u003ccode>raspi-config\u003c/code>, select Interface Options &gt; Serial Port, answer No to the login shell, and Yes to serial hardware, then reboot:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo reboot\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>After reboot, log back in and verify the UART device mapping and group membership:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ls -l /dev/serial0 /dev/ttyAMA0\nid -nG\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Expected mapping: \u003ccode>/dev/serial0 -&gt; ttyAMA0\u003c/code>. The account running USHER must belong to \u003ccode>dialout\u003c/code>; if \u003ccode>dialout\u003c/code> is not listed yet, add it once:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo usermod -aG dialout air\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Log out and back in so the new group membership takes effect.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html#preventing-sleep-and-suspend-states", "title": "Preventing Sleep and Suspend States", "text": "\u003cp>Raspberry Pi OS normally does not suspend a headless Pi. If a board is managed by software that might request suspend, disable those targets explicitly:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo systemctl mask sleep.target suspend.target hibernate.target hybrid-sleep.target\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>If you do not like the change for whatever reason, to reverse the change later, run:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo systemctl unmask sleep.target suspend.target hibernate.target hybrid-sleep.target\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html#save-multiple-wi-fi-networks", "title": "Save Multiple Wi-Fi Networks", "text": "\u003cp>Raspberry Pi OS Trixie uses NetworkManager. You can save more than one Wi-Fi profile so MORA can connect to a known network when it is available. Configure each profile for a network you expect MORA to use in the field.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Check the Wi-Fi interface name on MORA:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>nmcli device status\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>It is usually \u003ccode>wlan0\u003c/code>. Add each network, replacing the example SSIDs and profile names. \u003ccode>--ask\u003c/code> prompts for a password instead of putting it in shell history or the process argument list:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo nmcli --ask device wifi connect \"Desk SSID\" ifname wlan0 name \"Desk WiFi\"\nsudo nmcli --ask device wifi connect \"Field SSID\" ifname wlan0 name \"Field WiFi\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>NetworkManager saves each profile. To prefer one when it chooses among available autoconnect profiles, assign it a higher priority (the valid range is -999 to 999):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo nmcli connection modify \"Desk WiFi\" connection.autoconnect-priority 20\nsudo nmcli connection modify \"Field WiFi\" connection.autoconnect-priority 10\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>A higher priority is considered first when NetworkManager selects a profile to autoconnect; it does not disconnect an already active network just to switch to a higher-priority one. Check saved and active profiles with:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>nmcli connection show\nnmcli connection show --active\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>For a network that does not broadcast its SSID, add \u003ccode>hidden yes\u003c/code> to the connect command and provide the SSID explicitly. Hiding an SSID is not a security measure and can cause clients to probe for that network:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudo nmcli --ask device wifi connect \"Hidden SSID\" ifname wlan0 name \"Hidden WiFi\" hidden yes\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "setup-os-rpi-zero-2w.html#add-wi-fi-before-first-boot", "title": "Add Wi-Fi Before First Boot", "text": "\u003cp>Use this recovery path when MORA has no network connection and you cannot reach it over SSH. It requires a Linux development PC with an \u003ccode>nmcli\u003c/code> version that supports \u003ccode>--offline\u003c/code>, plus a way to read the microSD card. The saved profile contains the Wi-Fi password, so keep the card secure.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Shut MORA down, remove the microSD card, and attach it to the Linux PC. Mount     the card's Linux root filesystem partition (typically the larger ext4     partition), not its small FAT boot partition. Set \u003ccode>ROOT\u003c/code> below to that     partition's actual mount point; mount locations vary by PC and desktop.\u003c/li> \u003cli> \u003cp>Generate a WPA2-Personal profile and install it with the permissions     NetworkManager requires. Replace the example network name in both places.     This command deliberately does not put the password on the command line:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ROOT=/path/to/mounted/rootfs\nnmcli --offline connection add \\\n   type wifi ifname '*' con-name 'Field WiFi' ssid 'Field WiFi' \\\n   wifi-sec.key-mgmt wpa-psk connection.autoconnect yes \\\n   ipv4.method auto ipv6.method auto |\nsudo install -D -o root -g root -m 600 /dev/stdin \\\n   \"$ROOT/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/Field-WiFi.nmconnection\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003c/li> \u003cli> \u003cp>Edit the generated profile as root:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>sudoedit \"$ROOT/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/Field-WiFi.nmconnection\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>In its existing \u003ccode>[wifi-security]\u003c/code> section, add \u003ccode>psk=YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD\u003c/code> on a new line. Save the file. Use the actual password, not the placeholder. A WPA2 passphrase is normally 8 to 63 characters; a 64-character hexadecimal key is also accepted. 1. Safely unmount/eject the card from the PC, return it to MORA, and power on    the board. Allow time for it to join the access point and obtain an address.    Connect using the hostname configured in Imager. If mDNS does not resolve    that name, find MORA in the router's DHCP client list and connect using its    IP address.\u003c/p> \u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>After regaining access, use the previous section to add other Wi-Fi networks.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html", "title": "Spottercam: Look For Vehicles", "text": "\u003cp>Spottercam turns your airborne camera into a patient lookout. It inspects each captured photograph with a vehicle detector, keeps the original image untouched, and\u2014when combined with aircraft pose and camera geometry\u2014projects spotted vehicles onto the ground and ranks the five best finds as waypoints T1 to T5.\u003c/p> \u003cp>You do not need an airfield or a borrowed firetruck to try it. Start with a single saved picture or the interactive PC flight simulation, where two French 67-CCF firetrucks are parked on the map for your aircraft to discover, and move to MORA and the physical IMX500 AI Camera when you are ready to fly.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Detection terms | AI Camera setup | Paparazzi setup\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#live-vehicle-search-detect-and-respond", "title": "Live Vehicle Search: Detect And Respond", "text": "\u003cp>Think of airborne vehicle search as three calm, well-separated jobs:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Capture a clear view along with where the aircraft was and how it was    tilted at that moment.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Detect vehicles in the image and record their bounding boxes, subpixel    centres and confidence scores in a sidecar file (\u003ccode>*.objects.ini\u003c/code>) without    scribbling over the original photograph.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Project and rank the top five targets (\u003ccode>T1..T5\u003c/code>) on the ground while    leaving the flight plan in complete charge of where the aircraft actually    flies.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>A bounding box in a photograph is an observation, not a steering command. Even when Spottercam spots a bright red firetruck with high confidence, your flight plan keeps flying its survey rather than diving uninvited toward the truck.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#automatic-model-selection", "title": "Automatic Model Selection", "text": "\u003cp>One settings file\u2014\u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_settings.ini\u003c/code>\u2014serves both real flights on MORA and simulated flights on your PC:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\ninput_source=live\nmodel=models/nn_vehicles.rpk\nscore_threshold=0.6\nclass_index=0\nmax_detections=5\nonnx_threads=1\ninference_timeout_ms=2000\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Why point to \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code> when your laptop does not have an IMX500 sensor wired to its motherboard? Because \u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\u003c/code> knows the difference:\u003c/p> \u003cul> \u003cli>On MORA with the physical IMX500 AI Camera, Spottercam uploads   \u003ccode>models/nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code> directly into the camera's onboard neural processor.\u003c/li> \u003cli>On a PC in simulation or saved-picture analysis, Spottercam automatically   checks \u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/models/\u003c/code> for the matching ONNX exports   (\u003ccode>vehicle_yolo11n_int8.onnx\u003c/code> first, then \u003ccode>vehicle_yolo11n_fp32.onnx\u003c/code>) with   their \u003ccode>.ini\u003c/code> descriptors and runs whichever ONNX model the CPU runtime   supports.\u003c/li> \u003c/ul> \u003cp>Paths inside the INI are relative to the INI file itself. See choose a model before substituting another export.\u003c/p> Setting Purpose \u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\u003c/code> Use IMX500 on hardware for \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code>, or probe the sibling CPU \u003ccode>.onnx\u003c/code> models on PC \u003ccode>input_source=live\u003c/code> Capture new images from the camera rather than analyse a single fixed file \u003ccode>model\u003c/code> Select the primary model file; ONNX exports also pair with a same-name \u003ccode>.ini\u003c/code> descriptor \u003ccode>score_threshold\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>class_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_detections\u003c/code> Set the minimum confidence (\u003ccode>0.6\u003c/code>), class (\u003ccode>0\u003c/code>) and box cap (\u003ccode>5\u003c/code>) for CPU ONNX detection \u003ccode>onnx_threads\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>inference_timeout_ms\u003c/code> Control CPU inference threads and the per-photo timeout in milliseconds \u003cp>The supplied IMX500 RPK decoder uses class 0 (\u003ccode>vehicle\u003c/code>), a \u003ccode>0.6\u003c/code> confidence threshold and at most five boxes per image. Model choice and detector settings are ignored when you run in photo-only mode.\u003c/p> PC Demo Assignment What Happens Compatible ONNX export present (direct or sibling of \u003ccode>.rpk\u003c/code>) Real neural inference on every simulated camera photograph Unset/missing graph without a sibling ONNX export Explicitly labelled seeded random spotting Present but corrupt/incompatible graph or descriptor Clear startup error\u2014never silent random substitution Valid inference with no vehicles in view Zero detections (\u003ccode>count=0\u003c/code>), recorded cleanly as an empty frame \u003cp>Random spotting only exists as a fallback when no ONNX model is installed. The normal runtime never invents imaginary vehicles to cover up a broken model.\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#try-a-single-saved-picture-first", "title": "Try A Single Saved Picture First", "text": "\u003cp>Want to verify that the detector works on your PC in ten seconds flat, before starting a flight simulator? Run:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make -j32 spottercam\nbin/spottercam --check-model --settings modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_settings.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>To analyse any \u003ccode>.jpg\u003c/code> photograph on disk (using a fresh copy so no old \u003ccode>.objects.ini\u003c/code> sidecar is in the way):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cp resources/mock_image_01.jpg /tmp/test-spotter.jpg\nrm -f /tmp/test-spotter.objects.ini\nbin/spottercam --analyze /tmp/test-spotter.jpg \\\n  --settings modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_settings.ini --sequence 1\ncat /tmp/test-spotter.objects.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This runs real CPU ONNX inference without needing a camera attached. Your input JPEG is never re-encoded, and its SHA-256 hash is recorded inside \u003ccode>/tmp/test-spotter.objects.ini\u003c/code> so detections can never be mixed up with another picture. Build with \u003ccode>CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\u003c/code> only on machines where you intentionally skip camera support.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#five-target-mock-survey", "title": "Five-Target Mock Survey", "text": "\u003cp>In the local simulation, your aircraft flies the survey in \u003ccode>examples/paparazzi/conf/flight_plans/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_spotter_cam_demo.xml\u003c/code> using the IMX500 camera lens profile (\u003ccode>examples/pangea/aicam-2028.ini\u003c/code>) and native C USHER (\u003ccode>--spottercam --spottercam-targets\u003c/code>). On each wings-level shot, USHER captures a mock vehicle scene (\u003ccode>resources/mock_aicam_01.jpg\u003c/code>), runs the native YOLO11n ONNX detector in \u003ccode>bin/spottercam\u003c/code>, projects detected vehicles onto the ground from the aircraft's pose, and sends ranked target updates for waypoints T1..T5 directly over the simulated UART (\u003ccode>digital_cam_point\u003c/code>).\u003c/p> \u003cp>Follow these steps to fly the mission manually from takeoff to landing in Paparazzi Center\u2014no physical camera, MORA board, or Python helper is needed:\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#1-build-native-pc-binaries-and-install-paparazzi-links", "title": "1. Build Native PC Binaries And Install Paparazzi Links", "text": "\u003cp>From the Sensoraia root (with Paparazzi installed in \u003ccode>~/paparazzi\u003c/code>):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make usher soda spottercam\n./kickstart.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#2-launch-paparazzi-center-build-nps-and-execute-the-session", "title": "2. Launch Paparazzi Center, Build NPS, And Execute The Session", "text": "\u003cp>Start Paparazzi Center from your Paparazzi directory:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>cd ~/paparazzi\nMORA_UART_DEVICE=\"\" ./paparazzi\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>(Setting \u003ccode>MORA_UART_DEVICE=\"\"\u003c/code> forces PC-only simulation even if an FTDI cable is plugged in.)\u003c/p> \u003cp>In Paparazzi Center:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Set Config to \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_conf.xml\u003c/code> and    Control panel to \u003ccode>userconf/SENSORAIA/sensoraia_demo_control_panel.xml\u003c/code>    (\u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code> selects both by default).\u003c/li> \u003cli>Select aircraft \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code> and target \u003ccode>nps\u003c/code>, then click    Build.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Open the Session tab, choose \u003ccode>Sensoraia Spottercam NPS\u003c/code>, and click    Execute (starting \u003ccode>Sensoraia USHER\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Sensoraia NPS\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Data Link\u003c/code>,    \u003ccode>Server\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>GCS Classic\u003c/code>).\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#3-take-off-watch-t1t5-update-and-land", "title": "3. Take Off, Watch T1\u2013T5 Update, And Land", "text": "\u003cp>In GCS Classic:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Wait a few seconds until the aircraft icon appears and GPS shows a valid 3D    fix (\u003ccode>Wait GPS\u003c/code> switches to \u003ccode>Holding point\u003c/code>).\u003c/li> \u003cli>Click Takeoff in the GCS strip.\u003c/li> \u003cli>The aircraft climbs, spirals down at \u003ccode>SOUTHWEST\u003c/code> to the $44\\text{ m}$ AGL    survey height, and enters Survey (you can also click Find vehicle at    any time from Standby).\u003c/li> \u003cli>Every three seconds on wings-level survey legs (bank angle within 12\u00b0), the    autopilot triggers a shot. USHER runs native YOLO11n ONNX detection in    \u003ccode>bin/spottercam\u003c/code>, ranks confidence (\u003ccode>0..1000\u003c/code>) across the five target slots    (once all five slots are full, a strictly higher score replaces the lowest    one), and transmits correlated \u003ccode>USHER_CAM_POINT_UPDATE\u003c/code> messages over the    simulated UART so the flight plan moves T1..T5 onto the projected    coordinates using the ordinary GCS waypoint-update mechanism (\u003ccode>WP_MOVED\u003c/code>).\u003c/li> \u003cli>After the 160-second survey completes, the aircraft automatically flies its    baseleg, final glide, and flare to touchdown. Stop all session programs in    Center when finished.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#4-inspect-your-saved-photos-and-detections", "title": "4. Inspect Your Saved Photos And Detections", "text": "\u003cp>Check \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code> after the flight:\u003c/p> Saved File Or Folder What You Will Find Inside \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/ma*.jpg\u003c/code> Saved mock camera JPEGs tagged with EXIF flight pose and Spottercam detection summary \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/ma*.objects.ini\u003c/code> Validated \u003ccode>USHER_OBJECTS_V2\u003c/code> sidecars with bounding boxes, centres, scores, and SHA-256 bindings \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/detections/\u003c/code> Convenience copies of photographs containing accepted vehicle detections \u003cp>Locating real image boxes requires pose and camera projection. The supplied projection profile is illustrative, not a measured calibration. Unusable geometry (such as a steep bank during a U-turn) does not authorize moving a waypoint, and a valid model can find fewer than five objects or none.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#use-a-physical-camera", "title": "Use A Physical Camera", "text": "\u003cp>When you move from your desk to the airfield with a real vehicle on the ground, Spottercam runs on MORA with the Raspberry Pi AI Camera (IMX500) and executes \u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/models/nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code> directly on the camera's neural chip.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#1-check-the-camera-and-model-selection-on-your-pc", "title": "1. Check The Camera And Model Selection On Your PC", "text": "\u003cp>Spottercam is already the default camera (\u003ccode>CAMERA = spottercam\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code>), which configures MORA's \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> with:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ExecStart=/home/air/sensoraia/usher --spottercam\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>And \u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_settings.ini\u003c/code> already selects \u003ccode>model=models/nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>inference_backend=auto\u003c/code>.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Whenever you want MORA to boot with a different sensor instead of Spottercam, edit \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> (or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code>\u2014no service-file editing required):\u003c/p> Desired MORA Startup Mode \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code> Setting Spottercam (default: IMX500 + \u003ccode>nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code>) \u003ccode>CAMERA=spottercam\u003c/code> AIcam (IMX500 native photos, no model) \u003ccode>CAMERA=aicam\u003c/code> LWIRcam (Tiny1-C / TDK USB thermal camera) \u003ccode>CAMERA=lwircam\u003c/code> CHDKbridge (Canon USB camera with CHDK) \u003ccode>CAMERA=chdk\u003c/code> EARcam (USB microphone acoustic search) \u003ccode>CAMERA=earcam\u003c/code> LOOMcam (visual homing framework on IMX500) \u003ccode>CAMERA=loomcam\u003c/code> Diplacam (native GP/diffusion field planner) \u003ccode>CAMERA=diplacam\u003c/code> \u003cp>Spottercam, AIcam and LOOMcam are alternative owners of the same physical IMX500 ribbon camera\u2014pick one at a time. To package a custom Spottercam settings file and its model, pass \u003ccode>MORA_SPOTTERCAM_SETTINGS=/absolute/path/spottercam_settings.ini\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code>. See deploy and select a profile and model selection.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#2-build-and-deploy-to-mora", "title": "2. Build And Deploy To MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Prepare the AI Camera hardware (connect the ribbon cable with power off). Then, from the Sensoraia root on your PC, build and deploy the release to MORA:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make all\nmake mora-deploy MORA_SSH_TARGET=air@theatre.local MORA_INSTALL_DIR=/home/air/sensoraia\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Confirm that \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> is active and running \u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local 'systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service'\nssh air@theatre.local 'journalctl -u usher.service -n 50 --no-pager'\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#3-perform-a-bench-or-flight-line-check-before-takeoff", "title": "3. Perform A Bench Or Flight-Line Check Before Takeoff", "text": "\u003cp>Before launching the aircraft, verify that the IMX500 camera and \u003ccode>nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code> capture and analyse a real frame cleanly (discovering a lens cap on the bench is much nicer than discovering it at $44\\text{ m}$).\u003c/p> \u003cp>Either trigger a single camera-2 shot from your connected flight controller (or FTDI desk setup), or run a standalone check on MORA by stopping the service briefly, taking one test photo, and starting the service again:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/spottercam --probe /home/air/sensoraia/models/ai_cam/nn_vehicles.rpk\nbin/spottercam --capture /home/air/sensoraia/models/ai_cam/nn_vehicles.rpk /home/air/Pictures/bench-check.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Initial RPK upload to the IMX500 takes a few seconds longer than subsequent shots. Always use a new output filename and restart \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> after a standalone test so USHER owns the camera for flight.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#4-fly-the-real-survey-and-inspect-captured-evidence", "title": "4. Fly The Real Survey And Inspect Captured Evidence", "text": "\u003col> \u003cli>Flash or configure your autopilot with \u003ccode>digital_cam_uart\u003c/code> and the survey    flight plan (\u003ccode>sensoraia_spotter_cam_demo.xml\u003c/code>, adjusted for your airfield's    coordinates and safe altitude).\u003c/li> \u003cli>Connect the flight-controller UART to MORA (\u003ccode>/dev/serial0\u003c/code>, 115200 baud,    3.3 V logic, crossed TX/RX and common ground) and power the system.\u003c/li> \u003cli>After takeoff, enter Find vehicle (\u003ccode>Start demo\u003c/code> $\\rightarrow$ \u003ccode>Survey\u003c/code>).    On each periodic trigger, USHER captures a $2028 \\times 1520$ JPEG and    on-sensor \u003ccode>nn_vehicles.rpk\u003c/code> detections, writes synchronized flight EXIF    (\u003ccode>SENSOR02\u003c/code> frame timing, GPS, attitude, and detection summary), and copies    any photograph with a vehicle hit into \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/detections/\u003c/code>    while SODA writes annotated review crops.\u003c/li> \u003cli>After landing, retrieve the original \u003ccode>a*.jpg\u003c/code> photos, \u003ccode>a*.objects.ini\u003c/code>    sidecars, and \u003ccode>detections/\u003c/code> copies from \u003ccode>/home/air/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#optional-native-photo-only-mode-on-mora", "title": "Optional: Native Photo-Only Mode On MORA", "text": "\u003cp>If you want fast native IMX500 photographs without loading a model or running detection, simply deploy with \u003ccode>CAMERA=aicam\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>make mora-deploy CAMERA=aicam\u003c/code>), or run \u003ccode>--spottercam --native-photo\u003c/code> in a standalone session.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Repackage and deploy after changing the line. \u003ccode>--native-photo\u003c/code> needs no model, cannot be combined with \u003ccode>--spottercam-settings\u003c/code>, and saves a $2028 \\times 1520$ quality-90 JPEG with sensor timing evidence but no detection metadata or SODA analysis. For a standalone photo-only check on MORA from \u003ccode>/home/air/sensoraia\u003c/code> (with \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> stopped):\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bin/spottercam --photo-only --probe\nbin/spottercam --photo-only --capture /home/air/Pictures/spotter-photo.jpg\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Restore \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> afterward. See native photographs and the camera speed test for timing and throughput options.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#use-lwir-photos-future-multi-sensor-option", "title": "Use LWIR Photos (Future &amp; Multi-Sensor Option)", "text": "\u003cp>LWIRcam can remain the sole Tiny1C camera owner while a separate persistent Spottercam CPU worker analyses each completed thermal JPEG. Its assignment is independent of the AI Camera assignment. Supply an ONNX detector trained and independently evaluated on representative Tiny1C imagery, plus its matching \u003ccode>spotter_boxes_xyxy_v1\u003c/code> descriptor. The supplied \u003ccode>modules/spotter_cam/spottercam_lwir_settings.ini\u003c/code> is a template, not a trained thermal model. Keep the inference image rendering consistent with training. This includes JPEG quality, which normal LWIR captures now default to 95. Legends are rejected and missing real temperature/frame evidence is an error.\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make usher spottercam CAMERA_CAPTURE=0\nbin/spottercam --check-model --settings /absolute/lwir-detector.ini\n./usher --lwircam --lwir-spotter-settings /absolute/lwir-detector.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Capture and flight EXIF complete before inference enters the existing bounded saved-file queue. One warm detector retains the model; source images and temperature planes are never rewritten. Overflow retains originals for offline analysis. Detector errors never discard a photo or reuse an earlier detection. SODA retries do not repeat object inference. This is asynchronous per-photo analysis, not a promise to analyse every camera stream frame.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Each accepted result has an \u003ccode>.objects.ini\u003c/code> model record and an additional \u003ccode>.lwir-objects.ini\u003c/code> record binding the photo number, frame/boot identity, source and model hashes, native subpixel centres and nearest-pixel temperatures. Invalid endpoint temperature codes are reported unavailable, not converted to Celsius. A valid zero-detection result is different from failed analysis. Results are local evidence; this option does not move aircraft waypoints or add new flight-controller messages. Existing SODA AI-camera EXIF summaries are not automatically written into these unchanged LWIR originals.\u003c/p> \u003cp>To enable geographic estimates, additionally supply a measured native 256x192 \u003ccode>PANGEA_CAMERA_V1\u003c/code> profile with the \u003ccode>l\u003c/code> prefix, correct intrinsics and mounting:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>./usher --lwircam --lwir-spotter-settings /absolute/lwir-detector.ini \\\n  --lwir-spotter-profile /absolute/measured-lwir-camera.ini \\\n  --lwir-spotter-limits 5000,1\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The limits are maximum exposure-interval width in microseconds and maximum pose uncertainty in metres. Defaults are 5000 us and 1 m, not measured accuracy. Projection requires an exposure-qualified aligned pose belonging to the exact frame. Frame arrival alone, missing pose/calibration, broad timing or excessive uncertainty leaves detections and temperatures usable but omits target latitude and longitude. Use the existing clock alignment and measured \u003ccode>--lwir-latency-ms MIN,MAX\u003c/code> facilities where applicable; never guess latency or relax limits merely to obtain coordinates. All three aircraft attitude axes and camera mounting rotations are applied through shared geometry. Detector centres retain subpixel precision; only temperature sampling rounds to a native pixel. Ground projection assumes a local flat surface and a calibrated pinhole camera. Lens distortion, terrain, target elevation and physical timing/calibration errors still require independent field validation; extra decimal places do not prove pinpoint accuracy. Temperatures remain device-reported and uncalibrated.\u003c/p> \u003cp>An opt-in package includes the detector and chosen model without changing the ordinary LWIR-only default:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>make mora-package MORA_PROFILE=lwircam \\\n  MORA_LWIR_SPOTTER_SETTINGS=/absolute/lwir-detector.ini\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>The package enables saved-photo detection but does not invent a projection calibration. Deploy separately only when the selected trained model is ready. Measure latency and total shared 512 MB board headroom with the real model.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#keep-the-evidence", "title": "Keep The Evidence", "text": "\u003cp>USHER stores the selected detection summary with the original JPEG's flight metadata. SODA may make annotated/cropped review copies and a detection log. The original flight photo remains undecorated.\u003c/p> \u003cp>A bounding box is an image result, not automatically a geographic target. Where a valid target is integrated, the flight plan still decides whether to visit it. Confidence, target freshness and geographic accuracy are different questions.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#supply-a-different-vehicle-model", "title": "Supply A Different Vehicle Model", "text": "\u003cp>An arbitrary YOLO export or unrelated RPK is not automatically compatible. Keep the class mapping, preprocessing and output contract with the export. If using a Vehicles dataset, retain its exact source/version, permitted use and independent evaluation. The filename does not identify the dataset release or establish detector accuracy.\u003c/p> \u003cp>MIMIC views or saved photos may support external evaluation where imagery rights permit. Choose views representative of the camera, add independent labels and separate evaluation data. A north-up map or annotated SODA crop is not automatically a ready-made training dataset.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "spottercam.html#troubleshooting", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Symptom What To Check First \u003ccode>local mode is already running\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> busy Stop the previous Center session or remove \u003ccode>/tmp/usher-sim\u003c/code> after confirming no USHER is running Want hardware-in-the-loop over FTDI instead of PC USHER Select the Sensoraia MORA desk NPS session in Paparazzi Center (see the desk-test guide) \u003ccode>--mock-map needs zoom-18 XYZ JPEGs\u003c/code> Pan over the survey area at zoom 18 in \u003ccode>PprzGCS\u003c/code> so \u003ccode>~/.local/share/paparazzi/pprzgcs/map/GOOGLE/18\u003c/code> is populated Photos saved (\u003ccode>ma*.jpg\u003c/code>), but \u003ccode>T1..T5\u003c/code> stay at \u003ccode>HOME\u003c/code> Check the USHER console and \u003ccode>*.objects.ini\u003c/code> sidecars; shots during steep turns (bank angle 12\u00b0 or more) are skipped by the flight plan Real MORA camera busy Ensure only one IMX500 owner (\u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>--loomcam\u003c/code>) is running; stop \u003ccode>usher.service\u003c/code> before standalone \u003ccode>--probe\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--capture\u003c/code> \u003cp>Model errors stop fresh target publication; existing points are not proof of new observations. Check the selected assignment and worker log before restarting. A successful image with zero detections is not an analysis failure. For hardware problems, check camera ownership, cabling and runtime packages. Validate camera geometry, timing and loaded MORA resources separately before relying on real-flight locations.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html", "title": "Capture With USHER", "text": "\u003cp>USHER connects an aircraft's capture requests to the selected sensors on MORA. It saves the observation, keeps the available flight information with it, and reports the outcome. You can try the same flow on a Linux PC before connecting hardware.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Install and get started | Glossary | Simulated flights\u003c/p> \u003cp>\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#compute-board-baseline", "title": "Compute-Board Baseline", "text": "\u003cp>MORA is the onboard-computer role, not a Raspberry Pi model. Sensoraia starts from the Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W as a deliberately constrained 512 MB ARM64 reference. The current ready-made cross-build and deployment workflow targets 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Trixie and Cortex-A53. A different compute board can host Sensoraia when its OS/ABI, drivers, peripherals and resource budget are compatible; it may need a board-specific build profile, package and service configuration. The Pi baseline is not proof that every module fits or runs unchanged on every board.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#choose-a-camera", "title": "Choose A Camera", "text": "\u003cp>You never need to edit systemd unit templates by hand to switch sensors. Pick your camera in whichever way matches how you are running Sensoraia:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>For MORA deployment &amp; local standalone runs: Set \u003ccode>CAMERA = &lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> in    \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> at the repository root, or pass \u003ccode>CAMERA=&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> on the    command line (\u003ccode>make mora-deploy CAMERA=lwircam\u003c/code>, or    \u003ccode>bash examples/paparazzi/run_local_demo.sh --camera lwircam\u003c/code>).\u003c/li> \u003cli>For Paparazzi Center simulation: Select the matching aircraft and    session in the GUI after running \u003ccode>./kickstart.sh\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> value USHER CLI Flag Paparazzi Center Aircraft &amp; Session Use It For Start Here \u003ccode>spottercam\u003c/code> (default) \u003ccode>--spottercam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Easystar_3_SPOTTERCAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia Spottercam NPS Visible IMX500 photos + vehicle detection (\u003ccode>T1..T5\u003c/code>) Spottercam \u003ccode>aicam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--aicam --native-photo\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>--aicam\u003c/code>) \u003ccode>Easystar_3_AICAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia AIcam NPS Plain Raspberry Pi AI Camera photographs AI Camera \u003ccode>lwircam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--lwircam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LWIRCAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia LWIRcam NPS Tiny1-C / TDK USB thermal capture LWIRcam \u003ccode>chdk\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--chdk\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Easystar_3_CHDKCAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia CHDKcam NPS Canon compact camera running CHDK over USB PTP CHDKbridge \u003ccode>earcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--earcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Easystar_3_EARCAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia EARcam NPS Microphone observations and acoustic search EARcam \u003ccode>loomcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--loomcam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Easystar_3_LOOMCAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia LOOMcam NPS Experimental visual-return observations LOOMcam \u003ccode>diplacam\u003c/code> \u003ccode>--diplacam-config FILE\u003c/code> \u003ccode>Bebop_DIPLACAM\u003c/code> / Sensoraia Diplacam NPS Experimental 8-waypoint rotorcraft planner Diplacam \u003cp>AIcam, Spottercam and LOOMcam are alternative owners of the same physical IMX500 camera; do not start them together. A USB microphone and a supported thermal camera are separate devices, but their combined resource use still needs checking on MORA. \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=lwircam\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>MORA_PROFILE=diplacam\u003c/code> remain available when you want a smaller single-sensor MORA package instead of the full-suite package. Selecting a backend does not install board OS camera packages or physical calibration files.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#building-and-deploying-usher", "title": "Building And Deploying USHER", "text": "\u003cp>Follow Build on your PC for local binaries and Deploy to MORA for cross-building, packaging and activation. The root \u003ccode>make help\u003c/code> lists the supported commands.\u003c/p> \u003cp>After installing the PC prerequisites, \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> prepares its missing SDKs and source dependencies, builds every PC and ARM64 application, and checks the complete executable list. The same command works after \u003ccode>make clean\u003c/code>; no private camera-library checkout or manual SDK recovery is required.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Building and packaging do not contact MORA. Activation is a separate step that restarts USHER. Keep model/settings changes with the release you deploy.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#start-here-local-simulation", "title": "Start Here: Local Simulation", "text": "\u003cp>For a full simulated flight, use the Paparazzi demo walkthrough. Its Center sessions start the sensor companion, simulator and ground tools together. Do not start another USHER beside a session that already owns it.\u003c/p> \u003cp>For a terminal-only camera peer, use:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>bash examples/paparazzi/run_local_demo.sh\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>This starts local mock capture, not a complete flight. Leave the terminal open while your simulated aircraft connects. Stop it with Ctrl+C afterwards. MIMIC supplies views of cached terrain when you want changing camera imagery instead of a fixed test picture.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#deploy-to-mora", "title": "Deploy To MORA", "text": "\u003cp>Complete the installation and activation steps, then check the selected sensors on the bench. A running service means the program started; it does not establish that a camera is focused, a microphone gain is suitable or a geographic estimate is accurate.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#when-sudo-requires-your-password", "title": "When Sudo Requires Your Password", "text": "\u003cp>For completely hands-free \u003ccode>make mora-deploy\u003c/code> runs with zero password prompts, you can configure passwordless \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> on MORA or create \u003ccode>/home/air/pass.txt\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>chmod 600 /home/air/pass.txt\u003c/code>, replacing \u003ccode>replacewithyourpass\u003c/code> with your MORA \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> password) as shown in the desk-test automation guide.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If you prefer interactive \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> and upload succeeded before activation reported that \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> needs a password, connect with \u003ccode>ssh air@theatre.local\u003c/code>. In that same MORA terminal, substitute the exact staging path printed by your upload:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>stage=/home/air/sensoraia/.deploy.XXXXXXXX\n[[ -s \"$stage/deploy_mora_remote.sh\" ]] &amp;&amp; sudo -v &amp;&amp; \\\n   bash \"$stage/deploy_mora_remote.sh\" /home/air/sensoraia \"$stage\"\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Use your installation directory/account. \u003ccode>sudo -v\u003c/code> authenticates subsequent privileged operations in this terminal; another SSH session may not share it. This resumes the uploaded release's normal activation path, so no new build/upload is needed merely to enter a password.\u003c/p> \u003cp>If activation fails, inspect the reported error and service journal before trying again. Keep the previous release and the deployment's backup until you have confirmed the new one works.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#start-here-physical-desk-test-with-mora", "title": "Start Here: Physical Desk Test With MORA", "text": "\u003cp>For a laptop running NPS connected by FTDI, use the illustrated desk-test guide. It explains the cable's 5 V power output, crossed 3.3 V UART, Center session and mock-to-real sequence.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Connect the FC-to-MORA UART with the documented voltage levels and a common ground. Camera and microphone connections go to MORA, not to that UART.\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Check the selected sensor by following its standalone capture/listening    guide. Stop any existing owner before opening the same device separately.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Restore or start the reviewed USHER service configuration.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Send one capture request through the connected flight controller.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Check the saved file and the completion result, not just a GCS photo dot.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Inspect focus, expected image dimensions and the available flight metadata.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol> \u003cp>For service status and recent diagnostics:\u003c/p> \u003cpre>\u003ccode>systemctl --no-pager --full status usher.service\njournalctl -u usher.service -n 50 --no-pager\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre> \u003cp>Run these on MORA, either in an SSH session or a local terminal. Take one sensor at a time through this check before combining workloads.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#find-your-results", "title": "Find Your Results", "text": "\u003cp>USHER's usual photo directory is \u003ccode>~/Pictures/sensoraia/\u003c/code> for the account running it. The service account and any explicit output settings determine the actual location. A simulation companion prints its own session directory.\u003c/p> \u003cp>Keep original JPEGs. SODA can make separate annotated/cropped review images; PANGEA can make maps from accepted originals. Neither is a reason to replace the original capture with an edited copy.\u003c/p> What You See What It Means A GCS shot marker A trigger was admitted; inspect completion to confirm a saved image A saved, readable JPEG Image capture completed; optional analysis may still fail Zero accepted detections A successful model run may have found no accepted objects An invalid or missing point Do not treat it as a new geographic target A map or sound picture A derived result; retain its source observations too"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#position-and-timing", "title": "Position And Timing", "text": "\u003cp>For measured still-image throughput on MORA or a PC, follow the Camera Speed Test. Sensor frame rate and saved-image throughput are different measurements.\u003c/p> \u003cp>The aircraft keeps moving between a request and the actual measurement. Sensoraia records available pose and timing evidence, but not every camera provides the same exposure information. A request-time geotag must not be mistaken for a demonstrated exposure-time position.\u003c/p> \u003cp>See image metadata for what to inspect and the glossary for pose, latency and uncertainty. Use measured camera geometry and mounting when turning image positions into ground locations.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#hotspot-gps-coordinates-and-center-temperatures", "title": "Hotspot GPS Coordinates And Center Temperatures", "text": "\u003cp>Thermal geolocation needs a supported temperature plane, enabled analysis, camera calibration, mounting information and a suitable observation pose. Follow the thermal calibration guide before relying on projected locations. Image-only thermal capture cannot supply missing temperature measurements.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#lwir-integration-and-effective-lag", "title": "LWIR Integration And Effective Lag", "text": "\u003cp>Capture delay, pose timing and camera mounting are separate sources of error. A sharper display or a timing label alone does not validate them. Use the thermal guide's independent target checks, and inspect the recorded timing information rather than assuming the request and exposure coincide.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#spottercam", "title": "Spottercam", "text": "\u003cp>Use the Spottercam guide for model selection and same-image detections. A box in image coordinates needs valid projection and a flight-plan integration before it becomes a geographic target.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#earcam-acoustic-backend", "title": "EARcam Acoustic Backend", "text": "\u003cp>EARcam combines sound measurements with aircraft positions. Keep microphone gain fixed during a search, and inspect whether the result is usable rather than assuming every completed search returns a reliable location. Follow the sound-search guide.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#if-something-stops", "title": "If Something Stops", "text": "Symptom Check First Local endpoint already in use Stop the previous simulator/sensor session; do not start a second owner Camera busy or unavailable Check cabling, device permissions and other camera clients No saved photograph Read the capture error and check free space/output permissions Model rejected Check that the graph, descriptor and settings belong together Photo saved but no review copy There may be zero detections or an analysis error; inspect the original's metadata and worker log Target updates stop Inspect freshness/model/confirmation diagnostics; the existing target is not a fresh observation \u003cp>For the available options, run \u003ccode>./usher --help\u003c/code> and the selected sensor's \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code>. Diagnose the stage that failed before repeating a physical capture.\u003c/p>"}, {"location": "usher-camera-pipeline.html#tailor-or-add-a-module", "title": "Tailor Or Add A Module", "text": "\u003cp>Each Sensoraia sensor module is self-contained under \u003ccode>modules/&lt;module&gt;/\u003c/code>:\u003c/p> \u003col> \u003cli>Tailor an existing module: Edit its C/C++ sources or settings file under    \u003ccode>modules/&lt;module&gt;/\u003c/code> (for example \u003ccode>modules/lwir_cam/\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>modules/ear_cam/\u003c/code>),    update its \u003ccode>modules/&lt;module&gt;/README.md\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>modules/&lt;module&gt;/docs/*.md\u003c/code>    guide, and run \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code> to rebuild both PC and ARM64 binaries cleanly.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Create a new module:    - Create \u003ccode>modules/&lt;your_module&gt;/\u003c/code> with your C11/C++17 worker source,      \u003ccode>README.md\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>docs/&lt;your-module&gt;.md\u003c/code> user guide (keeping within      MORA's 512 MB total shared RAM and avoiding Python on MORA).    - Register the backend in \u003ccode>src/camera_backend_registry.c\u003c/code> using the shared      \u003ccode>struct camera_backend\u003c/code> interface in \u003ccode>src/camera_backend.h\u003c/code> while keeping      \u003ccode>src/usher_protocol.h\u003c/code> wire-compatible with the flight controller.    - Add your build rule to \u003ccode>build-support/make/camera-modules.mk\u003c/code> (and      \u003ccode>SUITE_APPLICATIONS\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>Makefile\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>build-support/mora-package.list\u003c/code>      if packaged for MORA) and expose a \u003ccode>CAMERA\u003c/code> option in \u003ccode>camera.conf\u003c/code> and      \u003ccode>build-support/package_mora.sh\u003c/code>.\u003c/li> \u003cli>Share via Fork &amp; Pull Request: Fork \u003ccode>sensoraia\u003c/code> on GitHub, commit your    module and documentation updates on a feature branch, verify \u003ccode>make all\u003c/code>, and    open a Pull Request.\u003c/li> \u003c/ol>"}]};
